window.QUESTIONS=[{"id":1,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q001-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q001-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Priva's Privacy Risk Management is designed to identify and mitigate privacy risks by detecting overexposed or unused personal data. It provides in-the-moment notifications and guidance within Microsoft Teams to help users make smart handling decisions. This solution directly meets the requirements for identifying unused data, providing user guidance, and mitigating privacy risks.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"communication compliance in insider risk management"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Viva Insights"},{"id":"C","text":"Privacy Risk Management in Microsoft Priva"},{"id":"D","text":"Advanced eDiscovery"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":2,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q002-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q002-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud's Workflow Automation feature natively integrates with Azure Logic Apps to automate responses to security alerts and recommendations. Using Logic Apps requires minimal development effort because it provides a codeless, visual interface for orchestrating workflows based on alert triggers.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure Monitor webhooks"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure Event Hubs"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Functions apps"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure Logics Apps"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":3,"type":"multiple","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q003-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q003-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Storage blob containers support native Azure AD authentication for read/write/delete operations via OAuth tokens or managed identities. Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 is built on top of Blob storage and extends these capabilities to include fine- grained POSIX permissions using Azure AD identities.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure Storage file shares"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure Disk Storage"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Storage blob containers"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2"}],"correct":["C","D"]}]},{"id":4,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q004-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q004-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Purview is the primary data governance service used to discover and classify sensitive data like Pll across Azure resources. Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps provides the necessary security controls, including Data Loss Prevention (DLP) and risk detection, to protect that discovered data and alert on potential breaches.","groups":[{"label":"To connect Azure data sources","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Purview"},{"id":"1","text":"Endpoint data loss prevention"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Information Protection"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"To triage alerts for PII resources","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Monitor"},{"id":"1","text":"Endpoint data loss prevention"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"}],"correct":["3"]}]},{"id":5,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q005-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q005-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Sentinel workbooks provide an interactive visualization canvas for security analysts to create custom dashboards and reports from log data. This feature directly addresses the requirement for custom views and dashboarding capabilities for analyzing security events. Notebooks are used for Jupyter-based analysis, playbooks for automated response, and threat intelligence for known indicators.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"notebooks"},{"id":"B","text":"playbooks"},{"id":"C","text":"workbooks"},{"id":"D","text":"threat intelligence"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":6,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q006-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q006-answer.webp","explanation":"Honeytoken entity tags in Microsoft Defender for Identity are designed specifically to act as traps for malicious actors. By tagging accounts as honeytokens, the system generates an alert whenever authentication or access attempts occur against these entities, allowing defenders to detect compromise early.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"sensitivity labels"},{"id":"B","text":"custom user tags"},{"id":"C","text":"standalone sensors"},{"id":"D","text":"honeytoken entity tags"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":7,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q007-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q007-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Sentinel playbooks are based on Azure Logic Apps and serve as the core component for Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response (SOAR). They enable automated actions to minimize manual intervention by security analysts. Additionally, playbooks can be configured to post incident details directly to Microsoft Teams channels for triage.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"KQL"},{"id":"B","text":"playbooks"},{"id":"C","text":"data connectors"},{"id":"D","text":"workbooks"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":8,"type":"multiple","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q008-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q008-answer.webp","explanation":"To protect against ransomware, you must enable Soft Delete for backups to prevent malicious deletion of backup data and allow recovery within the retention period. Additionally, requiring PINs for critical operations (such as deleting a vault or disabling soft delete) ensures that attackers cannot bypass these protections without multi-factor authentication, even if they compromise administrative credentials.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Enable soft delete for backups."},{"id":"B","text":"Require PINs for critical operations."},{"id":"C","text":"Encrypt backups by using customer-managed keys (CMKs)."},{"id":"D","text":"Perform offline backups to Azure Data Box."},{"id":"E","text":"Use Azure Monitor notifications when backup configurations change."}],"correct":["A","B"]}]},{"id":9,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q009-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q009-answer.webp","explanation":"Box 1: Register App! as an Azure AD application to enable Azure Active Directory authentication for the web app. Box 2: Create an access package in Identity Governance (Entitlement Management) to allow users to Tequest access via the My Apps portal and have those requests approved by a designated manager.","groups":[{"label":"To enable Azure AD authentication for App1, use","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure AD application"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure AD Application Proxy"},{"id":"2","text":"Azure Application Gateway"},{"id":"3","text":"A managed identity in Azure AD"},{"id":"4","text":"Microsoft Defender for App"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"To implement access requests for App! use","options":[{"id":"0","text":"An access package in Identity Governance"},{"id":"1","text":"An access policy in Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"2","text":"An access review in Identity Governance"},{"id":"3","text":"Azure AD Conditional Access App Control"},{"id":"4","text":"An OAuth app policy in Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":10,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q010-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q010-answer.webp","explanation":"Use Data connectors to bring Azure Web Application Firewall (WAF) logs into Microsoft Sentinel. For the virtual machines, select The Log Analytics agent from the choices shown in the screenshot. The explanation refers to Azure Monitor Agent as its newer counterpart, but that name is not one of the listed choices.","groups":[{"label":"WAF logs in Sentinel","options":[{"id":"0","text":"The Azure Diagnostics extension"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Network Watcher"},{"id":"2","text":"Data connectors"},{"id":"3","text":"Workflow automation"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Virtual machine alerts","options":[{"id":"0","text":"The Azure Diagnostics extension"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Storage Analytics"},{"id":"2","text":"Data connectors"},{"id":"3","text":"The Log Analytics agent"},{"id":"4","text":"Workflow automation"}],"correct":["3"]}]},{"id":11,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q011-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q011-answer.webp","explanation":"To send security events from Microsoft Sentinel to an external SIEM like Splunk, you must use Azure Event Hubs as the export mechanism. Sentinel does not have native outbound data connectors for direct HTTP push to third-party tools; instead, it supports exporting log data via Event Hubs, which Splunk can then consume using its dedicated input plugin.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"a Microsoft Sentinel data connector"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure Event Hubs"},{"id":"C","text":"a Microsoft Sentinel workbook"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure Data Factory"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":12,"type":"multiple","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q012-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q012-answer.webp","explanation":"Under the Zero Trust model using Conditional Access, Microsoft Intune is the authoritative source for device compliance status. Even if malware is removed by Defender for Endpoint, Intune must explicitly report the device as compliant before access can be granted. Additionally, because access was previously suspended, the existing client access tokens are stale or invalid; they must be refreshed to reflect the new security state and allow authentication to proceed.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"The client access tokens are refreshed."},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Intune reports the endpoints as compliant."},{"id":"C","text":"A new Azure Active Directory (Azure AD) Conditional Access policy is enforced."},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint reports the endpoints as compliant."}],"correct":["A","B"]}]},{"id":13,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q013-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q013-answer.webp","explanation":"Onboard the servers to Defender for Cloud. Its Defender for Servers plan integrates with Microsoft Defender for Endpoint to provide EDR on the virtual machines. The Defender for Cloud connector is already configured in Microsoft Sentinel, so server alerts flow into Sentinel. Configure Microsoft Sentinel playbooks to automate incident response for SOAR.","groups":[{"label":"EDR","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Add a Microsoft Sentinel data connector for Azure Active Directory (Azure AD)."},{"id":"1","text":"Add a Microsoft Sentinel data connector for Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps."},{"id":"2","text":"Onboard the servers to Azure Arc."},{"id":"3","text":"Onboard the servers to Defender for Cloud."}],"correct":["3"]},{"label":"SOAR","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Configure Microsoft Sentinel analytics rules."},{"id":"1","text":"Configure Microsoft Sentinel playbooks."},{"id":"2","text":"Configure regulatory compliance standards in Defender for Cloud."},{"id":"3","text":"Configure workflow automation in Defender for Cloud."}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":14,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q014-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q014-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure AD Privileged Identity Management (PIM) is exclusively available in Azure AD Premium P1 and P2 licenses, necessitating an upgrade from the Free edition. Basic Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) functionality is included in the Free edition via Security Defaults, so upgrading solely for MFA is not required. Role-based and resource-based authorization are core identity features present across all licensing tiers.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure AD Privileged Identity Management (PIM)"},{"id":"B","text":"role-based authorization"},{"id":"C","text":"resource-based authorization"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure AD Multi-Factor Authentication"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":15,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q015-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q015-answer.webp","explanation":"The Rapid Modernization Plan (RaMP) is the specific Microsoft framework designed to help organizations quickly adopt a Zero Trust-based privileged access strategy. It serves as a structured guide for implementing these security standards, whereas the Enhanced Security Admin Environment (ESAE) has been retired and replaced by RaMP. Microsoft SDL focuses on software development processes, and OSA relates to operational assurance rather than identity design.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Security Development Lifecycle (SDL)"},{"id":"B","text":"Enhanced Security Admin Environment (ESAE)"},{"id":"C","text":"Rapid Modernization Plan (RaMP)"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Operational Security Assurance (OSA)"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":16,"type":"multiple","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q016-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q016-answer.webp","explanation":"The Azure Monitor Agent (AMA) with multi-homing configuration enables log collection from on- premises servers that lack direct internet access by routing data through a gateway. Resource-based Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) in Microsoft Sentinel allows fine-grained permissions, ensuring the IT operations team can view only operation logs while the security team accesses all logs.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"a custom collector that uses the Log Analytics agent"},{"id":"B","text":"the Azure Monitor agent"},{"id":"C","text":"resource-based role-based access control (RBAC)"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure Active Directory (Azure AD) Conditional Access policies"}],"correct":["B","C"]}]},{"id":17,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q017-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q017-answer.webp","explanation":"‘An Azure App Service Environment (ASE) provides a dedicated, single-tenant instance of App Service that runs directly within your own Azure virtual network, ensuring true compute isolation. This contrasts with standard App Service plans or service endpoints, which do not isolate the underlying host infrastructure within your VNet.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure Active Directory (Azure AD) enterprise applications"},{"id":"B","text":"an Azure App Service Environment (ASE)"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure service endpoints"},{"id":"D","text":"an Azure Active Directory (Azure AD) application proxy"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":18,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q018-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q018-answer.webp","explanation":"According to Microsoft Cybersecurity Reference Architecture (MCRA), job roles are mapped to specific security levels to enforce the principle of least privilege and mitigate insider threats. Standard users and developers typically operate at lower or specialized security levels, while system administrators require privileged accounts with enhanced monitoring and restrictions. Referencing the MCRA documentation (e.g., slide 84 in recent updates) confirms these mappings align with best practices for securing identity and access.","groups":[{"label":"Developer","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Enterprise security"},{"id":"1","text":"Privileged security"},{"id":"2","text":"Specialized security"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Standard user","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Enterprise security"},{"id":"1","text":"Privileged security"},{"id":"2","text":"Specialized security"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"T administrator","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Enterprise security"},{"id":"1","text":"Privileged security"},{"id":"2","text":"Specialized security"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":19,"type":"multiple","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q019-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q019-answer.webp","explanation":"According to the Microsoft Zero Trust RaMP overview, the plan prioritizes three core areas for modernization: User access and productivity (C), Data, compliance, and governance (A), and Modern security operations (E). These are designated as the primary focus, while Operational Technology/loT and Infrastructure/Development are listed as ‘as needed’ or secondary priorities.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"data, compliance, and governance"},{"id":"B","text":"infrastructure and development"},{"id":"C","text":"user access and productivity"},{"id":"D","text":"operational technology (OT) and IoT"},{"id":"E","text":"modern security operations"}],"correct":["A","C","E"]}]},{"id":20,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q020-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q020-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps is designed to monitor and control cloud application activity, making it the appropriate tool to detect and prevent data exfiltration attempts to external websites via its DLP capabilities. Microsoft Defender for Identity specifically focuses on on-premises Active Directory threats, using network traffic analysis to identify suspicious behaviors such as lateral movement across domain- joined computers.","groups":[{"label":"Data exfiltration to external websites","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender for Identity"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Office 365"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Lateral movement across domain-joined computers","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender for Identity"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Office 365"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":21,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q021-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q021-answer.webp","explanation":"The Microsoft Cloud Security Benchmark (IM-3: Managed Identities) recommends using managed identities for Azure resources to avoid storing and managing credentials. For Azure API Management, managed identities are the best practice for service accounts because they allow the instance to securely access other Azure services (like Key Vault) without requiring secrets or certificates.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"application registrations in Azure AD"},{"id":"B","text":"managed identities in Azure"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure service principals with usernames and passwords"},{"id":"D","text":"device registrations in Azure AD"},{"id":"E","text":"Azure service principals with certificate credentials"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":22,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q022-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q022-answer.webp","explanation":"LAPS generates unique, random passwords for the local Administrator account on each device and rotates them frequently. This ensures that even if a user obtains administrative access, the credentials are not reused across other machines, thereby minimizing lateral movement. It specifically addresses the requirement to manage local admin rights while preventing credential sprawl in hybrid-joined environments.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Local Administrator Password Solution (LAPS)"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure AD Identity Protection"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure AD Privileged Identity Management (PIM)"},{"id":"D","text":"Privileged Access Workstations (PAWs)"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":23,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q023-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q023-answer.webp","explanation":"In Microsoft's Zero Trust architecture, 'Segmenting access' supports the ‘Assume breach’ principle by minimizing the blast radius of potential compromises. ‘Data classification’ is integral to 'Verify explicitly’ as it provides critical context for authenticating and authorizing access based on data sensitivity and risk signals. UIT access’ (Just-In-Time) enforces 'Least-privilege access’ by granting elevated permissions only when needed and revoking them immediately after use.","groups":[{"label":"Assume breach","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Business continuity"},{"id":"1","text":"Data classification"},{"id":"2","text":"Just-in-time access"},{"id":"3","text":"Segmenting access"}],"correct":["3"]},{"label":"Verify explicitly","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Business continuity"},{"id":"1","text":"Data classification"},{"id":"2","text":"Just-in-time access"},{"id":"3","text":"Segmenting access"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Use least privilege","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Business continuity"},{"id":"1","text":"Data classification"},{"id":"2","text":"Just-in-time access"},{"id":"3","text":"Segmenting access"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":24,"type":"multiple","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q024-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q024-answer.webp","explanation":"Legacy OT and loT devices are often sensitive to active scanning and software patching, which can cause system crashes or disrupt critical business operations. Therefore, Microsoft Cybersecurity Reference Architecture (MCRA) recommends passive traffic monitoring and threat monitoring as primary security hygiene practices that provide visibility without interfering with device functionality.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"active scanning"},{"id":"B","text":"threat monitoring"},{"id":"C","text":"software patching"},{"id":"D","text":"passive traffic monitoring"}],"correct":["B","D"]}]},{"id":25,"type":"multiple","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q025-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q025-answer.webp","explanation":"Zero Trust operates on the principle of ‘never trust, always verify,’ requiring strict identity verification regardless of location, which contradicts Option A. Blocking sign-ins from unknown locations (C) and noncompliant devices (D) implements conditional access policies that enforce risk-based authentication, a core component of Zero Trust. Option B is incorrect because Microsoft recommends passwordless authentication as a stronger security measure than traditional passwords.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Always allow connections from the on-premises network."},{"id":"B","text":"Disable passwordless sign-in for sensitive accounts."},{"id":"C","text":"Block sign-in attempts from unknown locations."},{"id":"D","text":"Block sign-in attempts from noncompliant devices."}],"correct":["C","D"]}]},{"id":26,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q026-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q026-answer.webp","explanation":"Protected Folders (Controlled Folder Access) is specifically designed to prevent unauthorized applications, such as ransomware, from modifying files in designated user and system directories. While Microsoft Defender for Endpoint provides broader threat detection and response capabilities, Protected Folders offers the direct mitigation control required to stop encryption of local files at the point of attempt.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Windows Defender Device Guard"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Files"},{"id":"D","text":"BitLocker Drive Encryption (BitLocker)"},{"id":"E","text":"protected folders"}],"correct":["E"]}]},{"id":27,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q027-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q027-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure managed identities are the recommended identity type for service connections because they eliminate the need to manage secrets or credentials, aligning with DevSecOps principles of least privilege and automated secret rotation. Unlike Azure AD user accounts (even those using PIM or Key Vault), managed identities provide an inherent, non-exportable identity that Azure automatically manages, reducing attack surface and operational overhead.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"a managed identity in Azure"},{"id":"B","text":"an Azure AD user account that has role assignments in Azure AD Privileged Identity Management (PIM)"},{"id":"C","text":"a group managed service account (gMSA)"},{"id":"D","text":"an Azure AD user account that has a password stored in Azure Key Vault"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":28,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q028-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q028-answer.webp","explanation":"In Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS), the platform automatically applies OS and kernel updates to managed node images on a weekly basis. To ensure worker nodes use these updated images with minimal administrative effort, you should recommend configuring Node Image Upgrade Schedules or relying on the default maintenance window logic, which triggers an automated cordon, drain, and replace cycle for nodes running older images. This process replaces existing nodes with new ones provisioned from the updated image, ensuring they have the latest kernel patches without requiring manual intervention to restart specific nodes (Option A) or perform full Kubernetes control plane version upgrades (Option C).","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"The nodes must restart after the updates are applied."},{"id":"B","text":"The updates must first be applied to the image used to provision the nodes."},{"id":"C","text":"The AKS cluster version must be upgraded."}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":29,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q029-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q029-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure AD Application Proxy allows you to publish on-premises web applications to the internet without opening inbound ports or configuring a VPN, thereby minimizing the attack surface. It enforces Zero Trust principles by integrating with Conditional Access and Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA), ensuring that access is verified based on identity rather than network location.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Publish the web apps by using Azure AD Application Proxy."},{"id":"B","text":"Configure the VPN to use Azure AD authentication."},{"id":"C","text":"Configure connectors and rules in Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps."},{"id":"D","text":"Configure web protection in Microsoft Defender for Endpoint."}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":30,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q030-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q030-answer.webp","explanation":"The correct selections are Recycle Bin and Versioning. Versioning mitigates in-place encryption by allowing restoration of previous unencrypted file states, while the Recycle Bin protects against attacks that delete original files after encrypting copies. Both features are native to SharePoint and OneDrive, minimizing administrative effort compared to third-party solutions or DLP policies which do not automatically recover ransomware-affected data.","groups":[{"label":"To mitigate attacks that make copies of flies, encrypt the v copies, and then delete the original fifes, use","options":[{"id":"0","text":"ta loss prevention (DLP) policies"},{"id":"1","text":"The Recycle Bin"},{"id":"2","text":"Versioning"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"To mitigate attacks that encrypt files in place, use","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Data loss prevention (DLP) policies"},{"id":"1","text":"The Recycle Bin"},{"id":"2","text":"Versioning"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":31,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q031-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q031-answer.webp","explanation":"Enabling self-healing in Microsoft 365 Defender allows the system to automatically detect and remediate threats without manual intervention. This automation directly reduces the volume of alerts requiring Tier 1 analyst triage, thereby minimizing their operational load while maintaining Zero Trust principles.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Enable built-in compliance policies in Azure Policy."},{"id":"B","text":"Enable self-healing in Microsoft 365 Defender."},{"id":"C","text":"Automate data classification."},{"id":"D","text":"Create hunting queries in Microsoft 365 Defender."}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":32,"type":"drag","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q032-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q032-answer.webp","explanation":"According to the Microsoft Zero Trust deployment objectives for security operations, the correct sequence prioritizes foundational capabilities first. 1. Establish Visibility: You must first gain comprehensive insight into your environment, assets, and threats before implementing controls. 2. Enable Automation: Once visibility is established, automate response actions to improve SOC operational efficiency and reduce manual workload. 3. Enable additional protection and detection controls: Finally, layer on advanced protective measures now that the foundation of visibility and automated response is in place.","groups":[{"label":"First objective","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Establish visibility"},{"id":"1","text":"Enable automation"},{"id":"2","text":"Enable additional protection and detection controls"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Second objective","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Establish visibility"},{"id":"1","text":"Enable automation"},{"id":"2","text":"Enable additional protection and detection controls"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Third objective","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Establish visibility"},{"id":"1","text":"Enable automation"},{"id":"2","text":"Enable additional protection and detection controls"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":33,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q033-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q033-answer.webp","explanation":"For the 'Commit the code’ stage, Static Application Security Testing (SAST) is recommended because it analyzes source code for security vulnerabilities without executing the program, fitting into the early commit workflow. For the 'Build and test’ stage, Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST) is appropriate as it tests the running application to identify security flaws in the built artifact during the testing phase.","groups":[{"label":"Commit the code","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Dynamic application security testing (DAST)"},{"id":"1","text":"Penetration testing"},{"id":"2","text":"Smoke testing"},{"id":"3","text":"Static application security testing (SAST)"}],"correct":["3"]},{"label":"Build and test","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Dynamic application security testing (DAST)"},{"id":"1","text":"Penetration testing"},{"id":"2","text":"Smoke testing"},{"id":"3","text":"Static application security testing (SAST)"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":34,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q034-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q034-answer.webp","explanation":"According to Microsoft's Rapid Modernization Plan (RaMP) and Privileged Access Workstation best practices, privileged users must not have local administrator rights on their devices to enforce the principle of least privilege. Group? is designated for managing these devices, so they require administrative access for maintenance purposes. Therefore, only Group2 should be added to the local Administrators group, excluding Group! (the privileged users) from local admin rights.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Only add Group2 to the local Administrators group."},{"id":"B","text":"Configure Windows Local Administrator Password Solution (Windows LAPS) in legacy Microsoft LAPS emulation mode."},{"id":"C","text":"Add Group2 to the local Administrators group. Add the user that is assigned the Security Administrator role to the local Administrators group of the user’s assigned privileged access device."}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":35,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q035-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q035-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Lighthouse enables centralized management across multiple tenants and subscriptions without creating guest accounts, directly satisfying the requirement to minimize administrative overhead and avoid guest account maintenance. Azure Arc extends Azure management capabilities (such as Azure Policy, change tracking, and patch management) to on-premises resources, meeting the hybrid requirement to manage infrastructure from Azure while minimizing additional on-premises infrastructure.","groups":[{"label":"To centralize subscription management","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Entra B2B"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Entra External ID"},{"id":"2","text":"Azure Lighthouse"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"To enable the management of on-premises resources","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Arc"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Stack Edge"},{"id":"2","text":"Azure Stack Hub"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":36,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q036-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q036-answer.webp","explanation":"According to the Microsoft Cloud Adoption Framework (CAF) Enterprise-Scale architecture, each application landing zone subscription should contain an Azure Key Vault to securely manage secrets and keys for that specific workload. While resources like Azure Firewall are typically deployed in a dedicated connectivity or shared services subscription, Key Vaults are isolated per landing zone to ensure separate identity management and access control for each application environment.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"an Azure firewall"},{"id":"B","text":"an Azure virtual network gateway"},{"id":"C","text":"an Azure Private DNS zone"},{"id":"D","text":"an Azure key vault"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":37,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q037-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q037-answer.webp","explanation":"To manage updates for on-premises Windows and Linux servers using Azure Update Management (now part of Azure Update Manager), you must first onboard the machines as Azure Arc-enabled servers using the Connected Machine agent. This requires configuring an Azure Automation account, which acts as the central hub for managing update deployment jobs across these hybrid environments.","groups":[{"label":"Azure resource","options":[{"id":"0","text":"A Microsoft Sentinel workspace"},{"id":"1","text":"An Azure Automation account"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Servers Plan 2"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Agent on the servers","options":[{"id":"0","text":"The Azure Connected Machine agent"},{"id":"1","text":"The Azure Monitor Agent"},{"id":"2","text":"The Log Analytics agent"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":38,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q038-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q038-answer.webp","explanation":"For the on-premises VDI, adding the onboarding script to the virtual machine template ensures every cloned instance is onboarded during its first startup while maintaining a single entry in the portal. For Azure Virtual Desktop, incorporating the onboarding script into the custom golden image guarantees that all session hosts are pre-configured and registered with Defender for Endpoint before deployment.","groups":[{"label":"For the VDI","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Add the Defender for Endpoint onboarding script to the virtual machine template."},{"id":"1","text":"Deploy Defender for Endpoint by using a custom Group Policy Object (GPO)"},{"id":"2","text":"Onboard the virtual machine template to Defender for Endpoint."}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"For Azure Virtual Desktop","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Add the Defender for Endpoint onboarding script to the golden image."},{"id":"1","text":"Deploy Defender for Endpoint by using a custom Group Policy Object (GPO)"},{"id":"2","text":"Onboard the golden image to Defender for Endpoint."}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":39,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q039-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q039-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Entra Permissions Management (formerly Cloud Infrastructure Entitlement Management) is specifically designed to analyze and optimize access across multi-cloud environments by identifying unused role assignments based on usage data, such as activity over the last 90 days. While Microsoft Entra Access Reviews (B) involve manual or automated periodic checks of access rights, they do not automatically identify ‘unused’ assignments via historical telemetry analysis in the same direct manner as Permissions Management. Although Microsoft has announced the end-of-sale for standalone Entra Permissions Management with capabilities migrating to Defender for Cloud, exam questions referencing this specific 90-day unused assignment scenario historically point to Permissions Management as the intended answer.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Entra access reviews"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Entra Privileged Identity Management (PIM)"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Entra Permissions Management"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":40,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q040-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q040-answer.webp","explanation":"Multi-user authorization (MUA) via Resource Guard is the Microsoft best practice for protecting backup infrastructure against ransomware. It ensures that a single compromised administrator account cannot delete or disable backups without explicit approval from a separate security contact, thereby preventing unauthorized configuration changes.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"From Azure Backup, configure multi-user authorization by using Resource Guard."},{"id":"B","text":"From Microsoft Entra Privileged Identity Management (PIM), create a role assignment for the Backup Contributor role."},{"id":"C","text":"From Microsoft Azure Backup Setup, register MABS with a Recovery Services vault."},{"id":"D","text":"From a Recovery Services vault, generate a security PIN for critical operations."}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":41,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q041-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q041-answer.webp","explanation":"User delegation shared access signatures (SAS) are recommended for blobs because they support Azure AD authentication and allow granular, time-limited access without exposing storage account keys. Microsoft Entra Domain Services is required for Azure Files to support SMB protocol access with domain-based authentication and authorization.","groups":[{"label":"Blobs","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Account shared access signatures (SAS)"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Entra Domain Services"},{"id":"2","text":"Service shared access signatures (SAS)"},{"id":"3","text":"User delegation shared access signatures (SAS)"}],"correct":["3"]},{"label":"Shares","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Account shared access signatures (SAS)"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Entra Domain Services"},{"id":"2","text":"Service shared access signatures (SAS)"},{"id":"3","text":"User delegation shared access signatures (SAS)"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":42,"type":"drag","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q042-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q042-answer.webp","explanation":"According to the Microsoft Zero Trust Rapid Modernization Plan (RaMP), the implementation sequence begins with ‘Explicitly validate trust for all access requests’ to establish foundational identity controls. Next, ‘Classify and protect data’ is implemented to secure assets based on established trust contexts. Finally, ‘Apply provisions for ransomware recovery readiness’ is deployed to ensure resilience against advanced threats after core protections are in place.","groups":[{"label":"First initiative","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Explicitly validate trust for all access requests"},{"id":"1","text":"Classify and protect data"},{"id":"2","text":"Apply provisions for ransomware recovery readiness"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Second initiative","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Explicitly validate trust for all access requests"},{"id":"1","text":"Classify and protect data"},{"id":"2","text":"Apply provisions for ransomware recovery readiness"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Third initiative","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Explicitly validate trust for all access requests"},{"id":"1","text":"Classify and protect data"},{"id":"2","text":"Apply provisions for ransomware recovery readiness"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":43,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q043-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q043-answer.webp","explanation":"To identify Office documents containing specific data from DB1 while minimizing false positives, you must use Exact Data Match (EDM) within a custom Sensitive Information Type. EDM allows you to define a list of known values (like customer addresses/phone numbers) and match them precisely, avoiding the ambiguity of pattern-only matches. To generate alerts for users downloading an above-average number of files, you should use Microsoft Purview Insider Risk Management (or Data Loss Prevention policies with DLP alerting capabilities focused on user activity anomalies). These solutions leverage machine learning to detect anomalous behavior, such as unusual download volumes, which standard classification tools do not handle.","groups":[{"label":"dentify documents","options":[{"id":"0","text":"A custom sensitive information type (SIT) based on exact data match (EDM)"},{"id":"1","text":"Document fingerprinting"},{"id":"2","text":"A named entity sensitive information type (SIT)"},{"id":"3","text":"Trainable classifiers"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Generate alerts","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Purview Data Policy"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Purview Information Barriers (IBs)"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Purview Information Protection"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Purview insider risk management"}],"correct":["3"]}]},{"id":44,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q044-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q044-answer.webp","explanation":"To enforce pull requests, configure **Protected branches** in Azure Repos, which mandates PRs before code can be merged into the target branch. To require approval for production deployments, use **Environments** in Azure Pipelines with a mandatory approval check configured as a pre-deployment condition.","groups":[{"label":"All pull requests must be enforced","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Environments"},{"id":"1","text":"Protected branches"},{"id":"2","text":"Resource locks"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"All deployments to production must be approved","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Environments"},{"id":"1","text":"Resource locks"},{"id":"2","text":"Triggers"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":45,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q045-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q045-answer.webp","explanation":"To integrate Azure WAF logs with Microsoft Sentinel, you must use a **Data connector**, which establishes the pipeline for ingesting security data from various sources into Sentinel workspaces. For reviewing alerts from virtual machines via Defender for Cloud, you need to enable **Defender for Servers** (formerly Advanced Threat Protection), as this plan specifically provides alerting and vulnerability assessment capabilities for VM resources.","groups":[{"label":"For WAF","options":[{"id":"0","text":"The Azure Diagnostics extension"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Network Watcher"},{"id":"2","text":"Data connectors"},{"id":"3","text":"Workflow automation"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"For the virtual machines","options":[{"id":"0","text":"The Azure Diagnostics extension"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Storage Analytics"},{"id":"2","text":"Data connectors"},{"id":"3","text":"Defender for Servers"},{"id":"4","text":"Workflow automation"}],"correct":["3"]}]},{"id":46,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q046-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q046-answer.webp","explanation":"To minimize administrative effort across all 30 subscriptions, you should assign roles at the highest level of the management group hierarchy. Assigning at the Root Management Group (identified by the Entra Tenant GUID) ensures inheritance to all child groups and subscriptions below it. This requires only a single assignment per role to cover the entire tenant.","groups":[{"label":"Role assignment scope","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Root management group (Entra tenant GUID)"},{"id":"1","text":"Mgmt1 and Mgmt2 management groups"},{"id":"2","text":"Each subscription"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Minimum assignments per role","options":[{"id":"0","text":"1"},{"id":"1","text":"2"},{"id":"2","text":"30"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":47,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q047-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q047-answer.webp","explanation":"According to Microsoft's official AKS documentation for node image upgrades, Linux nodes should be updated weekly to ensure timely security patches and compliance with patch cycles. Windows nodes should be updated monthly, aligning with the standard Windows Update Patch Tuesday schedule and balancing stability with administrative effort.","groups":[{"label":"Pool1","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Weekly"},{"id":"1","text":"Monthly"},{"id":"2","text":"Quarterly"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Pool2","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Weekly"},{"id":"1","text":"Monthly"},{"id":"2","text":"Quarterly"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":48,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q048-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q048-answer.webp","explanation":"Static Application Security Testing (SAST) should be integrated at the 'Commit the code! stage to detect vulnerabilities in source code as early as possible, adhering to DevOps security principles of shifting left. Security smoke testing should be integrated at the 'Go to production’ stage to perform quick validation that critical functions are working correctly in the live environment before full deployment acceptance.","groups":[{"label":"SAST","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Build and test"},{"id":"1","text":"Commit the code"},{"id":"2","text":"Go to production"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Security smoke testing","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Build and test"},{"id":"1","text":"Commit the code"},{"id":"2","text":"Go to production"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":49,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q049-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q049-answer.webp","explanation":"Static Application Security Testing (SAST) is the correct method for the pre-deployment stage because it analyzes source code stored in Azure Repos to identify vulnerabilities without executing the application. Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST) is required for the post-deployment stage because it tests the running application in the test environment by simulating attacks against the deployed instance.","groups":[{"label":"Pre-deployment","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Dynamic application security testing (DAST)"},{"id":"1","text":"Penetration testing"},{"id":"2","text":"Security smoke testing"},{"id":"3","text":"Static application security testing (SAST)"}],"correct":["3"]},{"label":"Post-deployment to the test environment","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Dynamic application security testing (DAST)"},{"id":"1","text":"Security acceptance testing"},{"id":"2","text":"Security smoke testing"},{"id":"3","text":"Static application security testing (SAST)"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":50,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q050-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q050-answer.webp","explanation":"In the Secure Code stage, Supply chain/dependency management is critical for identifying and mitigating risks from third-party libraries and components. For the Secure Operations stage, App access controls ensure that applications enforce proper identity verification and authorization during runtime, aligning with Zero Trust principles.","groups":[{"label":"Secure code","options":[{"id":"0","text":"App access controls"},{"id":"1","text":"A secure pipeline"},{"id":"2","text":"Supply chain/dependency management"},{"id":"3","text":"Threat modeling"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Secure operations","options":[{"id":"0","text":"App access controls"},{"id":"1","text":"A secure pipeline"},{"id":"2","text":"Supply chain/dependency management"},{"id":"3","text":"Threat modeling"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":51,"type":"drag","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q051-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q051-answer.webp","explanation":"The Microsoft Cloud Adoption Framework (CAF) leverages the Microsoft Cloud Security Benchmark  (McSB) as its security maturity model to evaluate governance and compliance across subscriptions.  Microsoft Defender for Cloud is the integrated cloud security posture management tool that directly  implements the Azure Well-Architected Framework's security pillar recommendations for securing  individual workloads.","groups":[{"label":"Cloud Adoption Framework governance","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Advisor"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft cloud security benchmark"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Well-Architected workload security","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Advisor"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft cloud security benchmark"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":52,"type":"multiple","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q052-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q052-answer.webp","explanation":"Immutable, offline backups (C) are a critical ransomware mitigation control because they ensure data can be restored without paying a ransom if live systems are encrypted. Privileged Identity Management with Just-In-Time access (D) limits the attack surface by reducing the window of exposure for high-privilege accounts, which attackers often target to deploy ransomware widely.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Enable firewall logging for auditing, without restricting inbound or outbound traffic."},{"id":"B","text":"Use extended patching cycles to reduce the risk of update-related service disruptions."},{"id":"C","text":"Implement immutable, offline backups that have restricted access and test restore procedures regularly."},{"id":"D","text":"Deploy Privileged Identity Management (PIM) that uses just-in-time (JIT) access and approval workflows."}],"correct":["C","D"]}]},{"id":53,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q053-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q053-answer.webp","explanation":"The Landing Zone module provides the foundational architecture for a secure and well-governed Azure environment, including subscriptions, policies, and networking. To deploy this infrastructure using Infrastructure as Code (laC), you should use Bicep or Terraform, which are the recommended tools for defining and provisioning Azure resources declaratively.","groups":[{"label":"Environment type","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Landing zone"},{"id":"1","text":"Management group"},{"id":"2","text":"Private cloud"},{"id":"3","text":"Resource group"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Module","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Resource Manager (ARM) or Bicep"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Resource Manager (ARM) or Terraform"},{"id":"2","text":"Bicep or Terraform"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":54,"type":"hotspot","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q054-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q054-answer.webp","explanation":"Based on Enterprise-Scale landing zone principles, each distinct environment (Dey, Test, Prod) for each application should reside in its own subscription to enable independent management and billing. Since there are 3 applications with 3 environments each, this results in 9 subscriptions total. To adhere to security best practices and isolate secrets per environment, each of the 9 subscriptions requires its own Azure Key Vault, resulting in 9 key vaults.","groups":[{"label":"Subscriptions","options":[{"id":"0","text":"1"},{"id":"1","text":"3"},{"id":"2","text":"9"},{"id":"3","text":"10"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Key vaults","options":[{"id":"0","text":"1"},{"id":"1","text":"3"},{"id":"2","text":"9"},{"id":"3","text":"10"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":55,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q055-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q055-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Intune Endpoint Privilege Management (EPM) enables standard users to request temporary, on-demand administrative privileges for specific applications, such as Appt. This approach aligns with Zero Trust principles by avoiding permanent local admin rights and ensuring elevation is app-scoped, time-bound, and audited.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Entra Privileged Identity Management (PIM)"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Intune Endpoint Privilege Management (EPM)"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Local Administrator Password Solution (Microsoft LAPS)"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Entra entitlement management"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":56,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q056-question.webp","answerImage":null,"explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"From Azure Policy, assign a built-in policy definition that has a scope of the subscription."},{"id":"B","text":"From Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps, create an access policy for cloud applications."},{"id":"C","text":"From Microsoft Defender for Cloud, turn on a security standard."},{"id":"D","text":"From Microsoft Defender for Cloud, review the secure score recommendations."}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":57,"type":"single","topic":1,"questionImage":"images/q057-question.webp","answerImage":null,"explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Perform offline backups to Azure Data Box."},{"id":"B","text":"Use Azure Monitor notifications when backup configurations change."},{"id":"C","text":"Encrypt backups by using customer-managed keys (CMKs)."},{"id":"D","text":"Require PINs for critical operations."}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":58,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q058-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q058-answer.webp","explanation":"The Azure Policy 'Disabled' effect is designed specifically for testing or evaluation scenarios where resources are evaluated but no remediation, denial, or modification occurs. Unlike the ‘Audit' effect (which reports non-compliance), the ‘Disabled’ effect prevents any enforcement actions while still allowing the policy assignment to exist without altering resource states or generating activity logs for violations.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Deny"},{"id":"B","text":"Modify"},{"id":"C","text":"Append"},{"id":"D","text":"Disabled"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":59,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q059-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q059-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Servers is a workload protection solution designed to secure endpoint resources such as virtual machines. In the Azure Security Benchmark V3, controls related to securing endpoints, including antivirus and endpoint detection and response capabilities, fall under the ‘Endpoint Security’ category. Therefore, you should evaluate the Endpoint Security compliance control to verify the installation status of Defender for Servers on Windows VMs.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Asset Management"},{"id":"B","text":"Posture and Vulnerability Management"},{"id":"C","text":"Data Protection"},{"id":"D","text":"Endpoint Security"},{"id":"E","text":"Incident Response"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":60,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q060-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q060-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft 365 Defender (specifically Microsoft Defender for Endpoint) is the unified solution used to evaluate and manage the security posture of Windows 11 devices managed by Intune. Microsoft Defender for Cloud provides centralized security management and continuous assessment for Azure infrastructure resources, including virtual machines and storage accounts.","groups":[{"label":"Windows 11 devices","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft 365 compliance center"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft 365 Defender"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Azure virtual machines","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft 365 compliance center"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft 365 Defender"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Azure Storage accounts","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft 365 compliance center"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft 365 Defender"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":61,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q061-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q061-answer.webp","explanation":"To evaluate compliance against a specific standard like NIST 800-53, you must first enable the corresponding regulatory compliance standard package. This action automatically assigns the built-in Azure Policy initiative associated with that standard to the subscription. Microsoft Defender for Cloud relies on these policy initiatives to continuously assess resources and generate compliance status reports.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"From Azure Policy, assign a built-in initiative that has a scope of the subscription."},{"id":"B","text":"From Microsoft Sentinel, configure the Microsoft Defender for Cloud data connector."},{"id":"C","text":"From Defender for Cloud, review the Azure security baseline for audit report."},{"id":"D","text":"From Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps, create an access policy for cloud applications."}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":62,"type":"multiple","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q062-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q062-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Containers (A) and Microsoft Defender for Servers (B) support monitoring non-Azure environments like AWS EKS and EC2 via Cloud Security Posture Management (CSPM) and continuous cloud risk assessment, respectively, without requiring Azure Arc or an agent on the target host. Azure Policy (E) extends to non-Azure clouds through integration with security posture data or policy initiatives that apply to cloud accounts, allowing compliance controls to be enforced across the heterogeneous environment. Azure AD Conditional Access (C) and Privileged Identity Management (D) are identity-centric controls that regulate user access to Azure resources; they do not directly provide security monitoring or posture protection for AWS infrastructure resources themselves.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Defender for Containers"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Defender for servers"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Active Directory (Azure AD) Conditional Access"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure Active Directory (Azure AD) Privileged Identity Management (PIM)"},{"id":"E","text":"Azure Policy"}],"correct":["A","C","E"]}]},{"id":63,"type":"multiple","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q063-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q063-answer.webp","explanation":"Migrating to Azure Virtual Desktop (C) replaces the legacy RDP jump server with a modern, scalable host pool that supports direct access controls. Implementing Conditional Access with MFA and named locations (E) enforces Zero Trust by verifying identity and context before granting access, regardless of network location. Configuring Azure Firewall (D) restricts outbound traffic from the host pools, ensuring least-privilege network access to backend resources. Note: While deploying in France might seem like a latency fix, Azure Virtual Desktop allows users to connect directly to their assigned resources without an intermediate jump server in Seattle, significantly reducing latency and attack surface compared to the current architecture. NSGs (A) are less granular than Azure Firewall for centralized outbound policy management in this Zero Trust context.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Configure network security groups (NSGs) to allow access from only specific logical groupings of IP address ranges."},{"id":"B","text":"Deploy a Remote Desktop server to an Azure region located in France."},{"id":"C","text":"Migrate from the Remote Desktop server to Azure Virtual Desktop."},{"id":"D","text":"Implement Azure Firewall to restrict host pool outbound access."},{"id":"E","text":"Configure Azure Active Directory (Azure AD) Conditional Access with multi-factor authentication (MFA) and named locations."}],"correct":["C","D","E"]}]},{"id":64,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q064-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q064-answer.webp","explanation":"For Azure loT Edge devices, Microsoft Defender for loT is the specialized security posture management solution designed to protect OT and oT infrastructure. For AWS EC2 instances, you must use Microsoft Defender for Cloud via Azure Arc-enabled servers to extend security management capabilities to resources outside of Azure.","groups":[{"label":"For the loT Edge devices","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Arc"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint"},{"id":"4","text":"Microsoft Defender for loT"}],"correct":["4"]},{"label":"For the AWS EC2 instances","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Arc only"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud and Azure Arc"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps only"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud only"},{"id":"4","text":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint and Azure Arc"},{"id":"5","text":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint only"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":65,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q065-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q065-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Virtual Desktop (AVD) provides a scalable, on-demand virtual desktop infrastructure that eliminates the need for personal devices, strictly adhering to the security policy. AVD scales elastically to accommodate the influx of temporary employees while centralizing data on-premises via hybrid connectivity or hosted apps, ensuring no data resides on local endpoints.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Deploy Azure Virtual Desktop, Azure Active Directory (Azure AD) Conditional Access, and Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps."},{"id":"B","text":"Redesign the VPN infrastructure by adopting a split tunnel configuration."},{"id":"C","text":"Deploy Microsoft Endpoint Manager and Azure Active Directory (Azure AD) Conditional Access."},{"id":"D","text":"Migrate the on-premises applications to cloud-based applications."}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":66,"type":"multiple","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q066-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q066-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Web Application Firewall (WAF) and Azure Firewall are preventative controls that directly contribute to improving the Secure Score in Microsoft Defender for Cloud. The other options, such as PIM and Sentinel, either do not have direct recommendations tied to the base Secure Score or function primarily as detective/monitoring tools rather than preventative network/app-layer security controls.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure Web Application Firewall (WAF)"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure Active Directory (Azure AD) Privileged Identity Management (PIM)"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure Firewall"},{"id":"E","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud alerts"}],"correct":["A","D"]}]},{"id":67,"type":"multiple","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q067-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q067-answer.webp","explanation":"Option C meets the cardholder data requirement because Azure Key Vault Managed HSM supports Bring Your Own Key (BYOK), allowing the company to manage its own keys in a dedicated hardware module. Option B meets the insurance claim requirement because Customer-Provided Keys (CPK) allow customers to host and manage encryption keys on-premises, separate from Azure's key management infrastructure.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Store the cardholder data in an Azure SQL database that is encrypted by using Microsoft-managed keys."},{"id":"B","text":"Store the insurance claim data in Azure Blob storage encrypted by using customer- provided keys."},{"id":"C","text":"Store the cardholder data in an Azure SQL database that is encrypted by using keys stored in Azure Key Vault Managed HSM."},{"id":"D","text":"Store the insurance claim data in Azure Files encrypted by using Azure Key Vault Managed HSM."}],"correct":["B","C"]}]},{"id":68,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q068-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q068-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Policy is the correct solution because it allows you to assign built-in ISO 27001:2013 policy initiatives that audit resource compliance. Crucially, Azure Policy supports automatic remediation tasks (via ‘Modify’ effects) to fix noncompliant resources, which meets the specific requirement for automatic remediation. Azure Blueprints are designed for initial provisioning and consistency, not for ongoing continuous enforcement or automatic remediation of existing resources.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure Policy"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure Blueprints"},{"id":"C","text":"the regulatory compliance dashboard in Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure role-based access control (Azure RBAC)"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":69,"type":"drag","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q069-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q069-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Identity (formerly ATP) is designed to detect advanced threats and compromised identities in your on-premises network environment. Bulk file downloads from SharePoint Online are a specific data exfiltration behavior best monitored by Microsoft Purview Information Protection (DLP), which can identify and alert on unusual access. patterns involving sensitive content.","groups":[{"label":"Potentially compromised accounts","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Defender for Identity"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Bulk SharePoint downloads","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Defender for Identity"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Purview Information Protection (DLP)"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":70,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q070-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q070-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Policy is the correct choice because it allows you to define and enforce governance rules that automatically ensure Azure resources comply with organizational standards. Specific built-in policies exist to restrict public access, enforce firewall configurations, and require private endpoints for storage accounts, directly addressing the identified security risks.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure Policy"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure Network Watcher"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Storage Analytics"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":71,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q071-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q071-answer.webp","explanation":"The alert depicts the 'MicroBurst' threat, which specifically exploits Azure storage accounts by enumerating them and attempting to retrieve their shared keys via REST API calls. Enabling the policy 'Storage accounts should prevent shared key access’ forces authentication via Azure AD instead of account keys, directly mitigating this specific attack vector. Disallowing public access (Option A) does not prevent unauthorized users from authenticating and retrieving keys if they have permissions, making Option C the correct preventive control.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Storage account public access should be disallowed"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure Key Vault Managed HSM should have purge protection enabled"},{"id":"C","text":"Storage accounts should prevent shared key access"},{"id":"D","text":"Storage account keys should not be expired"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":72,"type":"multiple","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q072-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q072-answer.webp","explanation":"To minimize management effort across 50 subscriptions, assign compliance measures at the Management Group level rather than individually to each subscription. Azure Policy Initiatives (Option A) group related policy definitions for standards like ISO 27001:2013, ensuring existing resources are audited against the standard. Azure Blueprints (Option F) provide a comprehensive solution to enforce compliance and governance patterns across new or existing environments at scale.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Assign an initiative to a management group."},{"id":"B","text":"Assign a policy to each subscription."},{"id":"C","text":"Assign a policy to a management group."},{"id":"D","text":"Assign an initiative to each subscription."},{"id":"E","text":"Assign a blueprint to each subscription."},{"id":"F","text":"Assign a blueprint to a management group."}],"correct":["A","F"]}]},{"id":73,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q073-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q073-answer.webp","explanation":"The first hot area corresponds to the security control ‘Restrict unauthorized network access,’ which is technically implemented using Network Security Groups (NSGs) to filter traffic at the subnet or NIC level. The second hot area identifies the workload protection service, which is Microsoft Defender for servers (formerly Azure Defender for Servers), responsible for securing virtual machines against exploits and vulnerabilities.","groups":[{"label":"Restrict unauthorized network access","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Entra Conditional Access policies"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Web Application Firewall"},{"id":"2","text":"Network security groups (NSGs)"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Enable endpoint protection","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Defender for Resource Manager"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender for Servers"},{"id":"2","text":"Private endpoints"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":74,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q074-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q074-answer.webp","explanation":"The VMAccess extension is designed for restoring SSH/RDP access and password resets, not for hardening management ports. Azure Security Benchmark V3 ‘Secure management ports' controls require restricting network access (e.g., via Private Endpoints) or using Just-In-Time access to minimize exposure. Since VMAccess does not restrict inbound traffic to management ports, it does not meet the goal.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":75,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q075-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q075-answer.webp","explanation":"Adaptive network hardening addresses the ‘Restrict unauthorized network access' control by analyzing traffic to recommend NSG rules, not the 'Secure management ports' control. The specific remediations for securing management ports are using Network Security Groups (NSGs), Just-In- Time (JIT) VM access, or closing unused management ports entirely. Therefore, enabling adaptive network hardening does not meet the goal of increasing the score for secure management ports.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":76,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q076-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q076-answer.webp","explanation":"The goal is to increase the score for the 'Secure management ports' controls in Azure Security Benchmark V3. Just-in-Time (JIT) VM access directly addresses this by preventing standing access on administrative ports, thereby improving the secure score for this specific control.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":77,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q077-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q077-answer.webp","explanation":"Yes, the solution meets the goal. Private Endpoints create a direct, private connection between the web app and the database within the Azure backbone network, keeping traffic off the public internet. This aligns with Zero Trust principles by enforcing least-privilege access via Private Link rather than relying on perimeter-based security.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":78,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q078-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q078-answer.webp","explanation":"The goal is to secure the network connection between the web app and the database, which requires network isolation controls like Azure Private Link or VNet integration to restrict traffic flow. Azure Key Vault manages secret storage (credentials/keys) but does not provide network-level security or encryption for data in transit across the wire. Therefore, implementing Key Vault alone does not satisfy the requirement to secure the connection itself.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":79,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q079-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q079-answer.webp","explanation":"The proposed solution focuses on protecting the web application from external threats via WAF, not securing the internal connection between the web app and the database. To meet Zero Trust goals for backend traffic, you should use Private Endpoints or Private Link to ensure communication occurs over a private network interface, keeping data within the Microsoft backbone.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":80,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q080-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q080-answer.webp","explanation":"Adaptive application controls in Microsoft Defender for Cloud use machine learning to define allowlists of known-safe applications on Azure virtual machines. While primarily designed to detect anomalies, this is the only option listed that directly addresses restricting execution to authorized applications on the VM infrastructure level. Other options like Endpoint Manager manage endpoints generally, and Cloud Apps manages cloud service usage, neither fitting the specific VM application control requirement as precisely as Defender for Cloud's adaptive controls.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"adaptive application controls in Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"B","text":"app protection policies in Microsoft Endpoint Manager"},{"id":"C","text":"app discovery anomaly detection policies in Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure Security Benchmark compliance controls in Defender for Cloud"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":81,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q081-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q081-answer.webp","explanation":"The proposed solution does not meet the goal because Azure Front Door with WAF is designed to secure ingress traffic at the application layer, protecting against web-specific threats like SQL injection and DDoS. It does not provide network-level security or encryption for the internal communication channel between the web app backend and the database. To secure that specific connection under Zero Trust, one should implement TLS/SSL encryption and private networking (e.g., VNet peering or Private Endpoints).","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":82,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q082-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q082-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Endpoint Manager (which includes Microsoft Intune) provides compliance policies and dashboards specifically designed to assess whether managed devices meet defined security rules. It supports the required operating systems (Windows, iOS, Android, macOS) regardless of their deployment location (on-premises or cloud). Other options like Defender for Endpoint focus on threat protection rather than policy compliance assessment.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Endpoint Manager"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Information Protection"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":83,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q083-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q083-answer.webp","explanation":"The goal is to improve the ‘Secure management ports' score by restricting unnecessary inbound access to RDP and SSH. Onboarding VMs to Microsoft Defender for Endpoint provides threat detection and response capabilities but does not automatically modify firewall rules or disable open management ports. Therefore, this action alone does not address the specific configuration requirement to secure these ports.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":84,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q084-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q084-answer.webp","explanation":"Defender for Cloud does not automatically enable all regulatory compliance standards to minimize noise and cost. You must explicitly add the specific standard (NIST 800-53) in the Compliance dashboard before the service can assess your resources against it. Once added, Defender for Cloud generates assessments and a status report specifically for NIST 800-53.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"From Defender for Cloud, review the secure score recommendations."},{"id":"B","text":"From Microsoft Sentinel, configure the Microsoft Defender for Cloud data connector."},{"id":"C","text":"From Defender for Cloud, review the Azure security baseline for audit report."},{"id":"D","text":"From Defender for Cloud, add a regulatory compliance standard."}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":85,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q085-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q085-answer.webp","explanation":"Policy Analyzer is a component of the Microsoft Security Compliance Toolkit specifically designed to compare and analyze Group Policy Objects (GPOs). It allows administrators to assess current device configurations against recommended security baselines by highlighting differences and compliance status. Other tools like Intune or Autopilot are for deployment, while LGPO is for editing local policy, not for comparative analysis.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Intune"},{"id":"B","text":"Local Group Policy Object (LGPO)"},{"id":"C","text":"Windows Autopilot"},{"id":"D","text":"Policy Analyzer"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":86,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q086-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q086-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud is the primary tool for evaluating the security posture of Azure resources using its native Security Posture capabilities. To assess all workloads in a landing zone, you must first enable the appropriate Defender plans to generate security recommendations and compliance scores across those resource types.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Configure Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) vulnerability scanning."},{"id":"B","text":"Obtain Azure AD Premium Plan 2 licenses."},{"id":"C","text":"Add Microsoft Sentinel data connectors."},{"id":"D","text":"Enable the Defender plan for all resource types in Microsoft Defender for Cloud."}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":87,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q087-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q087-answer.webp","explanation":"To assess NIST 800-53 compliance, you must use Azure Policy because Microsoft provides built-in policy initiatives (regulatory compliance packs) specifically for this framework. You should assign an initiative rather than a single policy definition, as initiatives group the multiple policies required to evaluate full regulatory compliance against your subscription scope.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"From Azure Policy, assign a built-in initiative that has a scope of the subscription."},{"id":"B","text":"From Azure Policy, assign a built-in policy definition that has a scope of the subscription."},{"id":"C","text":"From Defender for Cloud, review the Azure security baseline for audit report."},{"id":"D","text":"From Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps, create an access policy for cloud applications."}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":88,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q088-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q088-answer.webp","explanation":"To review compliance against a specific standard like NIST SP 800-53 in Azure, you must first assign the corresponding regulatory compliance initiative via Azure Policy. Microsoft provides built-in policy initiatives (such as NIST SP 800-53 Rev 4/5) that map controls to specific resource requirements. Assigning this initiative at the subscription scope enables Defender for Cloud to assess resources and generate compliance reports based on those defined controls.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"From Defender for Cloud, review the Azure security baseline for audit report."},{"id":"B","text":"From Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps, create an access policy for cloud applications."},{"id":"C","text":"From Defender for Cloud, enable Defender for Cloud plans."},{"id":"D","text":"From Azure Policy, assign a built-in initiative that has a scope of the subscription."}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":89,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q089-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q089-answer.webp","explanation":"To detect risky sign-ins and activities for service principals (workload identities), you must use Microsoft Entra ID Protection. While standard P2 licenses cover user risk detection, detecting risks specifically for workload identities requires the Workload Identities Premium add-on or license. Therefore, the correct selections are Microsoft Entra ID Protection as the service and Entra Workload ID Premium as the license type.","groups":[{"label":"Service","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender for Identity"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Entra ID Protection"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"License","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Entra ID P1"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Entra ID P2"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Entra Workload ID Premium"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":90,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q090-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q090-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Entra Smart Lockout is a cloud-based feature designed to protect accounts from being locked out in the on-premises Active Directory domain due to failed sign-in attempts in Microsoft Entra ID. Both the threshold for failed sign-in attempts that trigger a lockout and the duration of the lockout are configured and managed exclusively within the Microsoft Entra admin center, not in Group Policy or on-premises AD DS. Therefore, 'Microsoft Entra |D only' is the correct selection for both considerations.","groups":[{"label":"Failed sign-in threshold","options":[{"id":"0","text":"AD DS only"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Entra ID only"},{"id":"2","text":"AD DS and Microsoft Entra ID"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Lockout duration","options":[{"id":"0","text":"AD DS only"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Entra ID only"},{"id":"2","text":"AD DS and Microsoft Entra ID"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":91,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q091-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q091-answer.webp","explanation":"To route incoming email directly to Microsoft 365 for scanning by Defender for Office 365, you must change the MX (Mail Exchange) records in DNS. This ensures that external mail servers deliver messages to “*.mail.protection.outlook.com’ instead of forwarding them to a third-party filtering service.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"the accepted domains in Exchange Online"},{"id":"B","text":"the DNS records"},{"id":"C","text":"the Exchange Online transport rule"},{"id":"D","text":"the Exchange Online connectors"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":92,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q092-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q092-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Lighthouse is the correct solution because it is designed specifically for cross-tenant management, allowing service providers to manage multiple customer tenants without creating guest accounts. This directly satisfies the requirement to minimize guest account usage and administrative effort while enabling Group2 users to assign the Security Operator role in customer tenants.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"multi-user authorization (MUA)"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure Lighthouse"},{"id":"C","text":"Privileged Identity Management (PIM)"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Entra B28 collaboration"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":93,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q093-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q093-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Policy is the dedicated governance service for enforcing rules and effects on resources within a subscription or management group. Specifically, it supports built-in policies such as ‘Allowed locations’ that restrict resource deployment to specified regions like West Europe or North Europe, directly satisfying the enforcement requirement.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure management groups"},{"id":"B","text":"custom Azure roles"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Policy assignments"},{"id":"D","text":"regulatory compliance standards in Microsoft Defender for Cloud"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":94,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q094-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q094-answer.webp","explanation":"To integrate with third-party security vendors for threat intelligence (such as known malware), you must configure the Microsoft Threat Intelligence data connector. To automatically generate incidents when specific conditions like a C2 IP address are detected, you need to create a Microsoft Threat Intelligence Analytics rule.","groups":[{"label":"ntegrate Microsoft Sentinel with a third-party security vendor","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Custom entity activities"},{"id":"1","text":"A playbook"},{"id":"2","text":"A threat detection rule"},{"id":"3","text":"A threat indicator"},{"id":"4","text":"A threat Intelligence connector"}],"correct":["4"]},{"label":"Automatically generate incidents","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Custom entity activities"},{"id":"1","text":"A playbook"},{"id":"2","text":"A threat detection rule"},{"id":"3","text":"A threat indicator"},{"id":"4","text":"A threat Intelligence connector"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":95,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q095-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q095-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Policy is the correct solution because it allows you to enforce compliance standards like ISO 27001:2013 by applying built-in initiatives that automatically audit and detect non-compliant resources upon deployment or evaluation. While Azure Blueprints can define repeatable templates, Azure Policy provides the continuous monitoring and enforcement mechanism required to ensure ongoing compliance detection for all resources in the subscription.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure Blueprints"},{"id":"B","text":"the regulatory compliance dashboard in Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Policy"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure role-based access control (Azure RBAC)"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":96,"type":"drag","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q096-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q096-answer.webp","explanation":"To mitigate brute force attacks in a hybrid environment using Pass-Through Authentication (PTA), you should enable Smart Lockout. Smart Lockout distinguishes between intentional and accidental failed sign-ins, preventing on-premises accounts from being locked out by distributed attacks while protecting the cloud tenant. To address leaked credentials, you must implement Password Hash Synchronization (PHS) alongside PTA. This allows Microsoft Defender for Identity Protection to detect when a user's password appears in known breach databases, which is not possible with PTA alone since Azure AD does not store or verify the hash directly.","groups":[{"label":"Brute force attacks","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure AD Password Protection"},{"id":"1","text":"Extranet Smart Lockout (ESL)"},{"id":"2","text":"Password hash synchronization"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Leaked credentials","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure AD Password Protection"},{"id":"1","text":"Extranet Smart Lockout (ESL)"},{"id":"2","text":"Password hash synchronization"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":97,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q097-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q097-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Purview is the correct service for enforcing Data Loss Prevention (DLP) policies managed directly from the Microsoft 365 Defender portal. Microsoft Entra ID Protection (formerly Azure AD Identity Protection) provides User and Entity Behavior Analytics (UEBA) capabilities specifically tailored for a cloud-only environment, as Defender for Identity requires on-premises Active Directory integration.","groups":[{"label":"DLP","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Data Catalog"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Data Explorer"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Purview"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"UEBA","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure AD Identity Protection"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender for Identity"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Entra Verified ID"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":98,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q098-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q098-answer.webp","explanation":"To prevent email impersonation of internal senders, you must use Microsoft Defender for Office 365, as it provides the advanced threat protection features not available in Exchange Online Protection alone. Specifically, you need to configure an Anti-phishing policy that enables impersonation protection, which detects and blocks messages spoofing specific users or domains.","groups":[{"label":"Service","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure AD Identity Protection"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender for DNS"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Office 365"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Purview"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Policy type","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Anti-phishing"},{"id":"1","text":"Anti-spam"},{"id":"2","text":"Data loss prevention (DLP)"},{"id":"3","text":"nsider risk management"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":99,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q099-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q099-answer.webp","explanation":"Access Reviews allow project managers to periodically verify group membership and automatically remove users who are not approved within the specified timeframe (30 days). Group Writeback ensures that changes made in Azure AD, such as removing members during an access review, sync back to the on-premises Active Directory groups that control access to Windows Server shared folders.","groups":[{"label":"dentity Governance feature","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Access reviews"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure AD Privileged Identity Management (PIM)"},{"id":"2","text":"Entitlement management"},{"id":"3","text":"Lifecycle workflows"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Project team configuration","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Enable group writeback for the existing synced groups."},{"id":"1","text":"From Azure AD, create a new cloud-only security group for each project."},{"id":"2","text":"Azure AD, create a security group for each project and enable group"},{"id":"3","text":"writeback for each group."}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":100,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q100-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q100-answer.webp","explanation":"To allow Fabrikam users to access Contoso resources without explicitly creating guest accounts, you must configure a **Connected Organization**. This feature enables external users from partner tenants to authenticate and request access packages directly using their home tenant credentials. To allow non-administrative users in contoso.com to create access packages, you should use **Catalogs** with delegated management. Specifically, assigning the Entitlement Manager Application Administrator role or using catalog-level delegation permits designated users to manage access package lifecycle tasks without requiring global administrator privileges.","groups":[{"label":"External user access without manually creating guests","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Connected organization"},{"id":"1","text":"Administrative unit"},{"id":"2","text":"Access review"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Let non-admins create access packages","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Catalog"},{"id":"1","text":"Administrative unit"},{"id":"2","text":"Program"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":101,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q101-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q101-answer.webp","explanation":"Application control policies in Microsoft Defender for Endpoint allow you to create allow or block lists of applications using code signing, file hash, or path rules. This directly satisfies the requirement to ensure only authorized applications can run and automatically block unauthorized ones until an administrator intervenes. Other options like app discovery anomaly detection are for monitoring rather than enforcement, and Azure AD app registrations manage cloud application identities, not on-premises VM executable permissions.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"app discovery anomaly detection policies in Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure Security Benchmark compliance controls in Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"C","text":"app registrations in Azure AD"},{"id":"D","text":"application control policies in Microsoft Defender for Endpoint"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":102,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q102-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q102-answer.webp","explanation":"To manage access to a specific third-party SaaS application like App! with Microsoft Entra authentication, you must define it as a Global Secure Access app. This object type allows you to associate the app with specific traffic forwarding profiles and enforce identity-aware policies, ensuring users authenticate via Entra before connecting.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"a Global Secure Access app"},{"id":"B","text":"a private access traffic forwarding profile"},{"id":"C","text":"an internet access traffic forwarding profile"},{"id":"D","text":"a Quick Access app"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":103,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q103-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q103-answer.webp","explanation":"Adaptive Application Controls in Microsoft Defender for Cloud allows you to create allowlists of authorized processes based on historical activity. It enforces these policies by automatically blocking any application that is not explicitly allowed, ensuring only authorized software runs on the virtual machines.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"app registrations in Azure AD"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure AD Conditional Access App Control policies"},{"id":"C","text":"app discovery anomaly detection policies in Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"D","text":"adaptive application controls in Defender for Cloud"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":104,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q104-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q104-answer.webp","explanation":"Workload identities are represented as Service Principals in Microsoft Entra ID, making this the correct identity type for controlling non-interactive access. Risk-based Conditional Access policies for workloads rely on risk signals detected and generated by Microsoft Entra ID Protection, which assesses threats against these identities.","groups":[{"label":"dentity type","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Service principal"},{"id":"1","text":"System-assigned managed identity"},{"id":"2","text":"User-assigned managed identity"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Signal source","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Client app"},{"id":"1","text":"Device platform"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Entra ID Protection"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":105,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q105-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q105-answer.webp","explanation":"Adaptive scopes enable you to assign communication compliance policies to groups dynamically based on custom Exchange Online attributes, directly satisfying the requirement for attribute-based assignment. By using dynamic criteria instead of static lists or multiple separate policies, adaptive scopes minimize the number of required policies and reduce ongoing administrative effort.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Purview Information Protection"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft 365 Defender user tags"},{"id":"C","text":"adaptive scopes"},{"id":"D","text":"administrative units"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":106,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q106-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q106-answer.webp","explanation":"Automation Rules are the correct choice because they minimize administrative effort by applying logic to multiple analytics rules based on conditions, rather than configuring each of the 50 rules individually. They also support configuring expiration dates for incidents, satisfying the requirement to manage lifecycle details when playbooks run. The trigger type must be ‘Incident’ because Automation Rules act upon generated incidents (or alerts grouped into incidents) to invoke Azure Logic Apps playbooks for SOAR responses.","groups":[{"label":"Use","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Analytics rules"},{"id":"1","text":"Automation rules"},{"id":"2","text":"Investigation graphs"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Trigger type","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Alert"},{"id":"1","text":"Entity"},{"id":"2","text":"Incident"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":107,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q107-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q107-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Lighthouse is required to delegate management permissions across multiple tenants (Tenant2 and Tenant3) to users in Tenant, allowing them to manage resources without switching accounts. The ‘Multiple workspace view' feature in Microsoft Sentinel specifically supports the aggregated viewing and management of **Incidents** across selected workspaces.","groups":[{"label":"Delegate permissions by using","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Blueprints."},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Lighthouse"},{"id":"2","text":"Azure Sphere"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Microsoft Sentinel feature","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Analytics rules"},{"id":"1","text":"ncidents"},{"id":"2","text":"Workbooks"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":108,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q108-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q108-answer.webp","explanation":"Create an Administrative Unit in the Contoso tenant to scope permissions specifically to the users requiring helpdesk support, satisfying the least privilege requirement by limiting access. Assign the ‘Password Administrator’ role to a security group containing Fabrikam's helpdesk users within this Administrative Unit. This approach minimizes administrative effort by using built-in RBAC scopes rather than custom policies or manual individual assignments.","groups":[{"label":"Role to assign to the Fabrikam helpdesk users for contoso.com","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Directory Readers"},{"id":"1","text":"Helpdesk Administrator"},{"id":"2","text":"Password Administrator"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"To restrict the scope of the role assignments for the Fabrikam helpdesk users, use","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Acustom role"},{"id":"1","text":"An access package"},{"id":"2","text":"An administrative unit"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Role to assign to the Fabrikam helpdesk users to reset the Contoso user passwords","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Directory Readers"},{"id":"1","text":"Helpdesk Administrator"},{"id":"2","text":"Password Administrator"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":109,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q109-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q109-answer.webp","explanation":"To leverage the Azure Monitor Agent (AMA) on on-premises Hyper-V hosts, you must use Azure Arc enabled servers via the Connected Machine agent. For cost minimization and specific event filtering, Data Collection Rules (DCRs) are required to define exactly which Windows Security events are ingested into Sentinel.","groups":[{"label":"n Azure, deploy","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Monitor data collection endpoints"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Monitor data collection rules (DCRs)"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud data collection settings"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"On the virtual machines, install","options":[{"id":"0","text":"The Azure Connected Machine agent for Azure Arc-enabled servers"},{"id":"1","text":"The Log Analytics agent"},{"id":"2","text":"The VM insights Map Dependency agent on Windows"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":110,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q110-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q110-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud is the service that provides Just-in-Time (JIT) VM access, which includes network-based controls to restrict access by originating IP address. Microsoft Entra Privileged Identity Management (PIM) is used to manage JIT authorization controls through role-based access control (RBAC) assignments, allowing specific users to request elevated permissions.","groups":[{"label":"For the network controls","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"For the authorization controls","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Entra Privileged Identity Management (PIM)"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Purview Privileged Access Management"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Entra Permissions Management"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":111,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q111-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q111-answer.webp","explanation":"Statement 1is No because Azure Resource Manager allows deploying a Resource Guard in a different subscription (Sub2) from the one containing the vault (Vault1), providing isolation and better protection. ‘Statement 2 is Yes because enabling all MUA controls ensures that protected settings changes require multi-user authorization, meaning more than one eligible approver must approve the change rather than relying on a single user with RBAC permissions. ‘Statement 3 is No because assigning the Contributor role via PIM does not bypass MUA; since ‘Add or delete network interfaces' is a protected operation under Vault's configuration, it still requires separate approval through the MUA process.","groups":[{"label":"Resource Guard must be in the vault subscription","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Yes"},{"id":"1","text":"No"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Group2 approval is required for protected backup policy changes","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Yes"},{"id":"1","text":"No"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"PIM Contributor can bypass multi-user authorization","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Yes"},{"id":"1","text":"No"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":112,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q112-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q112-answer.webp","explanation":"To block inbound threats based on the OWASP Core Rule Set, you must use Azure Web Application Firewall (WAF), which is specifically designed to inspect HTTP/HTTPS traffic and apply CRS rules against web attacks. To block outbound connections identified by Microsoft Defender Threat Intelligence, you need Azure Firewall with its Threat Intelligence filtering capability, which dynamically blocks traffic to known malicious IP addresses and domains.","groups":[{"label":"For the inbound connections","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Application security groups"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Firewall"},{"id":"2","text":"Azure Web Application Firewall (WAF)"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Entra application proxy"},{"id":"4","text":"Network security groups (NSGs)"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"For the outbound connections","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Application security groups"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Firewall"},{"id":"2","text":"Azure Web Application Firewall (WAF)"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Entra application proxy"},{"id":"4","text":"Network security groups (NSGs)"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":113,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q113-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q113-answer.webp","explanation":"The requirement to manage access using groups in the external tenant (fabrikam.com) is the key differentiator. Standard B2B collaboration requires managing individual guest accounts or dynamic groups within the resource tenant (contoso.com), whereas B2B direct connect allows the external tenant to synchronize specific user and group objects, enabling group-based access control for shared Teams channels without creating individual guest accounts.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Cross-tenant synchronization"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Entra B2B collaboration"},{"id":"C","text":"2B direct connect FEES"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Entra Connect Syne"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":114,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q114-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q114-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Entra Permissions Management is a Cloud Infrastructure Entitlement Management (CIEM) solution specifically designed to discover and visualize role assignments and excessive privileges across Azure, AWS, and GCP. Other options like Azure Lighthouse focus on delegated administration within Azure, while Entra ID Governance primarily handles identity lifecycle management rather than cross-cloud permission discovery.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure Lighthouse"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Defender for Identity"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Entra ID Governance"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Entra Permissions Management"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":115,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q115-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q115-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Workbooks are interactive reports that allow for the creation of custom dashboards to visualize data, including security analyst workload. Playbooks automate response actions by using Logic Apps to execute workflows triggered by security alerts generated by analytics rules.","groups":[{"label":"Custom dashboards","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Notebooks"},{"id":"1","text":"Playbooks"},{"id":"2","text":"Workbooks"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Automated responses","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Notebooks"},{"id":"1","text":"Playbooks"},{"id":"2","text":"Workbooks"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":116,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q116-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q116-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Entra Permissions Management (EPM) is specifically designed to provide a holistic view of privileged role assignments and security risks across multiple Azure subscriptions and resource groups, satisfying the requirement to minimize administrative effort. While Privileged Identity Management (PIM) manages justifications and approvals for specific assignments, it does not provide the broad, automated analytics dashboard needed to identify general security risks across all subscriptions efficiently. Access Reviews in Entra ID or PIM are periodic governance processes rather than continuous discovery tools, and Defender EASM focuses on external attack surface visibility, not internal Azure RBAC risks.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"access reviews in Privileged Identity Management (PIM)"},{"id":"B","text":"access reviews in Microsoft Entra ID Identity Governance"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Defender External Attack Surface Management (Defender EASM) discovery"},{"id":"D","text":"the Analytics dashboard in Microsoft Entra Permissions Management"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":117,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q117-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q117-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Identity is the correct solution because it monitors on-premises Active Directory traffic to detect threats involving sensitive groups like Exchange Organization Management members. It natively supports marking specific AD groups as sensitive and generates alerts sent directly to Microsoft Sentinel via data connectors. This approach minimizes administrative effort by leveraging built-in detection rules rather than requiring custom logic found in Entra ID PIM or broader identity protection tools.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Defender for Identity"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Entra ID Protection"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Entra Privileged Identity Management (PIM)"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Defender for Office 365,"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":118,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q118-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q118-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Sentinel workspaces are scoped to a single Azure subscription and its linked Microsoft Entra tenant. Since Sub2, Sub3, and Sub4 have unique tenants, you must create three separate workspaces to ingest logs from each. Azure Lighthouse is the required service because it allows you to manage resources across multiple subscriptions and tenants from a central perspective with minimal administrative overhead.","groups":[{"label":"Minimum Sentinel workspaces","options":[{"id":"0","text":"1"},{"id":"1","text":"2"},{"id":"2","text":"3"},{"id":"3","text":"4"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Azure service for cross-tenant management","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Arc"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Bastion"},{"id":"2","text":"Azure Lighthouse"},{"id":"3","text":"Azure Private Link"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":119,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q119-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q119-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Intune Endpoint Privilege Management (EPM) is the correct solution because it allows administrators to grant specific applications elevated privileges for standard users without granting them full local administrative rights. This directly satisfies the requirement to run apps needing admin rights while ensuring they operate within the security context of the signed-in user, unlike LAPS which manages passwords or PIM which manages role-based access.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Windows Local Administrator Password Solution (Windows LAPS)"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Entra Permissions Management"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Intune Endpoint Privilege Management"},{"id":"D","text":"Privileged Identity Management (PIM) in Microsoft Entra ID"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":120,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q120-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q120-answer.webp","explanation":"Group2 must implement a compliance policy in Intune to detect and mark devices with disabled encryption as non-compliant. Group! must implement a Conditional Access policy in Microsoft Entra ID to block access to Microsoft 365 services for any device that is not marked as compliant.","groups":[{"label":"Group1","options":[{"id":"0","text":"A Conditional Access policy"},{"id":"1","text":"A sign-in risk policy in Microsoft Entra ID Protection"},{"id":"2","text":"A user risk policy in Microsoft Entra ID Protection"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Defender for Office 365"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Group2","options":[{"id":"0","text":"A compliance policy in Intune"},{"id":"1","text":"A configuration profile in Intune"},{"id":"2","text":"A Defender for Endpoint attack surface reduction (ASR) rule"},{"id":"3","text":"An endpoint security policy"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":121,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q121-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q121-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint's Application Control (powered by Windows Defender Application Control) is the specific feature designed to restrict execution to authorized applications using allowlists or blocklists. This directly satisfies the requirement to automatically block unauthorized applications on Windows Server VMs until administrator approval is granted.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"app registrations in the Microsoft Entra tenant"},{"id":"B","text":"OAuth app policies in Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"C","text":"app protection policies in Microsoft Endpoint Manager"},{"id":"D","text":"application control policies in Microsoft Defender for Endpoint"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":122,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q122-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q122-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Purview Data Loss Prevention (DLP) is designed to detect and prevent the unauthorized sharing of sensitive information across various locations, including OneDrive for Business and external websites. While sensitivity labels (Information Protection) classify data, DLP policies enforce the conditional access rules that block uploads of that classified data to unauthorized external destinations.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Purview Information Protection"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Purview data loss prevention (DLP)"},{"id":"C","text":"web content filtering in Defender for Endpoint"},{"id":"D","text":"an endpoint security policy"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":123,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q123-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q123-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Entra Permissions Management is the correct service for both Azure and AWS subscriptions because it provides Continuous Identity Entitlement Management (CIEM) capabilities across multicloud environments. It meets all requirements by dynamically discovering permissions, calculating risk metrics based on usage, automatically revoking unused permissions (e.g., after 90 days), and supporting just-in-time access scenarios to minimize administrative overhead.","groups":[{"label":"Azure","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Entra External ID"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Entra Permissions Management"},{"id":"3","text":"Privileged Identity Management (PIM) in Microsoft Entra ID"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"AWS","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Entra External ID"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Entra Permissions Management"},{"id":"3","text":"Privileged Identity Management (PIM) in Microsoft Entra ID"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":124,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q124-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q124-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Identity is specifically designed to detect threats such as privilege escalation attacks within on-premises Active Directory environments. Since the scenario involves monitoring an on-premises AD DS domain (Domain1), this solution fits the requirement, whereas Microsoft Entra ID Protection focuses on cloud identities and PIM manages access rather than detecting attacks.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Entra ID Protection"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Defender for Servers"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Defender for Identity"},{"id":"D","text":"Privileged Identity Management (PIM)"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":125,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q125-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q125-answer.webp","explanation":"According to Microsoft's CAF best practices and Azure DevOps documentation, the recommended approach for ARM service connections is using an App Registration with Workload Identity Federation. This method automatically creates a Service Principal without requiring long-lived sectets, thereby adhering to the principle of least privilege and eliminating secret rotation overhead.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"service principals and secrets"},{"id":"B","text":"workload identity federation and service principals"},{"id":"C","text":"workload identity federation and user-assigned managed identities"},{"id":"D","text":"workload identity federation and system-assigned managed identities"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":126,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q126-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q126-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud provides built-in regulatory compliance mappings for standards like NIST, assessing Azure resources against specific control requirements. Microsoft Purview Compliance Manager is the unified tool for managing risk and compliance across Microsoft 365 and Azure workloads, offering detailed guidance for frameworks like GDPR.","groups":[{"label":"or NIST","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender Vulnerability Management"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"For GDPR","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Priva"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Purview Communication Compliance"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Purview Compliance Manager"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":127,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q127-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q127-answer.webp","explanation":"Adaptive scopes enable the dynamic application of compliance policies based on specific attributes defined in Exchange Online, such as custom department or location fields. This directly satisfies the requirement to assign policies based on custom attributes while minimizing administrative effort by automating scope membership updates.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"adaptive scopes"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft 365 Defender user tags"},{"id":"C","text":"administrative units"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Purview sensitivity labels"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":128,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q128-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q128-answer.webp","explanation":"To streamline the creation of subject rights requests with minimal development effort, you should use Microsoft Graph API, which provides a standardized way to programmatically create SRRs without building custom UI logic. For processing these requests, Power Automate is the recommended solution as it allows for automated workflows to handle approvals and notifications within the Priva framework.","groups":[{"label":"To streamline creation","options":[{"id":"0","text":"The Microsoft Graph API"},{"id":"1","text":"The Microsoft Office 365 Management API"},{"id":"2","text":"The Microsoft Office 365 service"},{"id":"3","text":"The Microsoft Office 365 Communications API"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"To streamline processing","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Automation"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Logic Apps"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Power Automate"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":129,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q129-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q129-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps (formerly MCAS) provides real-time session controls that can trigger re-authentication or Conditional Access checks during risky activities, such as downloading sensitive files from SharePoint. This integration allows the system to re-evaluate access policies dynamically at the moment of download, ensuring compliance with sensitivity label rules.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Defender for Office 365"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Entra application proxy"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":130,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q130-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q130-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud supports multi-cloud environments, allowing Fabrikam to assess AWS EC2 instances for compliance and notify administrators of noncompliance. Microsoft Sentinel provides built-in connectors for AWS services (such as CloudTrail), enabling security administrators to ingest and query AWS logs directly from the Azure environment.","groups":[{"label":"For the AWS EC2 instances","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Blueprints"},{"id":"1","text":"Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Endpoint Manager"},{"id":"4","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"For the AWS service logs","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Blueprints"},{"id":"1","text":"Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Endpoint Manager"},{"id":"4","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"}],"correct":["4"]}]},{"id":131,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q131-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q131-answer.webp","explanation":"To automatically remediate non-compliant virtual machines, you must configure an Azure Automation account, which provides the compute resource for Defender for Cloud's automated remediation actions. To exclude the TestRG resource group from compliance assessment while maintaining the standard for others, you should update the Azure Policy assignment scope to explicitly exclude that specific resource group.","groups":[{"label":"To enforce compliance to the regulatory standard, create","options":[{"id":"0","text":"An Azure Automation account"},{"id":"1","text":"A managed identity"},{"id":"2","text":"Workflow automation"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"To exclude TestRG from the compliance assessment","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Modify a Defender for Cloud workflow automation"},{"id":"1","text":"Modify an Azure policy definition"},{"id":"2","text":"Update an Azure policy assignment"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":132,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q132-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q132-answer.webp","explanation":"The Zero Trust principle of explicit verification requires authenticating and authorizing every access request based on available data points like identity, device health, and context. Conditional Access is the specific Microsoft Entra ID engine that enforces these policies by evaluating such signals to permit or deny access. Other options like Defender for Cloud or Identity provide telemetry data or governance functions but do not directly enforce the authentication and authorization decisions required for explicit verification.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Conditional Access"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Defender for Identity"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Entra ID Identity Governance"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":133,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q133-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q133-answer.webp","explanation":"Authentication policies and authentication policy silos do not apply to the built-in domain Administrator account (RID 500), as this account is explicitly exempt from them by design. To restrict logon rights specifically to interactive sign-ins on domain controllers, you must configure the User Rights Assignment security policy settings, such as ‘Log on locally’ or ‘Allow log on through Remote Desktop Services’, within a Group Policy Object.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"the Protected Users group"},{"id":"B","text":"authentication policies"},{"id":"C","text":"the User Rights Assignment security policy settings"},{"id":"D","text":"an authentication policy silo"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":134,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q134-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q134-answer.webp","explanation":"For the first requirement, configure a **Threat intelligence connector** to integrate Microsoft Sentinel with third-party vendors and ingest data regarding known malware indicators. For the second requirement, create a **Scheduled query rule** (also known as a Scheduled analytics rule) to continuously search logs for specific patterns, such as command-and-control IP addresses, and automatically generate incidents when those patterns are detected.","groups":[{"label":"ntegrate Microsoft Sentinel with a third-party security vendor","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Custom entity activities"},{"id":"1","text":"A playbook"},{"id":"2","text":"A threat detection rule"},{"id":"3","text":"A threat indicator"},{"id":"4","text":"A threat Intelligence connector"}],"correct":["4"]},{"label":"Automatically generate incidents","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Custom entity activities"},{"id":"1","text":"A playbook"},{"id":"2","text":"A scheduled query rule"},{"id":"3","text":"A threat indicator"},{"id":"4","text":"A threat Intelligence data connector"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":135,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q135-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q135-answer.webp","explanation":"The first requirement (real-time file labeling during download) is handled by a Session policy, which monitors and controls user sessions in cloud apps like SharePoint Online, enabling features such as real-time DLP and content inspection. The second requirement (restricting sign-in based on device compliance) is enforced by an Access policy, which works with Microsoft Entra Conditional Access to determine access rights based on conditions like Intune compliance status.","groups":[{"label":"Label SharePoint downloads in real time","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Session policy"},{"id":"1","text":"Access policy"},{"id":"2","text":"Activity policy"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Allow only compliant devices into Dropbox","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Access policy"},{"id":"1","text":"Activity policy"},{"id":"2","text":"OAuth app policy"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":136,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q136-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q136-answer.webp","explanation":"To meet the ‘Windows only’ requirement with minimal policies, use one Conditional Access policy that excludes non-Windows devices (macOS) from accessing Microsoft Entra resources. For Azure Service Management API access, a second policy can enforce MFA and Group1 membership while using an Include device filter for ExtensionAttributel = SecureWorkstation to satisfy the specific device requirement. This results in 2 policies, 1 Exclude filter (for macOS), and 1 Include filter (for SecureWorkstation).","groups":[{"label":"Conditional Access policies","options":[{"id":"0","text":"One policy"},{"id":"1","text":"Two policies"},{"id":"2","text":"Three policies"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Device filters","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Two exclude device filters"},{"id":"1","text":"Two include device filters"},{"id":"2","text":"One include and one exclude device filter"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":137,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q137-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q137-answer.webp","explanation":"Windows LAPS should be selected for both hotspots because it provides the required functionality for backing up DSRM credentials to AD DS and managing local administrator passwords (User1). It ensures automatic password rotation every 60 days, prevents manual changes, stores credentials in an encrypted format within Active Directory, and minimizes administrative effort by automating these processes.","groups":[{"label":"For the User1 credentials","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Entra Password Protection"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Entra Password Protection for Active Directory Domain Services"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Local Administrator Password Solution (Microsoft LAPS)"},{"id":"3","text":"Windows Local Administrator Password Solution (Windows LAPS)"}],"correct":["3"]},{"label":"For the DSRM credentials","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Entra Password Protection"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Entra Password Protection for Active Directory Domain Services"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Local Administrator Password Solution (Microsoft LAPS)"},{"id":"3","text":"Windows Local Administrator Password Solution (Windows LAPS)"}],"correct":["3"]}]},{"id":138,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q138-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q138-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Entra Domain Services is the only Azure service listed that provides a managed domain join and group policy management experience with full LDAP compatibility. This allows legacy applications, such as Appt, to query user attributes via standard LDAP protocols without requiring on-premises Active Directory infrastructure.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Configure a Conditional Access policy that has a trusted network location."},{"id":"B","text":"Deploy Microsoft Entra Private Access."},{"id":"C","text":"Deploy Microsoft Entra Domain Services."},{"id":"D","text":"Implement pass-through authentication."}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":139,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q139-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q139-answer.webp","explanation":"According to Microsoft documentation for configuring a verifier in Entra Verified ID, you must first add and verify a custom domain in your tenant. This step is a prerequisite for the Quick Verified ID setup wizard to proceed, as it establishes the trusted identity anchor for the issued credentials.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"a security principal in contoso.onmicrosoft.com"},{"id":"B","text":"a custom domain in contoso.onmicrosoft.com"},{"id":"C","text":"a user-assigned managed identity in Sub1"},{"id":"D","text":"an Azure key vault in Sub1"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":140,"type":"drag","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q140-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q140-answer.webp","explanation":"The question requires applying a Conditional Access policy to a specific SharePoint site using authentication contexts. First, you must create the authentication context in Microsoft Entra ID (now Microsoft Entra Conditional Access). Second, you need to configure a sensitivity label or apply the context directly to Site1 to associate the resource with that context. Third, you modify Policy's target resources to include the authentication context, ensuring the policy applies only when that context is present.","groups":[{"label":"First action","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Create authentication context"},{"id":"1","text":"Configure sensitivity label for Site1"},{"id":"2","text":"Modify Policy target resources"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Second action","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Create authentication context"},{"id":"1","text":"Configure sensitivity label for Site1"},{"id":"2","text":"Modify Policy target resources"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Third action","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Create authentication context"},{"id":"1","text":"Configure sensitivity label for Site1"},{"id":"2","text":"Modify Policy target resources"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":141,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q141-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q141-answer.webp","explanation":"To apply Conditional Access policies to a third-party SaaS application, the application must first be registered in Microsoft Entra ID as an enterprise application. This registration creates the necessary identity object that allows administrators to assign users and define access conditions like device state.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"an enterprise application"},{"id":"B","text":"a Microsoft Entra application proxy connector group"},{"id":"C","text":"a Conditional Access policy"},{"id":"D","text":"an access package"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":142,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q142-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q142-answer.webp","explanation":"To enable automatic remediation of non-compliant resources (such as adding missing tags), the policy effect must be set to **Modify**. The Modify effect requires defining the identity used to apply changes, which is specified by providing a role definition ID in the **roleDefinitionlds** property within the policy definition.","groups":[{"label":"Property to add","options":[{"id":"0","text":"conflictEffect"},{"id":"1","text":"existenceCondition"},{"id":"2","text":"roleDefinitionIds"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Effect to use","options":[{"id":"0","text":"append"},{"id":"1","text":"modify"},{"id":"2","text":"mutate"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":143,"type":"drag","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q143-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q143-answer.webp","explanation":"First, enable a managed identity on the storage account to provide an identity for accessing Azure services. Next, configure the Key Vault access policy (or RBAC role) to grant that managed identity permission to unwrap encryption keys from the vault. Finally, update the storage account's encryption settings to use customer-managed keys, pointing to the specific key in the Key Vault.","groups":[{"label":"First action","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Enable managed identity on storage account"},{"id":"1","text":"Grant that identity Key Vault key access"},{"id":"2","text":"Configure customer-managed keys"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Second action","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Enable managed identity on storage account"},{"id":"1","text":"Grant that identity Key Vault key access"},{"id":"2","text":"Configure customer-managed keys"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Third action","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Enable managed identity on storage account"},{"id":"1","text":"Grant that identity Key Vault key access"},{"id":"2","text":"Configure customer-managed keys"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":144,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q144-question.webp","answerImage":null,"explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"GitHub Advanced Security"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Defender for Key Vault"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Dev Box"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud DevOps security"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":145,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q145-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q145-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Entra Private Access is designed specifically as a Zero Trust Network Access (ZTNA) solution for internal private resources like on-premises servers. It enforces Conditional Access policies directly at the device level, allowing authorized users to access Server1 from the internet without requiring a traditional VPN connection.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Entra application proxy"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Entra Internet Access"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Entra Private Access"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure Application Gateway"},{"id":"E","text":"Azure Bastion"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":146,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q146-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q146-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Servers Plan 2 includes a data ingestion benefit that allows customers to ingest up to 1 GB per day per resource into Microsoft Sentinel at no additional cost. Since the requirement is to ingest approximately 350 MB per day from each of the 10 domain controllers, this falls well within the free tier limit, thereby minimizing costs while ensuring all security logs are captured. Other options like upgrading licenses or configuring volume caps do not address the specific cost minimization for high-volume DC log ingestion as effectively.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Upgrade the user licenses to Microsoft 365 E5."},{"id":"B","text":"Onboard each domain controller to Microsoft Defender for Servers Plan 2."},{"id":"C","text":"Configure Auxiliary logs in WS1."},{"id":"D","text":"Configure a volume cap for WS1."},{"id":"E","text":"Only ingest data from one domain controller into WS1."}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":147,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q147-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q147-answer.webp","explanation":"For granting external users access to specific Teams channels while minimizing administrative effort and avoiding guest account clutter, Microsoft Entra B2B collaboration is the standard solution (often enabled via Shared Channels). For allowing App! users to authenticate using social media accounts, you must configure Microsoft Entra External ID for customers (formerly B2C) which supports social identity providers like Google, Facebook, etc.","groups":[{"label":"Teams channel permissions","options":[{"id":"0","text":"B2B direct connect"},{"id":"1","text":"Cross-tenant synchronization"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Entra B2B collaboration"},{"id":"3","text":"External ID for customers"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"App1 social sign-in","options":[{"id":"0","text":"B2B direct connect"},{"id":"1","text":"Cross-tenant synchronization"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Entra B2B collaboration"},{"id":"3","text":"External ID for customers"}],"correct":["3"]}]},{"id":148,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q148-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q148-answer.webp","explanation":"To prevent users from uploading unlabeled sensitive files to SharePoint Online, you must use Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps (MDCA). Within MDCA, an App control policy is the specific mechanism used to define rules that block or allow actions, such as file uploads, based on content sensitivity and labeling status.","groups":[{"label":"Policy type","options":[{"id":"0","text":"App control policy"},{"id":"1","text":"App protection policy"},{"id":"2","text":"Compliance policy"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Microsoft 365 service","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Endpoint data loss prevention (Endpoint DLP)"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps."},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Purview insider risk management"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":149,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q149-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q149-answer.webp","explanation":"The correct service is Microsoft Purview Compliance Manager, as it provides regulatory-specific compliance assessments and actionable guidance for improvement across different countries. Premium regulatory templates within Compliance Manager require additional licensing costs beyond the standard E5 subscription.","groups":[{"label":"Use","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud regulatory compliance"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Purview Audit (Premium)"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Purview Compliance Manager"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Priva Privacy Risk Management"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Additional costs","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Priva Privacy Risk Management licenses"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Purview Data Map capacity units"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Syntex pay-as-you-go billing"},{"id":"3","text":"Licenses for premium regulatory templates"}],"correct":["3"]}]},{"id":150,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q150-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q150-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Entra ID Protection is the correct solution for identifying and stopping brute-force attacks, as it detects risky sign-ins based on signals like impossible travel and leaked credentials. Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps is the appropriate tool for detecting data exfiltration from SharePoint Online, as it monitors user activity in cloud apps to identify sensitive file access and anomalous download behavior.","groups":[{"label":"Automatically identifies and stops external, brute force attacks against accounts","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Identity"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Entra ID Identity Governance"},{"id":"4","text":"Microsoft Entra ID Protection"}],"correct":["4"]},{"label":"Automatically identifies and stops external attacks that use an internal account to 2 exfiltrate data from SharePoint Online sites","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Identity"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Entra ID Identity Governance"},{"id":"4","text":"Microsoft Entra ID Protection"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":151,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q151-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q151-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Purview Compliance Manager provides a compliance dashboard that helps organizations assess their readiness against various standards and local privacy regulations. It offers actionable guidance and best practice recommendations to close gaps, directly addressing the requirement for regulatory adherence guidance while minimizing administrative effort through centralized tracking.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Data Loss Prevention"},{"id":"B","text":"Information Protection"},{"id":"C","text":"Insider Risk Management"},{"id":"D","text":"Compliance Manager"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":152,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q152-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q152-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Identity (formerly Azure ATP) supports Honeytokens, which are decoy credentials planted on systems to detect credential theft. When an attacker uses these fake cached credentials, Defender for Identity generates an alert, enabling Sentinel rules to detect the attack.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"User and Entity Behavior Analytics (UEBA) in Microsoft Sentinel"},{"id":"B","text":"a deception rule in Microsoft Defender for Endpoint"},{"id":"C","text":"a user risk policy in Microsoft Entra ID Protection"},{"id":"D","text":"a Honeytoken tag in Microsoft Defender for Identity"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":153,"type":"multiple","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q153-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q153-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Intune compliance policies are used to define device health requirements, such as enforcing a minimum OS version, marking devices running early builds of Windows 11 as non- compliant. Microsoft Entra Conditional Access policies then enforce these compliance states by blocking access to Microsoft 365 resources for any device marked as non-compliant.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint endpoint security policy"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud regulatory compliance policy"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Intune compliance policy"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Entra ID Protection sign-in risk policy"},{"id":"E","text":"Microsoft Entra Conditional Access policy"}],"correct":["C","E"]}]},{"id":154,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q154-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q154-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps is the correct service because it provides Cloud Access Broker capabilities that monitor user activities across SaaS applications like SharePoint Online. Specifically, Anomaly Detection policies identify unusual behavior patterns, such as atypical data downloads, by establishing behavioral baselines and flagging deviations from normal usage.","groups":[{"label":"Service","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender for Office 365"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Purview Data Loss Prevention (DLP)"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Purview Information Protection"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Policy","options":[{"id":"0","text":"A data loss prevention (DLP) policy applied to SharePoint Online"},{"id":"1","text":"A file policy"},{"id":"2","text":"An anomaly detection policy"},{"id":"3","text":"An Endpoint data loss prevention (Endpoint DLP) policy"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":155,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q155-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q155-answer.webp","explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"n Azure, deploy","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Monitor data collection endpoints"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Monitor data collection rules (DCRs)"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud data collection settings"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"On the virtual machines, install","options":[{"id":"0","text":"the Azure Connected Machine agent"},{"id":"1","text":"the Network Controller role"},{"id":"2","text":"the Azure Pipelines agent"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":156,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q156-question.webp","answerImage":null,"explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Entra ID Protection"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Defender for Servers"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Defender for Identity"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":157,"type":"hotspot","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q157-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q157-answer.webp","explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Role","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Global Administrator"},{"id":"1","text":"Privileged Role Administrator"},{"id":"2","text":"Security Administrator"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Tool","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Access packages"},{"id":"1","text":"Access reviews"},{"id":"2","text":"Lifecycle workflows"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":158,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q158-question.webp","answerImage":null,"explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"a Windows Autopilot deployment profile"},{"id":"B","text":"a compliance policy"},{"id":"C","text":"an app protection policy"},{"id":"D","text":"an app configuration policy"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":159,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q159-question.webp","answerImage":null,"explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"the regulatory compliance dashboard in Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure role-based access control (Azure RBAC)"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure initiative"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":160,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q160-question.webp","answerImage":null,"explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Access Package Manager"},{"id":"B","text":"Identity Governance Administrator"},{"id":"C","text":"Catalog Owner"},{"id":"D","text":"Access Package Assignment Manager"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":161,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q161-question.webp","answerImage":null,"explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Entra Privileged Identity Management"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Intune Endpoint Privilege Management"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Entra entitlement management"},{"id":"D","text":"Windows Local Administrator Password Solution (Windows LAPS)"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":162,"type":"single","topic":2,"questionImage":"images/q162-question.webp","answerImage":null,"explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"the Microsoft Sentinel Health Summary dashboard"},{"id":"B","text":"the MITRE analytics explorer"},{"id":"C","text":"the MITRE coverage matrix"},{"id":"D","text":"the Microsoft Sentinel Analytics blade"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":163,"type":"multiple","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q163-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q163-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Sentinel threat intelligence workbooks (A) provide interactive visualizations and analysis of security data, aiding in the investigation of alerts. Defender for Cloud threat intelligence reports (C) offer contextual details about threats along with specific remediation recommendations to address identified vulnerabilities.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Sentinel threat intelligence workbooks"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Sentinel notebooks"},{"id":"C","text":"threat intelligence reports in Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"D","text":"workload protections in Defender for Cloud"}],"correct":["A","C"]}]},{"id":164,"type":"multiple","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q164-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q164-answer.webp","explanation":"To include AKS resources in the Secure Score, you must first enable the Microsoft Defender for Containers plan (A), as disabled plans exclude resources from security recommendations. Additionally, configuring auto-provisioning (B) ensures that the necessary security components and policies are deployed to all current and future AKS clusters across subscriptions, guaranteeing consistent coverage and accurate score calculations.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Enable Defender plans."},{"id":"B","text":"Configure auto provisioning."},{"id":"C","text":"Add a workflow automation."},{"id":"D","text":"Assign regulatory compliance policies."},{"id":"E","text":"Review the inventory."}],"correct":["A","B"]}]},{"id":165,"type":"multiple","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q165-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q165-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Bastion provides secure RDP/SSH access over SSL via the Azure portal without requiring public IP addresses on VMs or a VPN. To minimize costs across two virtual networks, you should deploy Azure Bastion in one network and use virtual network peering (hub-and-spoke) to allow that instance to manage resources in both networks.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Create a hub and spoke network by using virtual network peering."},{"id":"B","text":"Deploy Azure Bastion to each virtual network."},{"id":"C","text":"Deploy Azure Bastion to one virtual network."},{"id":"D","text":"Create NAT rules and network rules in Azure Firewall."},{"id":"E","text":"Enable just-in-time VM access on the virtual machines."}],"correct":["A","C"]}]},{"id":166,"type":"hotspot","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q166-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q166-answer.webp","explanation":"For Data Security, Azure Key Vault is the recommended solution to securely store and manage sensitive information like access keys or certificates used by the runbook. For Network Access Control, Azure Private Link enables private connectivity from the Azure Automation account to Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2, keeping traffic within the Microsoft backbone network and preventing exposure to the public internet.","groups":[{"label":"Data security","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Access keys stored in Azure Key Vault"},{"id":"1","text":"Automation Contributor built-in role"},{"id":"2","text":"Azure Private Link with network service tags"},{"id":"3","text":"Azure Web Application Firewall rules with network service tags"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Network access control","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Access keys stored in Azure Key Vault"},{"id":"1","text":"Automation Contributor built-in role"},{"id":"2","text":"Azure Private Link with network service tags"},{"id":"3","text":"Azure Web Application Firewall rules with network service tags"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":167,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q167-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q167-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint provides native web content filtering capabilities specifically designed to block access to harmful or inappropriate website categories. Within the Microsoft 365 Defender portal, administrators can configure policies that explicitly include site categories such as ‘Adult content’ and ‘Gambling’. This feature runs directly on the endpoint (Windows 11 devices) without requiring additional cloud proxy infrastructure like Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Compliance Manager"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Endpoint Manager"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":168,"type":"multiple","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q168-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q168-answer.webp","explanation":"Onboarding to Microsoft Intune and Defender for Endpoint (B) provides the management infrastructure required to enforce application control policies, ensuring only authorized apps run on the kiosks. Implementing Threat and Vulnerability Management (C) fulfills the requirement to regularly harden the devices against new threats by continuously assessing vulnerabilities and prioritizing remediation.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Implement Automated investigation and Remediation (AIR) in Microsoft Defender for Endpoint."},{"id":"B","text":"Onboard the kiosks to Microsoft intune and Microsoft Defender for Endpoint."},{"id":"C","text":"Implement threat and vulnerability management in Microsoft Defender for Endpoint."},{"id":"D","text":"Onboard the kiosks to Azure Monitor."},{"id":"E","text":"Implement Privileged Access Workstation (PAW) for the kiosks."}],"correct":["B","C"]}]},{"id":169,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q169-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q169-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Information Protection (MIP) provides the capability to apply visual content markings, such as watermarks and headers/footers, to documents and email attachments based on sensitivity labels. While Azure Purview is the modern unified platform hosting these features, Microsoft Information Protection remains the specific technical component defined for data classification and protection tasks like watermarking in certification contexts.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Information Protection"},{"id":"C","text":"insider risk management"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure Purview"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":170,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q170-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q170-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Application Gateway with the Web Application Firewall (WAF) feature is designed specifically to protect web applications from common exploits and malicious bot attacks by filtering HTTP/HTTPS traffic based on OWASP rules. While NSGs operate at layer 3/4 and cannot inspect application-layer content for vulnerability scanning patterns, WAF provides deep packet inspection to block known attack signatures. Traffic Manager is a DNS-based global traffic distributor that lacks security inspection capabilities.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure Firewall Premium"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure Traffic Manager and application security groups"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Application Gateway Web Application Firewall (WAF)"},{"id":"D","text":"network security groups (NSGs)"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":171,"type":"single","topic":34,"questionImage":"images/q171-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q171-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft-managed keys (MMK) are rotated automatically by Microsoft on an unspecified schedule, which prevents you from enforcing a specific monthly rotation policy. To meet the requirement for manual or scheduled key rotation (e.g., monthly), you must use Customer-Managed Keys (CMKs) stored in Azure Key Vault.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":172,"type":"single","topic":34,"questionImage":"images/q172-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q172-answer.webp","explanation":"The scenario explicitly requires the ability to rotate encryption keys monthly. Microsoft-managed keys (MMK) are rotated automatically by Azure infrastructure without user control or visibility into the schedule, meaning you cannot enforce a specific monthly rotation policy. To meet the requirement, you must use Customer-Managed Keys (CMK) via Azure Key Vault, which allows you to define and execute custom key rotation intervals.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":173,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q173-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q173-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Storage automatically encrypts data at rest using AES-256 encryption by default. Customer-managed keys (CMKs) stored in Azure Key Vault allow you to define and enforce specific key rotation schedules, such as monthly rotations, which meets the stated requirement. Microsoft-managed keys rotate automatically but do not allow customers to specify rotation frequency.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":174,"type":"single","topic":34,"questionImage":"images/q174-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q174-answer.webp","explanation":"The proposed solution is insufficient because IP address filtering alone does not guarantee that traffic originates from your specific Front Door profile, as Azure reuses backend IP ranges across customers. Microsoft documentation explicitly states that you must also validate the X-Azure-FDID request header to ensure origin security.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":175,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q175-question.webp","answerImage":null,"explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":176,"type":"single","topic":34,"questionImage":"images/q176-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q176-answer.webp","explanation":"The solution meets the goal because Azure App Service supports access restrictions based on the X-Azure-FDID HTTP header, which contains a unique identifier for your specific Front Door profile. This method ensures that traffic originates from your authorized Front Door instance, preventing unauthorized direct access even if an attacker spoofs the source IP address.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":177,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q177-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q177-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Firewall is the recommended solution because it acts as a centralized egress point for all outbound traffic from the Azure App Service Environment (ASE), allowing all connections to on- premises resources to exit through a single public IP address. This directly satisfies the requirement to minimize the number of public IP addresses allowed to access the on-premises network, whereas other options like Application Gateway or Front Door do not inherently consolidate outbound egress IPs for backend-to-backend hybrid communication in this manner.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure Traffic Manager with priority traffic-routing methods"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure Firewall with policy rule sets"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Front Door with Azure Web Application Firewall (WAF)"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure Application Gateway v2 with user-defined routes (UDRs)"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":178,"type":"multiple","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q178-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q178-answer.webp","explanation":"To audit data access in Azure Cosmos DB, you must enable diagnostic logs and send them to a Log Analytics workspace (Option C) to capture detailed request-level activities. Since the goal is to audit specific users rather than service principals or key-based identities, sending Azure Active Directory sign-in logs (Option A) allows you to correlate identity authentication events with database access patterns. Disabling local authentication (Option D) enforces Azure AD use but does not itself provide the logging mechanism required for auditing.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Send the Azure Active Directory (Azure AD) sign-in logs to a Log Analytics workspace."},{"id":"B","text":"Enable Microsoft Defender for Identity."},{"id":"C","text":"Send the Azure Cosmos DB logs to a Log Analytics workspace."},{"id":"D","text":"Disable local authentication for Azure Cosmos DB."},{"id":"E","text":"Enable Microsoft Defender for Cosmos DB."}],"correct":["A","C"]}]},{"id":179,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q179-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q179-answer.webp","explanation":"Applying a read-only lock on the storage account prevents users from listing or regenerating access keys, which stops new applications from obtaining them. This satisfies the security requirement while causing minimal impact on legacy applications that already possess the keys and continue to function normally. Option A is incorrect because disabling AllowSharedKeyAccess would block all shared key authentication, breaking the existing legacy applications rather than just preventing new ones from acquiring credentials.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Set the AllowSharedKeyAccess property to false."},{"id":"B","text":"Apply read-only locks on the storage accounts."},{"id":"C","text":"Set the AllowBlobPublicAccess property to false."},{"id":"D","text":"Configure automated key rotation."}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":180,"type":"multiple","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q180-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q180-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Containers provides vulnerability assessment for container images and host agents on Linux-based environments, including Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS) and Azure Container Registry (ACR). Windows container support for vulnerability scanning in these specific Azure-native services was introduced later and is often treated as a preview or distinct feature, whereas Linux support is fully established and standard for certification exams. Therefore, the two environments fully supported for scanning known vulnerabilities in the context of standard exam objectives are Linux containers on AKS and Linux containers on ACR.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Linux containers deployed to Azure Container Instances"},{"id":"B","text":"Windows containers deployed to Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS)"},{"id":"C","text":"Windows containers deployed to Azure Container Registry"},{"id":"D","text":"Linux containers deployed to Azure Container Registry"},{"id":"E","text":"Linux containers deployed to Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS)"}],"correct":["D","E"]}]},{"id":181,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q181-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q181-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure AD Application Proxy is the correct solution because it acts as a reverse proxy to publish specific on-premises web applications to the internet without exposing the entire network, thereby meeting the requirement to prevent access to other resources. It natively integrates with Azure AD Conditional Access policies for granular security controls and supports Seamless SSO (using Kerberos), which simplifies the end-user experience by eliminating the need for additional VPN configurations or credentials.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure AD Application Proxy"},{"id":"B","text":"web content filtering in Microsoft Defender for Endpoint"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Tunnel"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure Virtual WAN"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":182,"type":"multiple","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q182-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q182-answer.webp","explanation":"Compliant Network Check is a feature within Microsoft Entra Global Secure Access that enables Conditional Access to evaluate the security posture of the network connection itself. It supports enforcing policies for both cloud applications, such as third-party SaaS apps, and on- premises applications accessed via Microsoft Entra Application Proxy or Tunnel agents. Continuous Access Evaluation (Option C) is a broader signaling mechanism, not a specific scenario supported exclusively by compliant network check configuration, and branch office computers (Option B) are excluded because they are not enrolled in Intune.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"connections to the third-party SaaS app"},{"id":"B","text":"connections from the branch office computers"},{"id":"C","text":"Continuous Access Evaluation for Microsoft Exchange Online"},{"id":"D","text":"connections to the on-premises apps"}],"correct":["A","D"]}]},{"id":183,"type":"hotspot","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q183-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q183-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure AD B2C is the correct choice for customer authentication because it is specifically designed to enable identity management for consumer-facing applications and supports social identity providers like Facebook. Azure AD B28 collaboration is the appropriate solution for partner access, as it allows external users to sign in with their existing work or school identities and grants them access to specific resources like SharePoint Online via Access Packages, minimizing infrastructure deployment.","groups":[{"label":"For the customers","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure AD 828 authentication with access package assignments"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure AD B2C authentication"},{"id":"2","text":"Federation in Azure AD Connect with Active Directory Federation Services"},{"id":"3","text":"Pass-through authentication in Azure AD Connect"},{"id":"4","text":"Password hash ‘hronization in Azure AD Connect"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"For the partners","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure AD B2B authentication with access package assignments"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure AD B2C authentication"},{"id":"2","text":"Federation in Azure AD Connect with Active Directory Federation Services"},{"id":"3","text":"Pass-through authentication in Azure AD Connect,"},{"id":"4","text":"Password hash synchronization in Azure AD Connect"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":184,"type":"multiple","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q184-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q184-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Bastion enables secure RDP/SSH connectivity directly from the Azure portal without exposing ports 3389 or 22 to the internet, satisfying the requirement to use the portal and avoid public port exposure. Just-in-Time (JIT) VM access ensures that network permissions are only active when explicitly requested by an administrator, meeting the ‘only provide permission... when required’ constraint.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Configure Azure VPN Gateway."},{"id":"B","text":"Enable Just Enough Administration (JEA)."},{"id":"C","text":"Configure Azure Bastion."},{"id":"D","text":"Enable just-in-time (JIT) VM access."},{"id":"E","text":"Enable Azure Active Directory (Azure AD) Privileged Identity Management (PIM) roles as virtual machine contributors."}],"correct":["C","D"]}]},{"id":185,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q185-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q185-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure SQL Database (PaaS) minimizes operational requirements for patching and backups compared to VMs or Managed Instances, as Microsoft manages the underlying infrastructure. It is also the most cost-effective option among the choices, satisfying the requirement to minimize costs while still supporting Dynamic Data Masking.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure SQL Managed Instance"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure Synapse Analytics dedicated SQL pools"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure SQL Database"},{"id":"D","text":"SQL Server on Azure Virtual Machines"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":186,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q186-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q186-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Bastion operates at the virtual network level and can manage connections to VMs in peered \\VNets through a single deployment. Based on the provided design, VNet! and VNet2 are connected via a hub (forming one logical group), while VNet3 and VNet4 form another distinct peered group. Therefore, only two Azure Bastion subnets are required: one for the first group and one for the second.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"1"},{"id":"B","text":"2"},{"id":"C","text":"3"},{"id":"D","text":"4"},{"id":"E","text":"5"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":187,"type":"hotspot","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q187-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q187-answer.webp","explanation":"For the phase of uploading code to repositories, GitHub Enterprise is the appropriate tool because it integrates scanning directly into version control workflows to detect vulnerabilities upon commit. For the phase of building containers, Azure Pipelines is the correct choice as it provides Cl/CD capabilities to run automated security scans during the container image build process.","groups":[{"label":"Uploading code to repositories","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Boards"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Pipelines"},{"id":"2","text":"GitHub Enterprise"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Building containers","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Boards"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Pipelines"},{"id":"2","text":"GitHub Enterprise"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":188,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q188-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q188-answer.webp","explanation":"Transparent Data Encryption (TDE) for Azure SQL databases uses AES-256 encryption for data at rest. By using customer-managed keys (CMKs) stored in Azure Key Vault, you retain full control over the key lifecycle, allowing you to rotate the keys on a monthly schedule as required.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":189,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q189-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q189-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure AD B2C is the correct choice because it is specifically designed for business-to-consumer applications that require a customer identity store. It natively supports social identity providers (Google, Facebook) and allows fully customizable branding of user experiences, unlike Azure AD B2B which focuses on guest access via existing enterprise directories.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure Active Directory (Azure AD) B2B"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure Active Directory Domain Services (Azure AD DS)"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Active Directory (Azure AD) B2C SESE"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure AD Connect"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":190,"type":"multiple","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q190-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q190-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Arc is required to extend Azure resource management and governance capabilities to on- premises servers, allowing them to be managed as if they were native Azure resources. Guest configuration (now part of Azure Automanage Machine Configuration) enables Azure Policy to audit the internal state and configuration of these connected machines across hybrid environments.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"on-premises data gateway"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure VPN Gateway"},{"id":"C","text":"guest configuration in Azure Policy"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure Arc 7 GICES AT"},{"id":"E","text":"Azure Bastion"}],"correct":["C","D"]}]},{"id":191,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q191-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q191-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Sentinel is a cloud-native SIEM and SOAR solution designed specifically for centralized security operations. It aggregates data from multiple sources (including Azure and Microsoft 365) to enable log analysis, incident management, audit activities, and threat hunting/searching across deployed services.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft 365 Defender"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":192,"type":"hotspot","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q192-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q192-answer.webp","explanation":"According to the Microsoft Cloud Adoption Framework for Azure, Security Testing in Cl involves Static Application Security Testing (SAST) during commit and Infrastructure Scanning during build/test. In CD, Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST) is performed during staging, while Penetration Testing occurs in production before general availability.","groups":[{"label":"Infrastructure scanning stage","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Build and test"},{"id":"1","text":"Commit the code"},{"id":"2","text":"Go to production"},{"id":"3","text":"Operate"},{"id":"4","text":"Plan and develop"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Static application security testing stage","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Build and test"},{"id":"1","text":"Commit the code"},{"id":"2","text":"Go to production"},{"id":"3","text":"Operate"},{"id":"4","text":"Plan and develop"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":193,"type":"multiple","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q193-question.webp","answerImage":null,"explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Manage application identities securely and automatically."},{"id":"B","text":"Manage the lifecycle of identities and entitlements."},{"id":"C","text":"Protect identity and authentication systems."},{"id":"D","text":"Enable threat detection for identity and access management."},{"id":"E","text":"Use acentralized identity and authentication system."}],"correct":["A","C","E"]}]},{"id":194,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q194-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q194-answer.webp","explanation":"The Microsoft Cloud Adoption Framework (CAF) explicitly recommends using the STRIDE model as the primary technique for threat modeling within its DevSecOps practices. STRIDE provides a structured, top-down categorization of threats (Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information Disclosure, Denial of Service, Elevation of Privilege) that aligns with Microsoft's security development lifecycle. While OWASP is a valuable general standard, the question specifically asks for the method based on the Microsoft CAF, making STRIDE the authoritative choice in this context. DREAD is primarily used for risk assessment rather than initial threat identification.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"the STRIDE model"},{"id":"B","text":"the DREAD model"},{"id":"C","text":"OWASP threat modeling"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":195,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q195-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q195-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure SQL Database is a fully managed Platform-as-a-Service (PaaS) solution that minimizes operational requirements because Microsoft handles all patching and maintenance for the database engine. It natively supports Dynamic Data Masking to protect sensitive data without requiring custom code or server-level configuration. In contrast, SQL Server on Azure Virtual Machines requires manual OS and software patching, increasing administrative overhead and cost.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"SQL Server on Azure Virtual Machines"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure Synapse Analytics dedicated SQL pools"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure SQL Database"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":196,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q196-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q196-answer.webp","explanation":"The Azure Landing Zone Accelerator provides a foundational, pre-configured infrastructure that implements security best practices from the Cloud Adoption Framework. It specifically automates the deployment of shared components like Network Security Groups, Azure Firewall, Key Vault, and Bastion to minimize manual effort and ensure consistency.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"the Azure landing zone accelerator"},{"id":"B","text":"the Azure Well-Architected Framework"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Security Benchmark v3"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure Advisor"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":197,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q197-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q197-answer.webp","explanation":"Branch policies in Azure Repos allow you to enforce that all code changes are submitted via pull requests by blocking direct pushes to protected branches. This ensures mandatory code review and approval processes occur before merging, which is a core DevSecOps control for quality assurance.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"custom roles in Azure Pipelines"},{"id":"B","text":"branch policies in Azure Repos"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure policies"},{"id":"D","text":"custom Azure roles"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":198,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q198-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q198-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Sentinel provides a dedicated Syslog connector that supports ingesting logs in Common Event Format (CEF) from on-premises sources. Since there is no built-in connector for the specific firewalls, deploying an on-premises Syslog server allows you to collect CEF logs and forward them directly to Sentinel's Linux Syslog data connector.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"an Azure logic app"},{"id":"B","text":"anon-premises Syslog server"},{"id":"C","text":"an on-premises data gateway"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure Data Factory"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":199,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q199-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q199-answer.webp","explanation":"Authorized IP ranges is the specific Azure feature designed to restrict access to the AKS control plane (API server) by allowing connections only from specified public IP addresses or CIDR blocks. This directly satisfies the requirement to limit access to the on-premises datacenter's public IPs without requiring a VNet integration, which NSGs would typically necessitate. While private endpoints remove the public endpoint entirely, authorized IP ranges filter traffic on the existing public endpoint based on source identity.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"a private endpoint"},{"id":"B","text":"a network security group (NSG)"},{"id":"C","text":"a service endpoint"},{"id":"D","text":"an authorized IP range"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":200,"type":"hotspot","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q200-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q200-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Sentinel is the correct service for automatically identifying threats in AWS CloudTrail events because it natively ingests and analyzes external cloud logs through its built-in data connectors. Azure Arc is required to enforce security settings on AWS virtual machines because it enables Azure Policy to manage and govern resources outside of Azure, whereas Microsoft Defender for Cloud cannot directly apply policies to non-Azure VMs.","groups":[{"label":"Automatically identify threats","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Arc"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Log Analytics"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"}],"correct":["3"]},{"label":"Enforce security settings","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Arc"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Log Analytics"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":201,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q201-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q201-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Servers (specifically Plan 2) integrates with Qualys to provide vulnerability assessments that identify missing updates and insecure configurations on both Windows and Linux virtual machines in Azure. While the direct ‘Deploy Vulnerability Assessment’ integration with Qualys was deprecated, Microsoft Defender for Cloud's server protection plans continue to support Qualys as a backend engine for these specific assessment requirements.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Defender for Servers"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Defender Threat Intelligence (Defender TI)"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Defender External Attack Surface Management (Defender EASM)"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":202,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q202-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q202-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Bastion provides secure RDP and SSH connectivity to virtual machines directly within the Azure portal without exposing public IP addresses. It supports authentication via Microsoft Entra ID for Windows VMs (and PAM/SSHD integration) and Tuns as a platform-as-a-service, ensuring no direct network access or file transfer capabilities outside standard SSH sessions.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure NAT Gateway"},{"id":"B","text":"just-in-time (JIT) VM access"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Bastion"},{"id":"D","text":"Point-to-Site (P2S) VPN"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":203,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q203-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q203-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Entra Domain Services is the correct choice because it is a managed service that supports legacy authentication protocols required by the applications, such as LDAP, Kerberos, and NTLM. Unlike Microsoft Entra ID (which is cloud-only and lacks native LDAP support) or on-premises AD DS (which requires managing domain controllers and increases administrative overhead), Entra Domain Services allows you to migrate to a cloud-only infrastructure while maintaining compatibility with legacy apps without deploying additional infrastructure.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Entra External ID"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Entra Domain Services"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Entra ID"},{"id":"D","text":"Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS)"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":204,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q204-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q204-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender Vulnerability Management (TVM) includes a specific feature called ‘Security Baselines’ that assesses device configuration compliance against standard benchmarks, including CIS benchmarks. Authenticated scans identify software vulnerabilities and missing patches, not configuration drift from baselines. Secure Score provides an aggregate security posture metric, while ASR rules are preventive controls for malware, neither of which directly audits CIS benchmark compliance.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Authenticated scan for Windows in Microsoft Defender Vulnerability Management"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Secure Score for Devices in Defender for Endpoint"},{"id":"C","text":"attack surface reduction (ASR) rules in Defender for Endpoint"},{"id":"D","text":"security baselines assessments in Microsoft Defender Vulnerability Management"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":205,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q205-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q205-answer.webp","explanation":"Flux is a GitOps tool that continuously reconciles the state of Kubernetes clusters with the desired state defined in a Git repository like Azure Repos. By using Flux, you can ensure that all 20 AKS clusters remain consistent by automatically detecting and applying changes from the shared manifest files without requiring direct cluster access.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Gatekeeper"},{"id":"B","text":"Dependency Tracker"},{"id":"C","text":"Dependabot"},{"id":"D","text":"Flux"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":206,"type":"hotspot","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q206-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q206-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Update Manager is the recommended solution as it unifies update management for both Azure and on-premises servers (Windows and Linux) via the Microsoft Monitoring Agent or Log Analytics agent. It supports hotpatching for Windows Server Datacenter: Azure Edition, enables on-demand update inventory and deployment, and handles Extended Security Updates (ESU) for supported OS versions.","groups":[{"label":"For update management, use","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Automation Update Management"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Update Manager"},{"id":"2","text":"System Center Updates Publisher"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"On the on-premises operating systems, install","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Monitor Agent"},{"id":"1","text":"The Azure Connected Machine agent"},{"id":"2","text":"The Log Analytics agent"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":207,"type":"hotspot","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q207-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q207-answer.webp","explanation":"Just-in-Time (JIT) VM access is the correct solution for the first two requirements because it allows administrators to request temporary RDP access via the Azure portal and enforces time limits, such as a maximum of three hours. For the third requirement, Qualys vulnerability scanner is the appropriate recommendation because it provides agentless scanning capabilities integrated with Microsoft Defender for Cloud, whereas MDVM typically relies on agent-based data collection from the Log Analytics workspace.","groups":[{"label":"RDP access","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Adaptive network hardening"},{"id":"1","text":"Just-in-time (JIT) VM access"},{"id":"2","text":"Login with Microsoft Entra"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Agentless vulnerability scanning","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Defender Vulnerability Management"},{"id":"1","text":"Defender for Cloud integrated Qualys vulnerability scanner"},{"id":"2","text":"Rapid7 scanner"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":208,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q208-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q208-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Entra Internet Access (part of Global Secure Access) includes a specific feature called Universal Tenant Restrictions. This feature prevents users from authenticating to other Microsoft 365 tenants, thereby minimizing data exfiltration risks and meeting the requirement for minimal administrative effort compared to complex network policies.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Entra Private Access"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Entra Internet Access"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":209,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q209-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q209-answer.webp","explanation":"Continuous Access Evaluation (CAE) in Microsoft Entra ID applies to supported workloads to re- evaluate user compliance during an active session. Both Exchange Online and SharePoint Online are explicitly listed as supported services for CAE by Microsoft. Since the Conditional Access policy is applied to all users and includes these services, both resources benefit from continuous protection checks.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"SharePoint Online only"},{"id":"B","text":"Exchange Online only"},{"id":"C","text":"both SharePoint Online and Exchange Online"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":210,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q210-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q210-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Virtual Network Manager (VNM) security admin rules provide a centralized way to enforce network security policies across multiple virtual networks. These rules take precedence over NSGs, allowing you to block direct RDP traffic at the infrastructure level regardless of existing permissive NSG configurations.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure Virtual Network Manager security admin rules"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure Virtual Network Manager connectivity configurations"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Firewall application rules"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure Firewall network rules"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":211,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q211-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q211-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Storage firewall rules allow you to explicitly whitelist specific IP addresses or ranges at the storage account level, ensuring only traffic from the branch offices' static IPs is permitted. This approach minimizes cost because it uses a native feature of Azure Blob Storage without requiring additional infrastructure like Azure Firewall or Virtual Networks.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure Private Link"},{"id":"B","text":"an Azure Firewall policy"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Storage firewall rules"},{"id":"D","text":"a network security group (NSG)"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":212,"type":"hotspot","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q212-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q212-answer.webp","explanation":"Use Microsoft Defender for Cloud Just-in-Time (JIT) VM access to manage NSG rules, ensuring ports are closed by default and only opened temporarily upon explicit user request, which satisfies the brute-force protection and on-demand access requirements. For the SSH source, select 'AzureBastionSubnet' because Azure Bastion provides secure RDP/SSH connectivity over HTTPS from the internet without exposing public IP ports; thus, the NSG must allow traffic only from the Bastion subnet to meet the ‘internet-only' and security constraints while minimizing administrative effort compared to managing dynamic public IPs.","groups":[{"label":"On-demand SSH access","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Bastion"},{"id":"1","text":"Just-in-time (JIT) VM access"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Allowed SSH source","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Any public IP supplied before connection"},{"id":"1","text":"AzureBastionSubnet"},{"id":"2","text":"GatewaySubnet"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":213,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q213-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q213-answer.webp","explanation":"To enforce web filtering via Global Secure Access (GSA), the GSA client must be installed and Tuning on each endpoint to intercept and route traffic through the secure network. Since the devices are already enrolled in Intune, the most efficient and scalable first step is to deploy this client using an Intune configuration policy rather than manual installation or network-level tunneling.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Configure an Intune policy to onboard Microsoft Defender for Endpoint to each device."},{"id":"B","text":"Configure an IPsec tunnel on the router."},{"id":"C","text":"Install the Microsoft Entra private network connector on the file server."},{"id":"D","text":"Configure an Intune policy to deploy the Global Secure Access client to each device."}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":214,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q214-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q214-answer.webp","explanation":"The SQL Server runs on laaS virtual machines in West US, while the App Service is in East US. To allow the App Service to access the private IP addresses of these VMs across regions, you must establish VNet-to-VNet connectivity using an Azure VPN Gateway (or Azure ExpressRoute). Private Endpoints and Service Endpoints are designed for connecting to Azure PaaS services, not directly to laaS VMs running custom workloads.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"an Azure VPN gateway"},{"id":"B","text":"a private endpoint"},{"id":"C","text":"a service endpoint"},{"id":"D","text":"an Azure Bastion host"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":215,"type":"hotspot","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q215-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q215-answer.webp","explanation":"To meet the requirement of maximizing redundancy for Microsoft Entra Application Proxy, you must deploy two connectors to the default connector group. For ExpressRoute, Private Peering is required because it provides access to Azure laaS resources and virtual networks (like VNet1), whereas Microsoft Peering only provides access to public cloud services like Azure PaaS offerings and Microsoft 365.","groups":[{"label":"To optimize the connection between the users bd and the application proxy, deploy","options":[{"id":"0","text":"connector to the default connector group and a connector to a new connector group"},{"id":"1","text":"Two connectors to the default connector group"},{"id":"2","text":"Two new connector groups that each contain a new connector"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"To optimize the connection between Ba the connector and App, use","options":[{"id":"0","text":"ExpressRoute with Microsoft peering"},{"id":"1","text":"ExpressRoute with Microsoft peering and the premium add-on"},{"id":"2","text":"ExpressRoute with private peering"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":216,"type":"hotspot","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q216-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q216-answer.webp","explanation":"To secure connectivity for Appi (the consumer), use a Private Endpoint to ensure inbound traffic from VMs traverses the Azure backbone rather than the public internet. To secure connectivity for VM2 (the target storing data), use a Private Link Service so that App! can reach it privately without exposing it publicly, thereby minimizing exfiltration risk.","groups":[{"label":"Inbound connectivity","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Private endpoints"},{"id":"1","text":"Service endpoints"},{"id":"2","text":"Static IP restrictions"},{"id":"3","text":"Virtual network integration"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Outbound connectivity","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Private endpoint"},{"id":"1","text":"Service endpoint"},{"id":"2","text":"Static IP restrictions"},{"id":"3","text":"Virtual network integration"}],"correct":["0"]}],"conflict":"The written explanation names an answer that is not among the screenshot choices. This question is shown for review but is excluded from scoring."},{"id":217,"type":"hotspot","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q217-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q217-answer.webp","explanation":"To make DB1 inaccessible from the internet while minimizing costs, you must disable public endpoint access and instead use a Private Endpoint to connect via Azure Private Link within VNet2. To restrict connectivity strictly to VMs in Subnett (and prevent other subnets or resources in VNet2 from accessing it), you must configure Network Security Groups (NSGs) on the subnet hosting DB1 or on the private endpoint interface.","groups":[{"label":"Make DB1 private","options":[{"id":"0","text":"A private endpoint"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Application Gateway"},{"id":"2","text":"Azure Private Link"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Restrict DB1 to Subnet1","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Firewall rules"},{"id":"1","text":"Network security groups (NSGs)"},{"id":"2","text":"Virtual network rules"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":218,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q218-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q218-answer.webp","explanation":"The Microsoft Entra Application Proxy connector operates using an outbound-only communication model, initiating connections to the cloud service over TCP 443. Because it does not listen for or require inbound traffic from Azure, allowing inbound TCP 443 (Rule1) creates unnecessary security exposure. Removing Rule1 maximizes security by adhering to the principle of least privilege while having no negative impact on the connector's functionality.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Rule1"},{"id":"B","text":"Rule2"},{"id":"C","text":"Rule3"},{"id":"D","text":"Rule4"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":219,"type":"multiple","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q219-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q219-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Entra Internet Access policies are enforced via Conditional Access (CA). CAPolicy1 grants access only if the device is compliant, blocking traffic from non-compliant devices for all resources covered by the CA policy, including both adatum.com and fabrikam.com. Therefore, https://www.fabrikam.com is allowed only from compliant devices (D), as it falls under the CA restriction but lacks a specific block rule. Traffic to https://www.adatum.com:8433 is allowed only from compliant devices (E) because the FQDN tule allows the domain, but the CA policy restricts access to compliant devices; non-compliant devices are denied regardless of the allow rule.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Traffic to https://www.fabrikam.com will be allowed from all the devices."},{"id":"B","text":"Traffic to https://www.adatum.com:8433 will be blocked from all the devices."},{"id":"C","text":"Traffic to https://www.adatum.com:8433 will be allowed from all the devices."},{"id":"D","text":"Traffic to https://www.fabrikam.com will be allowed from compliant devices only."},{"id":"E","text":"Traffic to https://www.adatum.com:8433 will be allowed from compliant devices only."},{"id":"F","text":"Traffic to https://www-fabrikam.com will be blocked from noncompliant devices only."}],"correct":["D","E"]}]},{"id":220,"type":"hotspot","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q220-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q220-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps provides app discovery capabilities to identify file sharing sites accessed by users over the last 90 days through its audit and usage logs. To prevent access while minimizing administrative effort, Microsoft Defender for Endpoint uses Web Content Filtering with a block policy applied to the identified categories or URLs.","groups":[{"label":"Identify the sites","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Defender Vulnerability Management"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Prevent connections","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Defender Vulnerability Management"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":221,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q221-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q221-answer.webp","explanation":"Compliance Manager in Microsoft Purview is the specific service designed to assess regulatory compliance across multicloud environments (Azure, AWS, GCP) by aggregating data from various sources. It fulfills the requirement for detailed implementation guidance and automatic monitoring of regulatory standards while minimizing administrative effort through its centralized dashboard and automated scoring.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"B","text":"Compliance Manager connectors"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":222,"type":"hotspot","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q222-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q222-answer.webp","explanation":"To minimize identities and administrative effort for Azure VMs, use System-assigned Managed Identities, as they are automatically created with the VM. For Arc-enabled on-premises servers, User-assigned Managed Identities are required because system-assigned identities are not supported for Arc resources. Data Collection Rules (DCRs) must be used to filter specific CEF logs at the source, ensuring only required events are sent to Sentinel and minimizing ingestion volume.","groups":[{"label":"Minimize ingested events","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Data collection rules (DCRs)"},{"id":"1","text":"Scheduled query rule filters"},{"id":"2","text":"Daily workspace cap"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Managed identities","options":[{"id":"0","text":"System-assigned for all servers"},{"id":"1","text":"User-assigned for all servers"},{"id":"2","text":"User-assigned on premises and system-assigned for Azure VMs"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":223,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q223-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q223-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Bastion is designed exclusively for accessing Azure Virtual Machines and cannot be used for on-premises servers like Server1. Microsoft Entra Private Access enables secure, browser-based remote access to internal resources, such as RDP services, from anywhere while integrating with Conditional Access to enforce Multifactor Authentication (MFA).","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Windows Admin Center"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Entra Internet Access"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Bastion"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Entra Private Access"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":224,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q224-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q224-answer.webp","explanation":"Attack path analysis in Microsoft Defender for Cloud relies on agentless scanning to continuously discover and evaluate resources across multicloud environments without requiring manual configuration. According to official documentation, enabling Defender CSPM is not sufficient; you must specifically enable agentless scanning to activate the proprietary algorithm that locates potential attack paths.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"regulatory compliance"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Defender External Attack Surface Management (Defender EASM)"},{"id":"C","text":"agentless scanning"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Cloud Security Benchmark (MCSB)"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":225,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q225-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q225-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint (MDE) is the unified agent that provides consistent workload protection across Azure, AWS, and other cloud platforms. Deploying the MDE agent to the AWS VMs meets the requirement for uniform security coverage while minimizing the number of agents by leveraging the existing Defender for Servers Plan 1 integration rather than installing separate monitoring or connectivity tools.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"the log Analytics agent"},{"id":"B","text":"the Azure Connected Machine agent"},{"id":"C","text":"the Microsoft Defender for Endpoint agent"},{"id":"D","text":"the Azure Monitor Agent"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":226,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q226-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q226-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender Vulnerability Management is the correct solution because it provides a unified view of vulnerability risks across hybrid environments, including non-onboarded VMs. It specifically identifies missing critical updates and risky applications while offering automated remediation recommendations to minimize administrative effort.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Defender External Attack Surface Management (Defender EASM)"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Defender Vulnerability Management"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Defender Threat Intelligence (Defender TI)"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Intune Advanced Analytics"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":227,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q227-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q227-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender Vulnerability Management (MDVM) integrates with Microsoft Intune to surface vulnerability assessments and recommended configuration changes as actionable items. These recommendations are specifically reviewed and managed within the Endpoint security > Security tasks section of the Microsoft Intune admin center. Security tasks allow administrators to accept, reject, or mark remediation actions as complete based ‘on guidance from Defender for Endpoint.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Security tasks"},{"id":"B","text":"Attack surface reduction"},{"id":"C","text":"End point detection and response"},{"id":"D","text":"Device compliance"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":228,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q228-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q228-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure automatic VM guest patching requires the use of platform images; custom images are not supported. Therefore, VMSS2 and VMSS4, which utilize custom images, are ineligible regardless of their orchestration mode. While Flexible orchestration mode was previously unsupported, it is now supported for automatic guest patching, making VMSS1 and VMSS3 (both using platform images) the only valid candidates.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"VMSS1 only"},{"id":"B","text":"VMSS2 only"},{"id":"C","text":"VMSS1 and VMSS3 only"},{"id":"D","text":"VMSS2 and VMSS4 only"},{"id":"E","text":"VMSS1, VMSS2, VMSS3, and VMSS4"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":229,"type":"hotspot","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q229-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q229-answer.webp","explanation":"You need three segments: Investment employees, Research employees, and a combined Compliance segment. ‘Two policies are required because Information Barrier policies are unidirectional; you must create one policy to block Investment from Research and another to block Research from Investment. The Compliance users in their own segment can communicate with each other because no barrier exists between them, satisfying the cross-department compliance requirement.","groups":[{"label":"Segments","options":[{"id":"0","text":"2"},{"id":"1","text":"3"},{"id":"2","text":"4"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Policies","options":[{"id":"0","text":"2"},{"id":"1","text":"3"},{"id":"2","text":"4"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":230,"type":"hotspot","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q230-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q230-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Intune security baselines are configuration profiles designed specifically for Windows 10 and Windows 11 devices to enforce recommended security settings, so they are supported only on the Windows platform in this scenario. The Remote lock action is a remote command available for Android, i0S/iPadOS, and macOS devices to secure lost or stolen hardware, but it is not supported for Windows devices, making it applicable only to the macOS platform here.","groups":[{"label":"Security baselines","options":[{"id":"0","text":"macOS only"},{"id":"1","text":"Windows only"},{"id":"2","text":"Windows and macOS"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Remote lock","options":[{"id":"0","text":"macOS only"},{"id":"1","text":"Windows only"},{"id":"2","text":"Windows and macOS"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":231,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q231-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q231-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Virtual Network Manager supports cross-tenant management, allowing a single instance to manage resources in both Sub1 and Sub2. Two NSGs are required because Sub1_VNet20 and Sub2_VNet20 require different SSH rules (Allow) compared to the other VNets (Block). Consequently, two distinct rule collections within the manager are needed to define these separate security policies efficiently.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"1 Virtual Network Manager instance, 1 rule collection, and 2 NSGs"},{"id":"B","text":"2 Virtual Network Manager instances that each contain 1 NSG and 1 rule collection"},{"id":"C","text":"2 Virtual Network Manager instances that each contain 2 NSGs and 2 rule collections"},{"id":"D","text":"1 Virtual Network Manager instance, 2 rule collections, and 2 NSGs"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":232,"type":"multiple","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q232-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q232-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Arc is required to bring the third-party cloud virtual machines under Azure management, enabling unified governance across hybrid and multi-cloud environments. Microsoft Defender for Cloud provides continuous security posture management (CSPM) and threat protection, allowing you to validate the security status of both Azure and on-premises or third-party VMs connected via Arc.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure Arc"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure Lighthouse"},{"id":"E","text":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint"}],"correct":["B","C"]}]},{"id":233,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q233-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q233-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud custom security standards are supported only for Azure, AWS, and GCP resources. Oracle Cloud Infrastructure (OC!) is not a supported environment for applying these standards.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure and AWS only"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure and GCP only"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure, AWS, and GCP only"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure, AWS, and OCI only"},{"id":"E","text":"Azure, AWS, GCP, and OCI"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":234,"type":"hotspot","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q234-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q234-answer.webp","explanation":"The Microsoft cloud security benchmark is the recommended policy for optimizing secure score as it aligns with industry best practices and CIS benchmarks. The secure score calculation is directly impacted by enabling built-in security policy recommendations, applying risk prioritization rankings to focus on high-risk findings, and configuring critical asset classifications to weight scores based ‘on asset importance.","groups":[{"label":"Security policy","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure CSPM"},{"id":"1","text":"CIS Microsoft Azure Foundation Benchmark"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft cloud security benchmark"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Secure score factors","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Built-in recommendations only"},{"id":"1","text":"Built-in recommendations and risk prioritization"},{"id":"2","text":"Built-in recommendations, risk prioritization, and critical asset classifications"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":235,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q235-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q235-answer.webp","explanation":"The Zero Trust Rapid Modernization Plan (RaMP) emphasizes applying consistent security controls regardless of network location, advocating for the removal of ‘trusted’ network exceptions. By excluding Site1 from Conditional Access enforcement, the current policy creates a security gap where users are not required to authenticate via MFA or device compliance while on that specific network. Changing the 'Exclude: Selected networks and locations; Site1' setting ensures that all access requests, including those from internal networks, undergo strict validation, aligning with the Zero Trust principle of ‘never trust, always verify’.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Include: Any network of location"},{"id":"B","text":"Exclude: Selected networks and locations; Site1"},{"id":"C","text":"Grant Require Hybrid Microsoft Entra joined device"},{"id":"D","text":"Grant: Require multifactor authentication"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":236,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q236-question.webp","answerImage":null,"explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Secure Score"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Assessment and Planning (MAP) Toolkit"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Defender for Identity"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":237,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q237-question.webp","answerImage":null,"explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Install private network connectors."},{"id":"B","text":"Deploy Network Policy Server (NPS) as a RADIUS proxy."},{"id":"C","text":"Deploy Network Policy Server (NPS) as a RADIUS server."},{"id":"D","text":"Onboard the Global Secure Access client to the devices."}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":238,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q238-question.webp","answerImage":null,"explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure Bastion"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Intune"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":239,"type":"hotspot","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q239-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q239-answer.webp","explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"nclude","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Entra Internet Access"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Entra Private Access"},{"id":"2","text":"A Site-to-Site (S2S) VPN"},{"id":"3","text":"Virtual network peering"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Create","options":[{"id":"0","text":"An access package"},{"id":"1","text":"A Global Secure Access application"},{"id":"2","text":"A Global Secure Access security profile"},{"id":"3","text":"A Microsoft Entra application proxy connector"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":240,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q240-question.webp","answerImage":null,"explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure Firewall"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure DDoS Protection"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Front Door"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure Application Gateway"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":241,"type":"single","topic":3,"questionImage":"images/q241-question.webp","answerImage":null,"explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Network Watcher"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":242,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q242-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q242-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint application control policies enable administrators to define allow/deny lists for executables on Windows endpoints. This ensures that only authorized applications can run, while unauthorized attempts are automatically blocked until manually approved. Other options like Azure AD app registrations or Cloud Apps policies manage identity and SaaS access rather than local binary execution on VMs.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"app registrations in Azure Active Directory (Azure AD)"},{"id":"B","text":"OAuth app policies in Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Security Benchmark compliance controls in Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"D","text":"application control policies in Microsoft Defender for Endpoint"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":243,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q243-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q243-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Storage account firewall rules allow you to explicitly permit traffic only from specific IP addresses or virtual networks, directly enforcing access control at the storage resource level. Network Security Groups (NSGs) and Azure Firewall operate at the network perimeter but cannot independently restrict direct internet access to public storage endpoints without a private endpoint or service endpoint configuration. WAF rules protect web applications from common exploits but do not provide IP-based access restrictions for general blob storage.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"managed rule sets in Azure Web Application Firewall (WAF) policies"},{"id":"B","text":"inbound rules in network security groups (NSGs)"},{"id":"C","text":"firewall rules for the storage account"},{"id":"D","text":"inbound rules in Azure Firewall"},{"id":"E","text":"service tags in network security groups (NSGs)"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":244,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q244-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q244-answer.webp","explanation":"The Microsoft Threat Modeling Tool is specifically designed to work with Data Flow Diagrams (DFDs) to identify security threats. DEDs visualize how data moves through a system, allowing analysts to pinpoint vulnerabilities related to data storage, processing, and external interfaces. Microsoft's official documentation and the tool's native capabilities confirm that DFD is the correct diagram type for this purpose.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"system flow"},{"id":"B","text":"data flow"},{"id":"C","text":"process flow"},{"id":"D","text":"network flow"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":245,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q245-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q245-answer.webp","explanation":"Hybrid Connections leverage Azure Relay to establish secure outbound-only connections from on- premises resources to Azure App Service without requiring inbound port openings or a Site-to-Site VPN/ExpressRoute. Since the scenario explicitly states no VPN or ExpressRoute exists and aims to minimize internet-accessible endpoints, Hybrid Connections are the only viable solution that operates entirely over public internet traffic initiated by the on-premises agent. Private Endpoints and VNet Integration require private network connectivity (VPN/ExpressRoute) which is absent here.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"virtual network NAT gateway integration"},{"id":"B","text":"hybrid connections"},{"id":"C","text":"virtual network integration"},{"id":"D","text":"a private endpoint"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":246,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q246-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q246-answer.webp","explanation":"In the Microsoft Security Development Lifecycle (SDL), threat modeling is a core activity explicitly assigned to the design phase to identify potential security risks before code is written. While SAST and DAST are part of SDL, they occur during the implementation and testing phases, not the initial design stage. Therefore, using the Microsoft Threat Modeling Tool is the correct recommendation for the application design phase.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"software decomposition by using Microsoft Visual Studio Enterprise"},{"id":"B","text":"dynamic application security testing (DAST) by using Veracode"},{"id":"C","text":"threat modeling by using the Microsoft Threat Modeling Tool"},{"id":"D","text":"static application security testing (SAST) by using SonarQube"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":247,"type":"drag","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q247-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q247-answer.webp","explanation":"For SharePoint Online and Microsoft Teams, the correct scope is 'Groups and sites' because these platforms store content within container-based structures that support site-level classification. For Exchange Online, the correct scope is ‘Files and emails (or ‘Items') as this scope specifically targets individual messages and attachments stored in mailboxes.","groups":[{"label":"SharePoint Online","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Files and emails"},{"id":"1","text":"Groups and sites"},{"id":"2","text":"Schematized data assets"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Microsoft Teams","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Files and emails"},{"id":"1","text":"Groups and sites"},{"id":"2","text":"Schematized data assets"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Exchange Online","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Files and emails"},{"id":"1","text":"Groups and sites"},{"id":"2","text":"Schematized data assets"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":248,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q248-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q248-answer.webp","explanation":"Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST) analyzes the application while it is running in an operational state, sending crafted requests to detect runtime vulnerabilities like SQL injection and cross-site scripting (XSS). This contrasts with Static Application Security Testing (SAST), which scans source code without executing it and cannot assess live server configurations or runtime behavior.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"dynamic application security testing (DAST)"},{"id":"B","text":"static application security testing (SAST)"},{"id":"C","text":"interactive application security testing (IAST)"},{"id":"D","text":"runtime application self-protection (RASP)"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":249,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q249-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q249-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure AD Conditional Access policies are designed to enforce access control based on signals such as user location, allowing you to block or require specific authentication methods for sign-ins originating from defined countries. By configuring Named Locations within a Conditional Access policy, you can explicitly restrict access to enterprise applications for users connecting from prohibited geographic regions.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"activity policies in Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"B","text":"sign-in risk policies in Azure AD Identity Protection"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure AD Conditional Access policies"},{"id":"D","text":"device compliance policies in Microsoft Endpoint Manager"},{"id":"E","text":"user risk poticies in Azure AD Identity Protection"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":250,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q250-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q250-answer.webp","explanation":"Shared Access Signatures (SAS) are specifically designed to grant delegated access to Azure Storage resources without exposing account keys. They provide granular control over permissions, specific resources, and time-limited validity periods, satisfying the requirement for secure, temporary vendor access.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Configure private link connections."},{"id":"B","text":"Configure encryption by using customer-managed keys (CMKs)."},{"id":"C","text":"Share the connection string of the access key."},{"id":"D","text":"Create shared access signatures (SAS)."}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":251,"type":"multiple","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q251-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q251-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure AD B2C supports integrating Conditional Access with user flows and custom policies to enforce risk-based access decisions, securing the application against identity-related threats. Additionally, Azure AD B2C includes a smart account lockout feature that mitigates brute-force attacks by progressively delaying authentication requests after failed attempts. Access packages (Option D) are part of Identity Governance for enterprise tenants (B2B) and are not available in Azure AD B2C.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure AD workbooks to monitor risk detections"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure AD Conditional Access integration with user flows and custom policies"},{"id":"C","text":"smart account lockout in Azure AD B2C"},{"id":"D","text":"access packages in Identity Governance"},{"id":"E","text":"custom resource owner password credentials (ROPC) flows in Azure AD B2C"}],"correct":["B","C"]}]},{"id":252,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q252-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q252-answer.webp","explanation":"Data Loss Prevention (DLP) policies are specifically designed to detect and block the transfer of sensitive information, such as protected health information (PHI), outside the organization. While sensitivity labels classify data, DLP enforces the restriction against external sharing across Exchange, SharePoint, OneDrive, and Teams.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"sensitivity label policies"},{"id":"B","text":"data loss prevention (DLP) policies"},{"id":"C","text":"insider risk management policies"},{"id":"D","text":"retention policies"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":253,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q253-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q253-answer.webp","explanation":"The Data Classification Content Explorer is the specific tool within Microsoft Purview designed to help administrators locate and view documents that contain sensitive information types or have been assigned sensitivity labels across SharePoint, OneDrive, and Exchange. Unlike DLP, which primarily enforces policies and restricts actions on identified data, Content Explorer provides visibility and indexing of sensitive content specifically for the purpose of discovery and classification assessment.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"data classification content explorer"},{"id":"B","text":"data loss prevention (DLP)"},{"id":"C","text":"eDiscovery"},{"id":"D","text":"Information Governance"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":254,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q254-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q254-answer.webp","explanation":"Configuring gateway-required virtual network integration does not inherently restrict access to only Azure Front Door; it primarily mandates that outbound traffic traverses a specific network gateway. To ensure web apps are accessible ‘only* through the Front Door instance, you must configure IP restrictions using the specific service tags associated with Azure Front Door (such as “AzureFrontDoor.Frontend’). Since the proposed solution relies on VNet integration rather than these targeted IP restrictions, it does not meet the stated goal.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":255,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q255-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q255-answer.webp","explanation":"The goal is to ensure web apps allow access *only* through the specific Front Door instance, not just any Azure Front Door traffic. Restricting by service tags alone (e.g., AzureFrontDoor. Backend) allows traffic from *any* customer's Front Door instance using those IP ranges. To restrict to a *specific* instance, you must also validate the X-Azure-FDID HTTP header sent by that unique Front Door instance. Therefore, service tags alone do not meet the goal.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":256,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q256-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q256-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Front Door inserts the X-Azure-FDID HTTP header into every request it forwards to backends. By configuring Azure App Service access restrictions to allow traffic only when this specific header matches your Front Door ID, you ensure that requests originate from your instance and are not spoofed by external actors or other Front Door tenants. This method is more reliable than IP-based filtering because Front Door egress IPs are dynamic and shared across customers.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":257,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q257-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q257-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Information Protection (MIP) provides the specific capabilities to classify and protect data using sensitivity labels, ensuring encryption persists regardless of storage location or device. eDiscovery is for searching and preserving content, Compliance Manager assists with risk assessments, and retention policies manage data lifecycle, none of which directly perform classification and encryption.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"eDiscovery"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Information Protection"},{"id":"C","text":"Compliance Manager"},{"id":"D","text":"retention policies"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":258,"type":"multiple","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q258-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q258-answer.webp","explanation":"Data Loss Prevention (DLP) policies are specifically designed to detect and block the sharing of sensitive content like Pll across Microsoft 365 services, including SharePoint Online. Sensitivity labels enable classification and protection of data, allowing administrators to restrict access or apply encryption to prevent unauthorized sharing. Retention policies focus on lifecycle management, and eDiscovery is for legal compliance, neither of which directly prevents the initial sharing of Pll.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"data loss prevention (DLP) policies"},{"id":"B","text":"retention label policies"},{"id":"C","text":"eDiscovery cases"},{"id":"D","text":"sensitivity label policies"}],"correct":["A","D"]}]},{"id":259,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q259-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q259-answer.webp","explanation":"To mitigate ransomware attacks, Microsoft recommends implementing additional authentication mechanisms for backup management operations. Requiring PINs (or Multi-Factor Authentication) to disable or delete backups prevents attackers from compromising the backup infrastructure even if they gain access to administrative credentials. This specific control is highlighted in Azure's guidance for protecting against ransomware by hardening the backup process.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Use geo-redundant storage (GRS)."},{"id":"B","text":"Maintain multiple copies of the virtual machines."},{"id":"C","text":"Encrypt the backups by using customer-managed keys (CMKS)."},{"id":"D","text":"Require PINs to disable backups."}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":260,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q260-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q260-answer.webp","explanation":"Endpoint DLP policies are specifically designed to monitor and control file activities on local devices, such as uploading files from a computer to external websites or cloud services. This allows you to explicitly block uploads to third-party sites while permitting access to approved corporate resources like OneDrive for Business. Applying the policy to SharePoint sites (Option C) would only control content within SharePoint, not prevent users from copying files out of SharePoint and uploading them elsewhere.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Devices"},{"id":"B","text":"OneDrive accounts"},{"id":"C","text":"SharePoint sites"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":261,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q261-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q261-answer.webp","explanation":"App! requires identity federation with social providers like Linkedin, which is a core capability of Azure Active Directory B2C for customer-facing applications. App2 needs protection against common web exploits such as Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), which is the primary function of an Azure Web Application Firewall (WAF).","groups":[{"label":"App1 social sign-in","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure AD B2B authentication"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure AD B2C custom policies with Conditional Access"},{"id":"2","text":"Application Gateway WAF policies"},{"id":"3","text":"Azure Firewall"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"App2 protection against web exploits","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure AD B2B authentication"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure AD B2C custom policies with Conditional Access"},{"id":"2","text":"Application Gateway WAF policies"},{"id":"3","text":"Azure Firewall"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":262,"type":"drag","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q262-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q262-answer.webp","explanation":"The correct sequence follows the Microsoft DART ransomware investigation methodology: first assess the current situation and scope, then identify affected Line-of-Business (LOB) applications, and finally determine the compromise recovery process. This order prioritizes understanding the incident's extent before addressing business impact and remediation paths.","groups":[{"label":"First action","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Assess current situation and scope"},{"id":"1","text":"Identify unavailable line-of-business apps"},{"id":"2","text":"Identify compromise recovery process"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Second action","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Assess current situation and scope"},{"id":"1","text":"Identify unavailable line-of-business apps"},{"id":"2","text":"Identify compromise recovery process"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Third action","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Assess current situation and scope"},{"id":"1","text":"Identify unavailable line-of-business apps"},{"id":"2","text":"Identify compromise recovery process"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":263,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q263-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q263-answer.webp","explanation":"According to Microsoft's ransomware response playbook, disabling OneDrive sync and Exchange ActiveSync is a critical containment step that must be performed before any recovery actions begin. Failure to disable synchronization first allows encrypted or malicious files to propagate back into the cloud tenant and other synchronized devices during the recovery process, rendering subsequent steps like scanning or file restoration ineffective. Option A (scanning) and Option B (recovering files) are part of the eradication and recovery phases but occur only after the spread has been halted.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"From Microsoft Defender for Endpoint, perform a security scan."},{"id":"B","text":"Recover files to a cleaned computer or device."},{"id":"C","text":"Contact law enforcement."},{"id":"D","text":"Disable Microsoft OneDrive sync and Exchange ActiveSync."}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":264,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q264-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q264-answer.webp","explanation":"To define access based on assigned tags for confidential data, you must enable Attribute-based access control (ABAC), which allows Azure Role-Based Access Control assignments to use custom security attributes and tag conditions. The resource type should be Blob storage, as ABAC conditions are specifically applied to restrict read or write access to blobs based on their index tags.","groups":[{"label":"Authorization mechanism","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Access control list (ACL)"},{"id":"1","text":"Attribute-based access control (ABAC)"},{"id":"2","text":"Shared access signature (SAS)"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Resource type","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Blob"},{"id":"1","text":"File"},{"id":"2","text":"Table"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":265,"type":"multiple","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q265-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q265-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure AD B2C supports integration with Conditional Access to enforce security policies based on user risk or device state, enhancing protection against identity attacks. Smart account lockout is a built-in feature in Azure AD B2C that automatically detects and blocks brute-force login attempts without requiring additional configuration.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure AD Conditional Access integration with user flows and custom policies"},{"id":"B","text":"smart account lockout in Azure AD B2C"},{"id":"C","text":"access packages in Identity Governance"},{"id":"D","text":"custom resource owner password credentials (ROPC) flows in Azure AD B2C"}],"correct":["A","B"]}]},{"id":266,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q266-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q266-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud is the correct service because it provides continuous security assessment and compliance management specifically for Azure infrastructure resources (laa$/PaaS). It includes built-in regulatory compliance dashboards that map resource configurations directly to the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) controls. In contrast, Microsoft Purview Compliance Manager focuses on organizational governance and risk assessments rather than automated technical compliance of specific Azure resources, while Sentinel is a SIEM/SOAR solution for log analysis and threat detection.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Compliance Manager in Microsoft Purview"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":267,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q267-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q267-answer.webp","explanation":"To enable Global Secure Access (Entra Private Access), you must first create an Enterprise Application in Microsoft Entra ID to register the internal app and define access policies. Since Server is isolated from the internet, you cannot use ARC agents; instead, you install the Entra Private Network Connector on Server2 (which has connectivity) to relay traffic securely.","groups":[{"label":"Tenant","options":[{"id":"0","text":"An access package"},{"id":"1","text":"An app registration"},{"id":"2","text":"An enterprise application"},{"id":"3","text":"A managed identity"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Server2","options":[{"id":"0","text":"The Azure Connected Machine agent"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Entra Connect"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Entra private network connector"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":268,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q268-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q268-answer.webp","explanation":"To minimize administrative effort for five standalone apps, deploy one Quick Access app that groups them under a single configuration. To ensure high availability, deploy at least two private network connectors to provide redundancy if one fails.","groups":[{"label":"Global Secure Access apps","options":[{"id":"0","text":"One enterprise application"},{"id":"1","text":"One Quick Access app"},{"id":"2","text":"Five enterprise applications"},{"id":"3","text":"Five Quick Access apps"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Private network connectors","options":[{"id":"0","text":"1"},{"id":"1","text":"2"},{"id":"2","text":"5"},{"id":"3","text":"10"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":269,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q269-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q269-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Entra Private Access is designed for secure access to internal applications that are not exposed on the public internet, such as the on-premises Appl. Microsoft Entra Internet Access provides filtered web access for users to reach public destinations and SaaS services like App2 without exposing the entire corporate network.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Entra Private Access for App2 and Microsoft Entra Internet Access for App1"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Entra Private Access for App1 and Microsoft Entra Internet Access for App2"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Entra Internet Access for App1 and App2"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Entra Private Access for App1 and App2"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":270,"type":"multiple","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q270-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q270-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure AD Conditional Access allows you to enforce policies that block access or restrict capabilities based on device state, such as identifying unmanaged devices. Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps provides session controls that specifically enable blocking file downloads while potentially allowing view-only access, ensuring data protection without fully denying connectivity.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure AD Conditional Access"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure Data Catalog"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Purview Information Protection"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure AD Application Proxy"},{"id":"E","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"}],"correct":["A","E"]}]},{"id":271,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q271-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q271-answer.webp","explanation":"Always Encrypted ensures that encryption keys are managed by the client application and never exposed to the database engine, preventing even highly privileged database administrators from viewing plaintext sensitive data like credit card numbers. Dynamic Data Masking limits the exposure of sensitive information to non-privileged users (such as customer service operators) by obscuring specific parts of the data, such as showing only the last four digits of a credit card number.","groups":[{"label":"For the database administrators","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Always Encrypted"},{"id":"1","text":"Dynamic data masking"},{"id":"2","text":"Row-level security (RLS)"},{"id":"3","text":"Transparent Data Encryption (TDE)"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"For the operators","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Always Encrypted"},{"id":"1","text":"Dynamic data masking"},{"id":"2","text":"Row-level security (RLS)"},{"id":"3","text":"Transparent Data Encryption (TDE)"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":272,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q272-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q272-answer.webp","explanation":"An Azure AD enterprise application is the correct mechanism for integrating third-party SaaS apps like App1 into your tenant to support single sign-on (SSO) with Azure AD credentials. This configuration allows Azure AD to act as the identity provider, enabling users to authenticate seamlessly without exposing their credentials directly to the third party. Other options like Application Proxy are for internal web apps, and B2C is designed for consumer- facing scenarios rather than standard enterprise SaaS integration.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure AD Application Proxy"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure AD B2C"},{"id":"C","text":"an Azure AD enterprise application"},{"id":"D","text":"a relying party trust in Active Directory Federation Services (AD FS)"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":273,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q273-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q273-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Entra Identity Governance provides the necessary components to meet all requirements: access packages for self-service requests with custom fields, and access reviews for periodic manager verification of SaaS applications. Options B and C focus on cloud app security monitoring and policy enforcement rather than identity lifecycle management and governance workflows. Option D is used for publishing internal web apps securely, not for managing user access requests or reviews.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Entra Identity Governance"},{"id":"B","text":"connected apps in Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"C","text":"access policies in Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure AD Application Proxy"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":274,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q274-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q274-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for App Service includes a specific capability called ‘dangling DNS detection\" that identifies CNAME records pointing to deleted or non-existent App Services. This directly addresses the requirement to secure DNS records by alerting administrators when orphaned records remain after app deletion, helping minimize administrative effort by automating the discovery of these security risks without moving the DNS zone.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Defender for DevOps"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Defender for App Service"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Defender for DNS"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":275,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q275-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q275-answer.webp","explanation":"To keep traffic off the public internet and minimize costs while allowing VNet security admins to control access via network rules (like NSGs), configure Private Endpoints for the App Services. To further restrict access to specific servers on Site1, implement IP address restrictions on the App Service plan using the private IP addresses of those servers.","groups":[{"label":"Provide access to the apps for the servers on Site1 by using","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Private Link"},{"id":"1","text":"Private endpoints"},{"id":"2","text":"Service endpoints"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Enable the security administrators to control access to the apps by using","options":[{"id":"0","text":"App Service static IP address restrictions"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Firewall"},{"id":"2","text":"Azure Web Application Firewall (WAF)"},{"id":"3","text":"Network security groups (NSGs)"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":276,"type":"drag","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q276-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q276-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Entra ID Protection (formerly Azure AD Identity Protection) is the dedicated service for detecting and remediating compromised user identities using risk detection policies. Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps provides cloud access security broker (CASB) capabilities, specifically including threat protection alerts that detect abnormal activities like mass data downloads or exfiltration from SharePoint Online.","groups":[{"label":"Potentially compromised accounts","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Entra ID Protection"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Identity"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Bulk SharePoint downloads","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Entra ID Protection"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Identity"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":277,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q277-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q277-answer.webp","explanation":"GitHub Enterprise Cloud is required to enable code scanning for both public and private repositories while minimizing administrative overhead compared to self-hosted solutions. Dependabot automates the generation of security update pull requests to remediate identified vulnerabilities in project dependencies, meeting the automation requirement with minimal cost.","groups":[{"label":"To automate vulnerability code scanning","options":[{"id":"1","text":"GitHub Enterprise Cloud"},{"id":"2","text":"GitHub Enterprise Server"},{"id":"3","text":"GitHub Team"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"To automatically generate pull requests","options":[{"id":"1","text":"Codespaces"},{"id":"2","text":"Dependabot"},{"id":"3","text":"Dependency Tracker"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":278,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q278-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q278-answer.webp","explanation":"To detect vulnerability scans of the apps, you should use Azure Web Application Firewall (WAF), which provides built-in rule sets to identify and block malicious traffic patterns such as scanning tools. To detect whether newly deployed apps are vulnerable to attack, Microsoft Defender for App Service is the appropriate choice as it actively monitors for security misconfigurations and known vulnerabilities in web applications.","groups":[{"label":"To detect vulnerability scans of the apps","options":[{"id":"0","text":"azure WAF"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender External Attack Surface Management (Defender EASM)"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for App Service"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"To detect whether newly deployed apps are vulnerable to attack","options":[{"id":"0","text":"azure WAF"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender External Attack Surface Management (Defender EASM)"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for App Service"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":279,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q279-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q279-answer.webp","explanation":"Defender for Servers (formerly Azure Security Center) includes ‘Adaptive Application Control’ which automatically generates application allowlists based on observed behavior. It blocks unauthorized applications by default and quarantines them, requiring administrator approval to restore or permanently allow them, meeting the specified requirements.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"a compute policy in Azure Policy"},{"id":"B","text":"app governance in Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"C","text":"admin consent settings for enterprise applications in Microsoft Entra ID"},{"id":"D","text":"adaptive application controls in Defender for Servers"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":280,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q280-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q280-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps provides UEBA analytics to detect anomalous user activities, such as unusually high file downloads from SharePoint indicative of data exfiltration. Microsoft Defender for Office 365 includes Zero-Hour Auto Purge (ZAP), which scans emails and ‘Teams messages delivered before a threat was identified and automatically removes or quarantines malicious content.","groups":[{"label":"Identify data exfiltration attempts","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Identity"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Defender for Office 365"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Block malicious Teams messages","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Identity"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Defender for Office 365"}],"correct":["3"]}]},{"id":281,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q281-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q281-answer.webp","explanation":"The requirement for server-side pattern matching on encrypted data necessitates the use of Always Encrypted with secure enclaves, which relies on Intel Software Guard Extensions (Intel SGX) for hardware-based isolation. Azure SQL Database supports this enclave technology to enable rich queries like LIKE and CONTAINS while preventing DBAs from accessing plaintext data via Azure Attestation. While standard Always Encrypted (Option C) prevents DBA access, it does not support server-side pattern matching without enclaves, making Option D the correct choice.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"SQL Server on Azure Virtual Machines with virtualization-based security (VBS) enclaves"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure SQL Database with virtualization-based security (VBS) enclaves"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure SQL Managed Instance that has Always Encrypted configured"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure SQL Database with Intel Software Guard Extensions (Intel SGX) enclaves"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":282,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q282-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q282-answer.webp","explanation":"In the Microsoft Purview framework, Information Protection (specifically Sensitivity Labels) is the primary tool for classifying data, but it is not an option. Content Explorer is the feature within Information Protection used to search, view, and identify items that contain sensitive information types or have been labeled across Exchange, SharePoint, OneDrive, and Teams. DLP is primarily focused on preventing unauthorized transmission or exposure of sensitive data rather than the initial identification and classification phase. Therefore, given the specific requirement to ‘identify’ and the absence of Sensitivity Labels as a choice, Content Explorer is the correct recommendation for locating these documents.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"content explorer"},{"id":"B","text":"data loss prevention (DLP)"},{"id":"C","text":"eDiscovery"},{"id":"D","text":"data lifecycle management"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":283,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q283-question.webp","answerImage":null,"explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"file"},{"id":"B","text":"container"},{"id":"C","text":"folder"},{"id":"D","text":"account"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":284,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q284-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q284-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Dapr is the recommended solution because it provides a language-agnostic set of building blocks that natively handle secrets management and secure service-to-service communication using mTLS, all while abstracting away complex infrastructure configuration to minimize administrative effort. Although Istio (D) supports mTLS, it operates at the network/proxy level rather than providing built-in application-level secrets management APIs, and its operational complexity typically requires more administrative overhead than Dapr.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Flux"},{"id":"B","text":"Envoy"},{"id":"C","text":"Dapr"},{"id":"D","text":"Istio"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":285,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q285-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q285-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Storage includes a malware scanning feature specifically designed to detect threats in files stored in Azure Blob Storage. This solution analyzes uploaded blobs for malicious content, directly addressing the requirement to detect threats after upload. Other options like runtime protection or vulnerability assessment target container infrastructure rather than the file contents within storage accounts.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"sensitive data threat detection in Microsoft Defender for Storage"},{"id":"B","text":"runtime threat protection in Microsoft Defender for Containers"},{"id":"C","text":"vulnerability assessment in Microsoft Defender for Containers"},{"id":"D","text":"malware scanning in Microsoft Defender for Storage"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":286,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q286-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q286-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Policy is the correct solution because it allows you to define and enforce governance rules that ensure Azure resources comply with specific security baselines, such as the Microsoft Cloud Security Benchmark (MCSB). While Defender for Cloud provides recommendations and monitoring, Azure Policy is the mechanism used to actively enforce compliance standards during deployment and resource lifecycle management.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"DevOps security in Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Defender for App Service"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Policy"},{"id":"D","text":"a branch policy in Azure DevOps"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":287,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q287-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q287-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Entra Internet Access traffic policies are only enforced on devices that have the Global Secure Access (GSA) client installed and connected. Since Office! lacks the GSA client, its users bypass this specific policy entirely, whereas Office2 users are subject to the enforcement. The Conditional Access policy requires device compliance; therefore, compliant Office2 users gain access while non-compliant Office2 users are blocked.","groups":[{"label":"Compliant devices allowed","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Office1 only"},{"id":"1","text":"Office2 only"},{"id":"2","text":"Office1 and Office2"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Noncompliant devices allowed","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Office1 only"},{"id":"1","text":"Office2 only"},{"id":"2","text":"Office1 and Office2"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":288,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q288-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q288-answer.webp","explanation":"For Azure Blob Storage, encryption scopes allow customer-managed keys to be applied at the individual blob level, providing the most granular control. For Azure Files, the minimum granularity for customer-managed key encryption is the file share level, as individual files within a share cannot be encrypted with separate CMKs.","groups":[{"label":"For the containers","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Account"},{"id":"1","text":"Blob"},{"id":"2","text":"Container"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"For the shares","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Account"},{"id":"1","text":"File"},{"id":"2","text":"Share"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":289,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q289-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q289-answer.webp","explanation":"For Dedicated SQL pools, you should recommend Transparent Data Encryption (TDE) with customer- managed keys, as this feature is fully supported and allows you to manage the encryption keys via Azure Key Vault. For Serverless SQL pools, you must select Azure Storage Infrastructure Encryption with Microsoft-managed keys, because serverless pools do not support their own TDE configuration and rely on the underlying Azure Storage account's encryption standards, which default to Microsoft-managed keys for this specific exam context.","groups":[{"label":"Serverless SQL pool","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Storage infrastructure encryption with Microsoft-managed keys"},{"id":"1","text":"TDE with customer-managed keys"},{"id":"2","text":"TDE with Microsoft-managed keys"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Dedicated SQL pool","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Storage infrastructure encryption with Microsoft-managed keys"},{"id":"1","text":"TDE with customer-managed keys"},{"id":"2","text":"TDE with Microsoft-managed keys"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":290,"type":"drag","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q290-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q290-answer.webp","explanation":"First, create a Recovery Services Vault to provide the necessary infrastructure for storing Azure Files backups. Next, enable vault immutability to protect backup data against ransomware and unauthorized modifications during the retention period. Finally, lock the immutability policy to prevent any further changes to the retention settings or deletion of the vault, ensuring strict compliance with security requirements.","groups":[{"label":"First action","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Create Recovery Services vault"},{"id":"1","text":"Enable vault immutability"},{"id":"2","text":"Lock vault immutability"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Second action","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Create Recovery Services vault"},{"id":"1","text":"Enable vault immutability"},{"id":"2","text":"Lock vault immutability"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Third action","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Create Recovery Services vault"},{"id":"1","text":"Enable vault immutability"},{"id":"2","text":"Lock vault immutability"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":291,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q291-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q291-answer.webp","explanation":"Universal Tenant Restrictions v2 protects the authentication plane for all applications that authenticate with Microsoft Entra ID, including Microsoft and third-party apps. For the data plane, protection is specifically limited to SharePoint Online, Exchange Online, and Microsoft Graph, rather than covering all Microsoft 365 applications universally.","groups":[{"label":"Authentication plane resources","options":[{"id":"0","text":"All Microsoft Entra ID integrated apps, including third-party apps"},{"id":"1","text":"All Microsoft 365 apps"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft SharePoint Online and Exchange Online only"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Data plane resources","options":[{"id":"0","text":"All Microsoft Entra ID integrated apps, including third-party apps"},{"id":"1","text":"All Microsoft 365 apps"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft SharePoint Online and Exchange Online only"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":292,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q292-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q292-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Front Door is a global service that provides high availability across regions, ensuring access remains maintained during a regional outage while minimizing the need for redundant resources in every region. In contrast, Azure Application Gateway is regional and would require separate deployments to meet high-availability requirements. To block requests containing OWASP Core Rule Set (CRS) security threats, you must include Web Application Firewall (WAF) capabilities within the design.","groups":[{"label":"Resource type to provision","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Application Gateway"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Firewall Premium"},{"id":"2","text":"Azure Front Door"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Defender for App Service"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Option to enable","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Firewall web categories"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Web Application Firewall (WAF)"},{"id":"2","text":"ntrusion detection and prevention system (IDPS)"},{"id":"3","text":"Threat intelligence-based filtering"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":293,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q293-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q293-answer.webp","explanation":"The Standard tier (specifically StandardV2 in modern contexts) is required because it supports Role- Based Access Control (RBAC) and subscription management per Product, which allows restricting specific developer groups to specific sets of APIs. Applying rate limit policies at the 'Product' scope satisfies the requirement to configure limits per set of APIs while minimizing administrative effort compared to configuring each individual API.","groups":[{"label":"Pricing tier","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Basic V2"},{"id":"1","text":"Consumption"},{"id":"2","text":"Standard V2"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Scope","options":[{"id":"0","text":"API"},{"id":"1","text":"Product"},{"id":"2","text":"Workspace"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":294,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q294-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q294-answer.webp","explanation":"The application should use the Azure Instance Metadata Service (IMDS) endpoint to retrieve tokens without storing sensitive credentials or connection strings on the VM, satisfying the requirement to minimize sensitive data storage. A system-assigned managed identity is recommended because it is automatically created and deleted with the virtual machine, minimizing administrative effort during regular redeployments compared to user-assigned identities.","groups":[{"label":"Endpoint type","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Instance Metadata Service (IMDS)"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Graph REST API v1.0"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Identity Platform OAuth 2.0 access token"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"dentity type","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Service principal"},{"id":"1","text":"System-assigned managed identity"},{"id":"2","text":"User-assigned managed identity"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":295,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q295-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q295-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Resource Guard for multi-user authorization (MUA) is a regional resource, meaning the guard and the protected vault must reside in the same Azure region. Since the vaults are distributed across two distinct regions (as indicated by the table context implied by the correct answer logic), one Resource Guard is required per region to enforce MUA policies. Therefore, a total of 2 Resource Guard resources are needed to cover both regions.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"1"},{"id":"B","text":"2"},{"id":"C","text":"3"},{"id":"D","text":"4"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":296,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q296-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q296-answer.webp","explanation":"To maximize security isolation, Azure Resource Guard for Backup should be deployed in a subscription separate from the Recovery Services vault (or even a different tenant), though it must reside in the same Azure region. For multi-user authorization (MUA), the team responsible for managing backups requires only the Reader role on the Resource Guard. Assigning higher privileges like Contributor is explicitly prohibited by Microsoft documentation to maintain strict separation of duties and prevent privilege escalation.","groups":[{"label":"Location","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Sub1 in the East US Azure region"},{"id":"1","text":"Sub2 in the East US Azure region"},{"id":"2","text":"Sub1 in the West US Azure region"},{"id":"3","text":"Sub2 in the West US Azure region"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Role","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Contributor"},{"id":"1","text":"Owner"},{"id":"2","text":"Reader"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":297,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q297-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q297-answer.webp","explanation":"To enable users to authenticate to a third-party SaaS application using Microsoft Entra credentials, you must register the application as a Microsoft Entra enterprise application. This configuration establishes Single Sign-On (SSO) via protocols like SAML or OIDC, allowing Entra ID to act as the identity provider for App! without requiring on-premises infrastructure like AD FS.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"a relying party trust in Active Directory Federation Services (AD FS)"},{"id":"B","text":"a Microsoft Entra enterprise application"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Entra Application Proxy"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Entra External ID"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":298,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q298-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q298-answer.webp","explanation":"To enable threat hunting on historical data in Azure Data Lake, Microsoft Sentinel Notebooks provide the interactive environment for analysts to query and visualize data from external sources. Azure Data Factory is the recommended service for exporting large volumes of historical log data from the Log Analytics workspace to Azure Data Lake Storage, ensuring the data is available for downstream analysis.","groups":[{"label":"Microsoft Sentinel feature","options":[{"id":"0","text":"A notebook"},{"id":"1","text":"A playbook"},{"id":"2","text":"A threat intelligence connector"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Azure resource","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Data Factory pipeline"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Logic Apps connector"},{"id":"2","text":"Azure Synapse workspace"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":299,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q299-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q299-answer.webp","explanation":"Document fingerprinting is the correct inspection method because it specifically identifies standard forms containing predefined structures, which aligns with the requirement to detect documents based on a predefined form. The ‘Authentication context’ Conditional Access option is the appropriate session policy addition, as it triggers step-up authentication when specific conditions defined in Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps are met.","groups":[{"label":"nspection method","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Exact data match (EDM)"},{"id":"1","text":"Fingerprint"},{"id":"2","text":"Trainable classifier"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Option","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Authentication context"},{"id":"1","text":"Authentication strength"},{"id":"2","text":"Custom control"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":300,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q300-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q300-answer.webp","explanation":"The solution is incorrect because web content filtering in Microsoft Defender for Endpoint primarily manages access based on URL categories and reputations, not the specific risk scores of SaaS applications. To meet the goal of allowing low-risk and blocking high-risk SaaS apps based on Microsoft's identification, you must use Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps (formerly CASB) to create app control policies that leverage the Cloud App Catalog risk scores.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":301,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q301-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q301-answer.webp","explanation":"The solution meets the goal because Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps uses risk scores to categorize SaaS applications. By unsanctioning apps identified as high risk, you effectively block access to them via integrated enforcement (such as MDE or firewall policies), while allowing sanctioned low-risk apps to remain accessible. Although marking an app ‘unsanctioned’ is a policy classification rather than an immediate network block by itself, in the context of Microsoft Defender XDR solutions, this configuration step enables the necessary controls to restrict access based on risk tier, satisfying the requirement to differentiate between high and low-risk access.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":302,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q302-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q302-answer.webp","explanation":"SaaS Security Posture Management (SSPM) is designed to assess and remediate security configurations within sanctioned SaaS applications, not to enforce access controls based on risk scores. To block high-risk apps and allow low-risk ones, you should use Cloud App Discovery insights combined with Conditional Access policies or App Control policies in Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":303,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q303-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q303-answer.webp","explanation":"The proposed solution fails because Intune app protection policies manage data handling within apps (e.g., copy/paste controls) rather than controlling access based on risk scores. Additionally, standard Conditional Access policies evaluate device compliance and user attributes but do not natively block or allow SaaS applications based on Microsoft's specific risk ratings. To meet the requirement of blocking high-risk and allowing low-risk SaaS apps, you must configure Access Policies within Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps (formerly MCAS), which integrates with Defender XDR to enforce decisions based on application reputation and risk.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes"},{"id":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":304,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q304-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q304-answer.webp","explanation":"FIPS 140-2 Level 3 compliance mandates hardware-based key protection, which is provided by Azure Key Vault using the Premium SKU (for HSM-backed keys) or Managed HSM. The Premium SKU is recommended over Managed HSM to minimize costs while meeting the single-tenancy and compliance requirements. To follow the principle of least privilege with minimal administrative effort for 20 apps, Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) should be used instead of legacy Access Policies, as RBAC provides more granular, centralized permission management across a single vault.","groups":[{"label":"Azure service","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Key Vault Premium SKU"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Key Vault Standard SKU"},{"id":"2","text":"Azure Key Vault Managed HSM"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Authorization","options":[{"id":"0","text":"20 vaults with RBAC"},{"id":"1","text":"One vault with RBAC"},{"id":"2","text":"One vault with RBAC and access policies"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":305,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q305-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q305-answer.webp","explanation":"To block attempts from malicious bots with minimal administrative effort, you should configure the Managed Rule Set, as it includes built-in protections against common web threats and bot attacks. For rate limiting incoming connections, you must configure a Custom Rule, since rate limiting is not available in the default managed rule sets and requires specific custom policies.","groups":[{"label":"Rate limit","options":[{"id":"0","text":"A custom rule"},{"id":"1","text":"An exclusion list"},{"id":"2","text":"A managed rule set"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Block malicious bots","options":[{"id":"0","text":"A custom rule"},{"id":"1","text":"An exclusion list"},{"id":"2","text":"A managed rule set"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":306,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q306-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q306-answer.webp","explanation":"Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) in Azure API Management is primarily used to manage administrative permissions for resources, which are organized and scoped within Workspaces. API keys, on the other hand, are generated at the Subscription level to authenticate client applications and control their consumption of specific API products.","groups":[{"label":"RBAC roles","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Products"},{"id":"1","text":"Subscriptions"},{"id":"2","text":"Workspaces"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Keys","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Products"},{"id":"1","text":"Subscriptions"},{"id":"2","text":"Workspaces"}],"correct":["1"]}]},{"id":307,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q307-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q307-answer.webp","explanation":"A service principal is the local representation of an application within a specific Microsoft Entra tenant. Creating it in contoso.com allows users from that tenant to authenticate to the multi-tenant App! registered in fabrikam.com.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"a service principal"},{"id":"B","text":"asystem-assigned managed identity"},{"id":"C","text":"an application object"},{"id":"D","text":"auser-assigned managed identity"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":308,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q308-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q308-answer.webp","explanation":"Teams channel messages are stored in Exchange Online mailboxes; the Preservation Hold Library retains copies that cannot be deleted or altered, providing immutable protection against ransomware. Teams channel files reside in SharePoint Online, where the Files Restore feature enables point-in-time recovery of document libraries compromised by encryption.","groups":[{"label":"Teams messages","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Exchange Online Protection"},{"id":"1","text":"Single Item Recovery"},{"id":"2","text":"The Preservation Hold library"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Teams files","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Files Restore"},{"id":"1","text":"Litigation Hold"},{"id":"2","text":"The Preservation Hold library"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":309,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q309-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q309-answer.webp","explanation":"Supply chain attacks in this context stem from vulnerabilities in third-party and open-source components used by the applications. Dependabot alerts automatically scan these dependencies for known security vulnerabilities and create pull requests to update them, directly mitigating the risk of compromised software packages.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"secret scanning"},{"id":"B","text":"Dependabot alerts"},{"id":"C","text":"app governance in Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"D","text":"NuGet Audit"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":310,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q310-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q310-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps is a Cloud Access Security Broker (CASB) specifically designed to provide visibility into usage of SaaS applications. It enables real-time detection and prevention of risky behaviors, such as unauthorized data access or malicious file uploads, which aligns with Microsoft Cybersecurity Reference Architectures (MCRA) recommendations.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Purview Information Protection"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":311,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q311-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q311-answer.webp","explanation":"The Recovery Services Agent (RSA) is the correct choice for backing up data from an on-premises Windows Server when direct internet connectivity to Azure Backup is restricted, as it supports offline media transfer methods (like tape or disk) for air-gapped scenarios. The Recovery Services vault serves as the designated Azure storage repository within subscription Sub! to store and manage these backups securely.","groups":[{"label":"On-premises datacenter","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure virtual machine extension"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Azure Backup Server (MABS)"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Azure Recovery Services (MARS) agent"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Azure subscription","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Recovery Services vault"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Backup vault"},{"id":"2","text":"Azure Storage block blobs"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":312,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q312-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q312-answer.webp","explanation":"To scan Azure Blob Storage accounts, you should use **Microsoft Purview Data Map** (or simply 'Scan' in the context of cloud connectors), as Microsoft Purview natively integrates with Azure storage services to discover and classify data using custom Sensitive Information Types (SITs) without additional agents. For on-premises shared folders containing Windows Server data, you should use a **Microsoft Purview Scanner** (specifically an on-premises scanner deployed via the scanner package), because on-premises file shares require an installed agent to crawl directories and apply classification labels based on custom SITs.","groups":[{"label":"Storage blobs","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Compliance Manager"},{"id":"1","text":"Data Map"},{"id":"2","text":"nsider risk management"},{"id":"3","text":"The Information Protection scanner"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Shared folders","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Compliance Manager"},{"id":"1","text":"Data Map"},{"id":"2","text":"nsider risk management"},{"id":"3","text":"The Information Protection scanner"}],"correct":["3"]}]},{"id":313,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q313-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q313-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Managed Identity allows App! to authenticate directly to Azure SQL Database without storing credentials in the application code or configuration. This eliminates the risk of credential leakage and misuse by developers, as no passwords or secrets are exposed for SRV. It minimizes administrative effort compared to other options by automatically handling credential rotation and management within Azure Active Directory.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"a managed identity"},{"id":"B","text":"a group managed service account (gMSA)"},{"id":"C","text":"a delegated managed service account (dMSA)"},{"id":"D","text":"a federated identity credential"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":314,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q314-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q314-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Key Vault is the purpose-built Azure service designed to securely store secrets, including passwords for external services. To meet the requirement of secure authentication without managing credentials manually, App! should use a Managed Identity. This allows the app to authenticate to Key Vault using its own identity, ensuring zero-credential management and enhanced security.","groups":[{"label":"Service","options":[{"id":"0","text":"azure Key Vault"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Entra ID Protection"},{"id":"2","text":"Privileged Identity Management (PIM)"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Authentication method","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Certificate"},{"id":"1","text":"Group managed service account (gMSA)"},{"id":"2","text":"Guest account"},{"id":"3","text":"Managed identity"}],"correct":["3"]}]},{"id":315,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q315-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q315-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud is the unified cloud security platform that supports vulnerability assessments for on-premises SQL Server instances when connected via Azure Arc. It specifically provides the capability to scan databases for vulnerabilities and misconfigurations, aligning with the requirement to secure data in hybrid environments.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Microsoft Purview data loss prevention (DLP)"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Purview data governance"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Defender Vulnerability Management"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":316,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q316-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q316-answer.webp","explanation":"Microsoft Defender Vulnerability Management (MDVM) provides specific capabilities to assess and identify outdated hardware drivers and firmware on hybrid servers, which are not included in Defender for Servers Plan 1. Adding the MDVM add-on extends the existing protection with these assessment features without requiring a full upgrade to Plan 2 or switching to unrelated management tools like Azure Update Manager.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Change all the servers to Microsoft Defender for Servers Plan 2."},{"id":"B","text":"Add the Microsoft Intune Suite add-on."},{"id":"C","text":"Onboard all the servers to Azure Update Manager."},{"id":"D","text":"Add Microsoft Defender Vulnerability Management add-ons."}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":317,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q317-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q317-answer.webp","explanation":"To block Al prompts containing confidential data on third-party websites, you must recommend **Microsoft Defender for Endpoint** (or Microsoft Intune/Endpoint Manager with conditional access), as Purview DLP primarily protects M365 services and does not natively inspect general web traffic on endpoints without specific endpoint protection integration. To scan Al prompts submitted to these third-party sites, you need to **Onboard each device to Microsoft Purview** (specifically leveraging Microsoft 365 Copilot or Purview Information Protection capabilities integrated with endpoint agents) or use **Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps** if the focus is on cloud access brokerage. However, based on standard SC-100 solutions: 1, **Block Al prompts**: Use **Microsoft Defender for Endpoint** to enforce policies that prevent sending sensitive data identified by SITs to unauthorized third-party Al services via endpoint security controls. 2. **Enable scanning**: Use **Microsoft Purview Information Protection** (via MIP SDK or Endpoint DLP) integrated into the device to scan and classify data before it leaves the device.","groups":[{"label":"Microsoft Purview solution to use to block Al prompts","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Data loss prevention"},{"id":"1","text":"nformation barriers"},{"id":"2","text":"nformation protection"},{"id":"3","text":"nsider risk management."}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"To enable scanning of the Al prompts","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Assign a Microsoft 365 Copilot license to each user."},{"id":"1","text":"Deploy the Microsoft Purview Information Protection scanner."},{"id":"2","text":"mplement a data connector."},{"id":"3","text":"Onboard each device to Microsoft Purview."}],"correct":["3"]}]},{"id":318,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q318-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q318-answer.webp","explanation":"Deploying two connectors in a new connector group ensures high availability and maximizes redundancy for the on-premises connection. Using Microsoft Peering for ExpressRoute allows Private Access traffic to traverse the Microsoft global network directly, minimizing latency and maximizing security by avoiding the public internet.","groups":[{"label":"Private network connectors","options":[{"id":"0","text":"One default and one new group connector"},{"id":"1","text":"Two connectors in a new connector group"},{"id":"2","text":"Two connectors in the default connector group"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"ExpressRoute peering","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Microsoft peering"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft peering with premium add-on"},{"id":"2","text":"Private peering"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":319,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q319-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q319-answer.webp","explanation":"To monitor third-party generative Al app usage via Microsoft Purview Data Security Posture Management for Al (DSPM for Al), you must first onboard endpoint devices to Microsoft Purview. This is a mandatory prerequisite because DSPM for Al relies on the Microsoft Defender for Endpoint agent installed on Windows devices to detect and report telemetry regarding unmanaged third-party Al applications. Enabling insider risk management or configuring data connectors are separate functionalities that do not satisfy the specific prerequisites for DSPM for Al.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Enable Microsoft Purview insider risk management for all the users."},{"id":"B","text":"Onboard all endpoint devices to Microsoft Purview."},{"id":"C","text":"Configure Microsoft Purview data connectors for the generative Al apps."},{"id":"D","text":"License all the users for Microsoft 365 Copilot."}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":320,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q320-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q320-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Front Door is a global service that routes traffic to the nearest backend based on latency, satisfying the geographic proximity requirement. Its integrated Web Application Firewall (WAF) provides inspection using the OWASP Core Rule Set (CRS). Additionally, it supports Microsoft- managed X.509 certificates, minimizing administrative effort compared to regional solutions like Application Gateway or Azure Firewall.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure Firewall Premium"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure Front Door with a web application firewall (WAF)"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Firewall Standard"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure Application Gateway with a web application firewall (WAF)"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":321,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q321-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q321-answer.webp","explanation":"For the dedicated SQL pool (Pool), enable **Bring Your Own Key (BYOK)** to add a second layer of encryption using customer-managed keys in Azure Key Vault, supplementing the default platform- managed TDE. For the serverless SQL pool, you cannot configure a separate key management layer as it is platform-managed; therefore, no additional actionable encryption recommendation exists for it in this context (often represented as ‘None’ or leaving it blank depending on the specific dropdown options provided).","groups":[{"label":"Dedicated SQL pool","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Infrastructure encryption"},{"id":"1","text":"Server-side encryption"},{"id":"2","text":"TDE with customer-managed keys"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Serverless SQL pool","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Infrastructure encryption"},{"id":"1","text":"Server-side encryption"},{"id":"2","text":"No additional customer-managed encryption option"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":322,"type":"multiple","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q322-question.webp","answerImage":null,"explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure Cloud HSM"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Cloud PKI"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Key Vault"},{"id":"D","text":"service-side encryption (SSE)"},{"id":"E","text":"Transparent Data Encryption (TDE)"},{"id":"F","text":"Azure Disk Encryption"}],"correct":["C","D"]}]},{"id":323,"type":"hotspot","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q323-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q323-answer.webp","explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Azure service","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Key Vault Premium SKU"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Key Vault Standard SKU"},{"id":"2","text":"Azure Key Vault Managed HSM"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Authorization","options":[{"id":"0","text":"One vault with RBAC"},{"id":"1","text":"One vault with access policies"},{"id":"2","text":"20 vaults with RBAC"},{"id":"3","text":"20 vaults with access policies"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":324,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q324-question.webp","answerImage":null,"explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"a Conditional Access policy"},{"id":"B","text":"a Quick Access app"},{"id":"C","text":"a traffic forwarding profile"},{"id":"D","text":"a security profile"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":325,"type":"single","topic":4,"questionImage":"images/q325-question.webp","answerImage":null,"explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"a Conditional Access policy that grants access from only compliant devices"},{"id":"B","text":"a forwarding profile that directs all outbound internet traffic through the Microsoft backbone network"},{"id":"C","text":"a connector group that contains the connectors used to access the web apps and a single endpoint for the internal fully qualified domain names (FQDNs)"},{"id":"D","text":"an access policy that limits connections based on user groups"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":326,"type":"single","topic":5,"questionImage":"images/q326-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q326-answer.webp","explanation":"The Microsoft DART ransomware approach explicitly emphasizes isolating compromised systems from the network without powering them off to preserve forensic evidence. Machine isolation is the specific mechanism in Microsoft Defender for Endpoint that cuts off network connectivity for a targeted device while maintaining its operational state for investigation.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"controlled folder access"},{"id":"B","text":"application isolation"},{"id":"C","text":"memory scanning"},{"id":"D","text":"machine isolation"},{"id":"E","text":"user isolation"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":327,"type":"single","topic":5,"questionImage":"images/q327-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q327-answer.webp","explanation":"The Microsoft Cloud Security Benchmark provides specific security baselines and recommendations for cloud-centric control areas like endpoints, data protection, and storage. It aligns with the Microsoft Cloud Adoption Framework to help organizations implement consistent security standards across Azure resources. The other options either refer to broader business goals (A), non-standard terminology (B), or a subset of controls that is fully encompassed by the comprehensive benchmark guidelines (C).","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"business resilience"},{"id":"B","text":"modem access control"},{"id":"C","text":"network isolation"},{"id":"D","text":"security baselines in the Microsoft Cloud Security Benchmark"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":328,"type":"hotspot","topic":5,"questionImage":"images/q328-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q328-answer.webp","explanation":"According to the Microsoft Cloud Adoption Framework (CAF) for Secure DevOps, the first stage requires implementing protected branches in Git to prevent direct pushes and enforce code review workflows. The second stage involves securing deployment credentials by storing secrets in Azure Key Vault rather than embedding them in pipelines or scripts.","groups":[{"label":"Git workflow","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Key Vault"},{"id":"1","text":"Custom build-agent roles"},{"id":"2","text":"Protected branches"},{"id":"3","text":"Azure resource locks"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Deployment credentials","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Key Vault"},{"id":"1","text":"Custom build-agent roles"},{"id":"2","text":"Protected branches"},{"id":"3","text":"Azure resource locks"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":329,"type":"hotspot","topic":5,"questionImage":"images/q329-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q329-answer.webp","explanation":"For Azure Backup, enable Security PIN protection to prevent unauthorized users from altering or deleting backup configurations. For Azure Storage, use Immutable storage (Blob Immutability) to ensure data cannot be modified or deleted for a set period, protecting backups against ransomware encryption.","groups":[{"label":"Azure Backup","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Access policies"},{"id":"1","text":"Access tiers"},{"id":"2","text":"Encryption by using platform-managed keys"},{"id":"3","text":"immutable storage"},{"id":"4","text":"A security PIN"}],"correct":["4"]},{"label":"Azure Storage","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Access policies"},{"id":"1","text":"Access tiers"},{"id":"2","text":"Encryption by using platform-managed keys"},{"id":"3","text":"immutable storage"},{"id":"4","text":"A security PIN"}],"correct":["3"]}]},{"id":330,"type":"single","topic":5,"questionImage":"images/q330-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q330-answer.webp","explanation":"Multi-user authorization (MUA) using Resource Guard is the recommended best practice for protecting Azure Backup vaults against ransomware and compromised administrator accounts. It enforces a separation of duties by requiring approval from a separate Security Admin account to authorize critical operations like backup deletion, ensuring that a single compromised Backup Admin account cannot delete data independently.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"From Azure Backup, configure multi-user authorization by using Resource Guard."},{"id":"B","text":"From Microsoft Azure Backup Setup, register MABS with a Recovery Services vault."},{"id":"C","text":"From a Recovery Services vault, generate a security PIN for critical operations."},{"id":"D","text":"From Azure AD Privileged Identity Management (PIM), create a role assignment for the Backup Contributor role."}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":331,"type":"single","topic":5,"questionImage":"images/q331-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q331-answer.webp","explanation":"The question specifies ‘without being locked out;' which directly points to maintaining access during an incident. Emergency access accounts (break-glass accounts) are explicitly designed to ensure administrative access remains available if primary credentials or systems are compromised by ransomware, preventing total lockout. While PAWs limit initial damage scope, they do not guarantee access if the identity infrastructure itself is targeted.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"device compliance policies"},{"id":"B","text":"Privileged Access Workstations (PAWs)"},{"id":"C","text":"Customer Lockbox for Microsoft Azure"},{"id":"D","text":"emergency access accounts"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":332,"type":"single","topic":5,"questionImage":"images/q332-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q332-answer.webp","explanation":"According to Microsoft Security Best Practices for ransomware protection, the first phase is to prepare a recovery plan. This step ensures the organization has a viable alternative to paying ransoms by defining specific actions, protocols, and recovery objectives before implementing technical controls like privileged access strategies or data protection.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Develop a privileged identity strategy."},{"id":"B","text":"Implement data protection."},{"id":"C","text":"Develop a privileged access strategy."},{"id":"D","text":"Prepare a recovery plan."}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":333,"type":"hotspot","topic":5,"questionImage":"images/q333-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q333-answer.webp","explanation":"The Microsoft Cloud Adoption Framework (CAF) defines DevSecOps controls mapped to specific CI/CD pipeline stages to ensure security is integrated throughout the lifecycle. Code scanning and secret detection occur during the Code stage, while dependency checks and container image scanning happen during Build. Security policies are validated in Test, infrastructure-as-code compliance is checked in Release, and runtime protection along with incident response monitoring occurs in Operate.","groups":[{"label":"Threat modeling","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Build and test"},{"id":"1","text":"Commit the code"},{"id":"2","text":"Go to production"},{"id":"3","text":"Operate"},{"id":"4","text":"Plan and develop"}],"correct":["4"]},{"label":"Actionable intelligence","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Build and test"},{"id":"1","text":"Commit the code"},{"id":"2","text":"Go to production"},{"id":"3","text":"Operate"},{"id":"4","text":"Plan and develop"}],"correct":["3"]},{"label":"Dynamic application security testing (DAST)","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Build and test"},{"id":"1","text":"Commit the code"},{"id":"2","text":"Go to production"},{"id":"3","text":"Operate"},{"id":"4","text":"Plan and develop"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":334,"type":"single","topic":5,"questionImage":"images/q334-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q334-answer.webp","explanation":"Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST) involves analyzing a running application to identify security vulnerabilities, which is the core function of automated penetration testing. In contrast, unit testing and dependency checks are static analysis methods performed on code or artifacts before execution, while threat modeling is a proactive design-phase activity.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"unit testing"},{"id":"B","text":"penetration testing"},{"id":"C","text":"dependency checks"},{"id":"D","text":"threat modeling"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":335,"type":"multiple","topic":5,"questionImage":"images/q335-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q335-answer.webp","explanation":"Continuous Access Evaluation (CAE) is the specific Azure AD feature designed to revoke or deny access in near-real-time based on changes in user status, such as account deletion, password reset, or token revocation. CAE operates by updating the access token validation logic used by Conditional Access policies, allowing them to enforce immediate restrictions without waiting for token expiration.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"continuous access evaluation"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure AD Application Proxy"},{"id":"C","text":"a sign-in risk policy"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure AD Privileged Identity Management (PIM)"},{"id":"E","text":"Conditional Access"}],"correct":["A","E"]}]},{"id":336,"type":"hotspot","topic":5,"questionImage":"images/q336-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q336-answer.webp","explanation":"To prevent a compromised administrator from permanently deleting backups, you must use Multi- user authorization (MUA) because it requires a second authorized user to approve critical deletions, neutralizing the threat of a single rogue account. To prevent the disabling of backup jobs on the MARS agent, you should use a Security PIN, as this feature enforces an additional authentication step locally for administrative actions like stopping or uninstalling the agent.","groups":[{"label":"Deleted backups","options":[{"id":"0","text":"A security PIN for critical operations"},{"id":"1","text":"Encryption by using a customer-managed key"},{"id":"2","text":"Multi-user authorization by using Resource Guard"},{"id":"3","text":"Soft delete of backups"}],"correct":["3"]},{"label":"Disabled backups","options":[{"id":"0","text":"A security PIN for critical operations"},{"id":"1","text":"Encryption by using a customer-managed key"},{"id":"2","text":"Multi-user authorization by using Resource Guard"},{"id":"3","text":"Soft delete of backups"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":337,"type":"single","topic":5,"questionImage":"images/q337-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q337-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Application Gateway is the recommended ingress controller for AKS because it provides Layer 7 load balancing and integrates with the Azure Web Application Firewall (WAF) to protect web services from common threats like SQL injection and XSS. Unlike Azure Load Balancer (Layer 4) or Azure Firewall (network-level), Application Gateway offers application-aware security features specifically designed for web traffic ingress.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure Load Balancer"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure Application Gateway"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Front Door"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure Firewall"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":338,"type":"single","topic":5,"questionImage":"images/q338-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q338-answer.webp","explanation":"To review NIST 800-53 compliance, you must assign the specific built-in initiative designed for this standard. Azure Policy provides a ‘NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5 Regulatory Compliance’ built-in initiative that maps Azure resources to the required controls. Assigning this initiative at the subscription scope ensures all resources are evaluated against the compliance framework.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"From Azure Policy, assign a built-in initiative that has a scope of the subscription."},{"id":"B","text":"From Azure Policy, assign a built-in policy definition that has a scope of the subscription."},{"id":"C","text":"From Defender for Cloud, review the Azure security baseline for audit report."},{"id":"D","text":"From Defender for Cloud, enable Defender for Cloud plans."}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":339,"type":"single","topic":5,"questionImage":"images/q339-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q339-answer.webp","explanation":"The Microsoft Cloud Adoption Framework (CAF) recommends aligning management groups under Landing Zones with workload archetypes, primarily distinguished by their access and security requirements. This typically results in splitting workloads into 'Corp' (internal/hybrid connectivity) and ‘Online’ (public-facing) groups to apply appropriate network and policy controls. Option C is technically more accurate regarding the CAF's specific archetype definition (Corp vs Online), but in this specific exam question context, Option D is often cited as the correct answer because SDLC environments (Dew/Test/Prod) are a common alternative architectural pattern for organizing workloads within those archetypes to enforce lifecycle-specific policies. However, note that recent CAF updates emphasize access patterns (Option C) over pure SDLC separation at the top level.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"geographical locations"},{"id":"B","text":"the internal billing chargeback structure"},{"id":"C","text":"the hybrid connectivity requirements"},{"id":"D","text":"software development lifecycle (SDLC) environments"}],"correct":["D"]}]},{"id":340,"type":"single","topic":5,"questionImage":"images/q340-question.webp","answerImage":null,"explanation":"","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"spoof intelligence insight in Exchange Online Protection (EOP)"},{"id":"B","text":"Attack path analysis in Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"C","text":"Attack simulation training in Microsoft Defender for Office 365"},{"id":"D","text":"user activity reports in Microsoft Purview insider risk management"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":341,"type":"hotspot","topic":6,"questionImage":"images/q341-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q341-answer.webp","explanation":"Box 1: Azure AD Identity Protection (now Entra ID Identity Protection) is required to detect leaked credentials in the Azure AD tenant and enforce smart lockout policies that prevent on-premises AD DS accounts from being locked out by brute force attacks targeting cloud identities. Box 2: Microsoft Defender for Identity (formerly Azure ATP) is deployed on-premises to monitor network traffic and directly detect brute force attacks, such as LDAP enumeration, against the AD DS forest.","groups":[{"label":"Azure AD-targeted threats","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure AD Identity Protection"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure AD Password Protection"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"AD DS-targeted threats","options":[{"id":"0","text":"AD DS account lockout policy"},{"id":"1","text":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Identity"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":342,"type":"hotspot","topic":6,"questionImage":"images/q342-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q342-answer.webp","explanation":"Box 1 should be ‘Region and Azure AD tenant’ because Litware has subsidiaries in France requiring data residency compliance (mandating regional separation) and multiple Azure AD tenants from acquisitions (requiring tenant-specific workspaces for connectors). Box 2 should be ‘Azure Lighthouse’ as it enables centralized, cross-tenant subscription management without the overhead of maintaining guest accounts, directly satisfying the hybrid requirements.","groups":[{"label":"Segment Sentinel workspaces","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure AD tenant"},{"id":"1","text":"Enterprise"},{"id":"2","text":"Region and Azure AD tenant"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"Integrate subscriptions","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure AD B2B self-service signup"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure AD B2C self-service signup"},{"id":"2","text":"Azure Lighthouse subscription onboarding"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":343,"type":"hotspot","topic":6,"questionImage":"images/q343-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q343-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure Lighthouse is the correct solution for centralized, cross-tenant subscription management because it allows managing multiple tenants without maintaining guest accounts, directly addressing the hybrid requirement. Azure Arc enables the management of on-premises resources from Azure by extending Azure Policy, change tracking, asset inventory, and patch management capabilities to non-Azure environments.","groups":[{"label":"To centralize subscription management","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure AD B2B"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure AD B2C"},{"id":"2","text":"Azure Lighthouse"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"To enable the management of on-premises resources","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Arc"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Stack Edge"},{"id":"2","text":"Azure Stack Hub"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":344,"type":"single","topic":6,"questionImage":"images/q344-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q344-answer.webp","explanation":"The case study explicitly requires providing a secure score scoped to the landing zone. Microsoft Defender for Cloud is the service that calculates and provides Secure Score, while also helping to minimize data exfiltration risks through its security recommendations and continuous assessment capabilities. Other options like Azure Private DNS or ExpressRoute do not provide security posture scoring.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"an ExpressRoute gateway"},{"id":"B","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"C","text":"an Azure Private DNS zone"},{"id":"D","text":"Azure DDoS Protection Standard"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":345,"type":"hotspot","topic":7,"questionImage":"images/q345-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q345-answer.webp","explanation":"The requirement for Contoso developers to use their existing contoso.onmicrosoft.com credentials necessitates creating B2B guest accounts in the Fabrikam tenant, as cross-tenant access is achieved via Azure AD B28 collaboration. Additionally, the mandate to verify group membership every month requires implementing an Access Review (specifically a Microsoft Entra ID Governance access review) to periodically assess and approve or remove user assignments.","groups":[{"label":"Developer account type","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Guest in Contoso tenant"},{"id":"1","text":"Guest in Fabrikam tenant"},{"id":"2","text":"Synced user in Fabrikam AD DS"},{"id":"3","text":"User in Fabrikam tenant"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Identity Governance component","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Connected organization"},{"id":"1","text":"Access package"},{"id":"2","text":"Access review"},{"id":"3","text":"Azure AD role"}],"correct":["2"]}]},{"id":346,"type":"single","topic":7,"questionImage":"images/q346-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q346-answer.webp","explanation":"The requirement specifies that the InfraSec group should only configure specific resources (NSGs, Azure Firewall, WAF, and Front Door). Standard built-in roles like Network Contributor grant broader permissions than necessary, violating the principle of least privilege. A custom RBAC role allows you to define precise permissions limited strictly to these resource types and actions, ensuring secure delegation without excess access. Assigning a subscription or management group is incorrect as they are scope containers, not permission definitions, and using built-in roles fails the specific constraint of limiting access to only the listed technologies.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"a subscription"},{"id":"B","text":"a custom role-based access control (RBAC) role"},{"id":"C","text":"a resource group"},{"id":"D","text":"a management group"}],"correct":["B"]}]},{"id":347,"type":"hotspot","topic":8,"questionImage":"images/q347-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q347-answer.webp","explanation":"The requirement to notify administrators of non-compliant AWS EC2 instances is met by onboarding the AWS account to Microsoft Defender for Cloud, which provides a unified secure score and compliance assessment for multi-cloud resources. The requirement to query AWS service logs directly from the Azure environment is fulfilled by using the AWS connector in Microsoft Sentinel, which ingests these logs for analysis and querying.","groups":[{"label":"For the AWS EC2 instances","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Blueprints"},{"id":"1","text":"Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Defender for servers"},{"id":"4","text":"Microsoft Endpoint Manager"},{"id":"5","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"For the AWS service logs","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Blueprints"},{"id":"1","text":"Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"2","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"3","text":"Microsoft Defender for servers"},{"id":"4","text":"Microsoft Endpoint Manager"},{"id":"5","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"}],"correct":["5"]}]},{"id":348,"type":"single","topic":8,"questionImage":"images/q348-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q348-answer.webp","explanation":"The case study states that the 20 virtual machines are not onboarded to Microsoft Defender for Cloud and generate a recommendation for a vulnerability assessment solution. To resolve this, you must first enable the integrated Qualys scanner within Defender for Cloud (Option A), as it is the native solution for VM vulnerability assessment in this context. Onboarding to Azure Arc (Option D) or Defender for Endpoint (Option B) is not required for basic vulnerability scanning of Azure VMs already managed by Defender for Cloud, and device compliance policies (Option C) address different security requirements.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Enable the Qualys scanner in Defender for Cloud."},{"id":"B","text":"Onboard the virtual machines to Microsoft Defender for Endpoint."},{"id":"C","text":"Create a device compliance policy in Microsoft Endpoint Manager."},{"id":"D","text":"Onboard the virtual machines to Azure Arc."}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":349,"type":"single","topic":8,"questionImage":"images/q349-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q349-answer.webp","explanation":"The security requirements explicitly state that administrators must connect to a secure host for remote administration and that this host must be provisioned from a custom operating system image. Azure Virtual Desktop (AVD) supports the deployment of session hosts using custom images, fulfilling the specific requirement for a standardized, secure admin environment. While Azure Bastion provides secure connectivity, it is a service for accessing existing resources rather than a provisioning solution for custom-image-based administrative hosts.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"just-in-time (JIT) VM access"},{"id":"B","text":"an Azure Bastion host"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure Virtual Desktop"},{"id":"D","text":"a network security group (NSG)"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":350,"type":"hotspot","topic":8,"questionImage":"images/q350-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q350-answer.webp","explanation":"To automatically remediate existing resources to comply with the HIPAA HITRUST standard, you must assign a policy definition or initiative that includes a ‘deploylfNotExists' or 'modify' effect. The remediation task runs using a **Managed Identity**, which provides the necessary permissions for the policy engine to create or modify resource configurations (such as enabling vulnerability assessment agents) on the target virtual machines. To exclude the TestRG from this compliance assessment, you must +*Update a policy assignment**. Exclusions are defined at the assignment level via excluded scopes, allowing you to specify which resources or resource groups should not be subject to the policy enforcement.","groups":[{"label":"Automated remediation identity","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Automation account"},{"id":"1","text":"Managed identity"},{"id":"2","text":"Workflow automation"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Exclude TestRG","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Edit Azure blueprint"},{"id":"1","text":"Modify workflow automation"},{"id":"2","text":"Modify policy definition"},{"id":"3","text":"Update policy assignment"}],"correct":["3"]}]},{"id":351,"type":"hotspot","topic":9,"questionImage":"images/q351-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q351-answer.webp","explanation":"The requirement to leverage built-in Azure Policy definitions for regulatory compliance across the entire managed environment is best met by assigning Azure Policy initiatives to Management Groups, as this scope covers all subscriptions and resources within the hierarchy. Additionally, to evaluate compliance on hybrid resources (on-premises servers) using Azure Policy, Azure Arc must be used to onboard these non-Azure resources into the Azure resource management plane.","groups":[{"label":"Regulatory compliance scope","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Policy definitions at management groups"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure Policy initiatives at management groups"},{"id":"2","text":"Azure Policy initiatives at subscriptions"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Hybrid resource assessment","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Azure Arc"},{"id":"1","text":"Group Policy"},{"id":"2","text":"PowerShell DSC"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":352,"type":"single","topic":10,"questionImage":"images/q352-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q352-answer.webp","explanation":"Service chaining is the recommended strategy because it uses User Defined Routes (UDRs) to direct internet-bound traffic (0.0.0.0/0) from the spoke landing zones through a centralized network virtual appliance, such as Azure Firewall, in the hub subscription. This approach ensures that all outbound traffic passes through the security control point for inspection and logging before egressing to the internet, satisfying the requirement to route traffic via Azure Firewall while maintaining a hub-and- spoke architecture.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"local network gateways"},{"id":"B","text":"forced tunneling"},{"id":"C","text":"service chaining"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":353,"type":"hotspot","topic":10,"questionImage":"images/q353-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q353-answer.webp","explanation":"To meet the requirement of minimizing data exfiltration risk and maximizing security, +*Private Endpoints** should be selected. Private Endpoints provision a network interface into the virtual network via Azure Private Link, allowing VMs to access App Service web apps securely over the Microsoft backbone without exposing them to public internet endpoints. While VNet integration allows outbound connectivity from the app, it does not inherently provide secure inbound private connectivity from other resources in the landing zone. Therefore, Private Endpoints are the correct strategy for secure inbound communication as mandated by the landing zone requirements.","groups":[{"label":"For connectivity from App Service web Y apps to virtual machines, use","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Private endpoints"},{"id":"1","text":"Service endpoints"},{"id":"2","text":"Virtual network integration"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"For connectivity from virtual machines to Z App Service web apps, use","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Private endpoints"},{"id":"1","text":"Service endpoints"},{"id":"2","text":"Virtual network integration"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":354,"type":"hotspot","topic":10,"questionImage":"images/q354-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q354-answer.webp","explanation":"Azure AD Identity Protection is the recommended solution because it natively supports leaked credential detection to identify compromised accounts. It also detects brute-force attacks and includes smart lockout features that prevent on-premises AD DS account lockouts when Azure AD accounts are targeted, meeting all specified identity requirements.","groups":[{"label":"Delegated management of users and groups","options":[{"id":"0","text":"AD DS organizational units"},{"id":"1","text":"Azure AD administrative units"},{"id":"2","text":"Custom Azure AD roles"}],"correct":["1"]},{"label":"Leaked credential detection","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Enable password hash synchronization in Azure AD Connect"},{"id":"1","text":"Enable security defaults in Azure AD"},{"id":"2","text":"Replace pass-through authentication with AD FS"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":355,"type":"hotspot","topic":11,"questionImage":"images/q355-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q355-answer.webp","explanation":"FD1is an Azure Front Door instance, which is a global, non-regional load-balancing service that can distribute traffic across origin pools in different regions, such as East US and West Europe. Because FD1 has Azure Web Application Firewall (WAF) enabled, it provides Layer 7 security protection for all App Service instances routed through it, satisfying the requirement to protect all instances of ClaimsApp. Note: Without the specific text of the three statements in the hotspot image, this explanation assumes the standard SC-100 scenario where the statements concern: (1) FD1 protecting all App Service instances, (2) WAF preventing North Korean connections via IP restrictions or policies, and (3) other security configurations (like NSG restrictions or database access) that are typically true in this context. The consensus in the provided comments leans heavily toward 'Yes/Yes/Yes', indicating that FD1's global nature and WAF capabilities cover all stated requirements.","groups":[{"label":"FD1 can protect all ClaimsApp instances","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Yes"},{"id":"1","text":"No"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"FD1 requires a certificate for claims.fabrikam.com","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Yes"},{"id":"1","text":"No"}],"correct":["0"]},{"label":"Blocking North Korea requires an FD1 custom rule","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Yes"},{"id":"1","text":"No"}],"correct":["0"]}]},{"id":356,"type":"single","topic":11,"questionImage":"images/q356-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q356-answer.webp","explanation":"The requirement specifies scanning application code for security vulnerabilities and secrets at the time it is pushed to a repository stored in GitHub Enterprise. GitHub Advanced Security provides built-in code scanning (using CodeQL) and secret scanning capabilities that integrate directly with the git push workflow, fulfilling this need. Azure Key Vault manages secrets but does not scan code; Azure DevTest Labs manages test environments; Application Insights monitors runtime performance.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"GitHub Advanced Security"},{"id":"B","text":"Azure Key Vault"},{"id":"C","text":"Azure DevTest Labs"},{"id":"D","text":"Application Insights in Azure Monitor"}],"correct":["A"]}]},{"id":357,"type":"multiple","topic":12,"questionImage":"images/q357-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q357-answer.webp","explanation":"The requirement specifies monitoring and controlling access to SharePoint Online and Exchange Online data in real time. Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps provides the necessary visibility, anomaly detection, and session control capabilities for these SaaS applications. Azure AD Conditional Access enforces security policies at the authentication level, ensuring that only compliant users and devices can access these resources, which is a foundational part of securing application data.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Azure AD Conditional Access"},{"id":"B","text":"access reviews in Azure AD"},{"id":"C","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"id":"D","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps"},{"id":"E","text":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint"}],"correct":["A","D"]}]},{"id":358,"type":"multiple","topic":12,"questionImage":"images/q358-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q358-answer.webp","explanation":"The case study requires identifying internal applications that support single sign-on (SSO) via Azure AD Application Proxy. According to Microsoft documentation for configuring SSO modes in Application Proxy, the supported authentication methods are Security Assertion Markup Language (SAML) and Kerberos. Therefore, these two protocols must be supported by the applications to meet the requirement.","groups":[{"label":"Select all that apply","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Security Assertion Markup Language (SAML)"},{"id":"B","text":"NTLMv2"},{"id":"C","text":"certificate-based authentication"},{"id":"D","text":"Kerberos"}],"correct":["A","D"]}]},{"id":359,"type":"single","topic":13,"questionImage":"images/q359-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q359-answer.webp","explanation":"The ContosoDevelopers group is assigned the db_owner role, which grants CONTROL permission and allows bypassing Dynamic Data Masking. Always Encrypted performs client-side encryption using keys that are never revealed to the database engine or DBAs, ensuring that even privileged users like db_owners cannot view the plaintext data in the MedicalHistory column.","groups":[{"label":"Choose an answer","options":[{"id":"A","text":"row-level security (RLS)"},{"id":"B","text":"Transparent Data Encryption (TDE)"},{"id":"C","text":"Always Encrypted"},{"id":"D","text":"data classification"},{"id":"E","text":"dynamic data masking"}],"correct":["C"]}]},{"id":360,"type":"hotspot","topic":13,"questionImage":"images/q360-question.webp","answerImage":"images/q360-answer.webp","explanation":"To satisfy the requirement that ClaimsDB is accessible only from Azure virtual networks, a Private Endpoint is recommended as it brings the service into the VNet via private IP, effectively removing public access. To meet the authentication requirement for App Services connecting to Azure SQL, Managed Identity is the correct choice as it enables secure, password-less connectivity using system-assigned or user-assigned identities.","groups":[{"label":"ClaimsDB network access","options":[{"id":"0","text":"NAT gateway"},{"id":"1","text":"Network security group"},{"id":"2","text":"Private endpoint"},{"id":"3","text":"Service endpoint"}],"correct":["2"]},{"label":"ClaimsApp permission to ClaimsDB","options":[{"id":"0","text":"Custom RBAC role"},{"id":"1","text":"Managed identity"},{"id":"2","text":"Access package"},{"id":"3","text":"PIM"}],"correct":["1"]}]}];
